// More explanations, generated from ex/*.txt
EX(1,"2025 standard deduction: Single or MFS","The standard deduction is a flat amount everyone may subtract in place of itemizing. For 2025 a single person or a married person filing separately gets $15,750. The new law raised it during the year, so the older $15,000 figure is a wrong answer choice on this exam.");
EX(1,"2025 standard deduction: MFJ or QSS","A married couple filing jointly gets double the single amount: $15,750 × 2 = $31,500. A qualifying surviving spouse uses the same amount as a joint return.");
EX(1,"2025 standard deduction: Head of Household","Head of Household sits between single and joint because the person supports a home for someone else. For 2025 it is $23,625, which is one and a half times the single amount.");
EX(1,"2025 additional standard deduction for age 65+ or blind","People who are 65 or older, or blind, get an extra amount added to the regular standard deduction. Unmarried people get $2,000 for each condition; married people get $1,600 each. Example: a single person who is 67 and blind adds $4,000. A married couple both over 65 adds $3,200.");
EX(1,"2025 standard deduction for someone claimed as a dependent","A dependent, such as a teenager with a job, gets a smaller standard deduction so parents cannot shelter investment income in a child's name. It is the larger of $1,350 or earned income plus $450, but never more than the normal amount. Example: wages of $3,000 give $3,450.");
EX(1,"Gross income filing requirement for a married person filing separately","A married person filing separately must file with only $5 of gross income. The reason: if the other spouse itemizes, this spouse gets no standard deduction, so almost any income is taxable.");
EX(1,"Self-employment filing trigger","Self-employment tax pays for Social Security and Medicare, and it starts at $400 of net profit. So a person with $400 or more of net self-employment earnings must file, even if total income is far below the normal filing threshold.");
EX(1,"Church employee filing trigger","Churches may be exempt from paying employer Social Security tax. Their employees then pay self-employment tax on those wages themselves, and must file once the wages reach $108.28.");
EX(1,"Date that determines marital status for the year","Your filing status depends on whether you are married on the last day of the year. Marry on December 31 and you are married for the whole year. Divorce by December 31 and you are unmarried for the whole year. If a spouse dies, the survivor is still treated as married for that year.");
EX(1,"Qualifying Surviving Spouse: how long?","After a spouse dies, the survivor files jointly for the year of death. For the next 2 years, a survivor who keeps a home for a dependent child and has not remarried can use Qualifying Surviving Spouse, which gives joint return rates and standard deduction. Example: spouse dies in 2025: joint for 2025, QSS for 2026 and 2027.");
EX(1,"Head of Household: cost-of-home test","Head of Household is for an unmarried person who carries the main cost of a home for a child or relative. The test is money: you must pay more than half of the household's yearly costs.");
EX(1,"Costs that count toward keeping up a home","Only the costs of running the home itself count: rent, mortgage interest, property tax, insurance, repairs, utilities and food eaten at home. Personal costs such as clothing, schooling, medical bills and vacations do not count. Mortgage principal does not count either.");
EX(1,"Qualifying child: the 5 tests","To be your qualifying child a person must pass every test: be related to you in the right way, be young enough, live with you more than half the year, not pay more than half of their own support, and not file a joint return. Notice the support test looks at what the child paid, not what you paid.");
EX(1,"Qualifying child age test","The child must be under 19 at the end of the year, or under 24 if a full-time student, and younger than you. There is no age limit for a child who is permanently and totally disabled. Example: a 22-year-old full-time student passes; a 22-year-old who works and is not in school fails.");
EX(1,"Full-time student definition","A student counts as full-time for the year if enrolled full time during some part of 5 calendar months. The months do not need to be in a row. Example: January to May is enough, even after graduating in May.");
EX(1,"Qualifying relative: the 4 tests","A qualifying relative is a dependent who is not a qualifying child, such as a parent or an adult child. Four tests: not anyone's qualifying child; either related to you or living with you all year; gross income under $5,200; and you pay more than half of their support. There is no age test.");
EX(1,"2025 qualifying relative gross income limit","A qualifying relative must have gross income under $5,200 for 2025. Only taxable income counts, so Social Security that is not taxable is ignored. Example: a mother with $4,000 of interest and $14,000 of nontaxable Social Security passes this test.");
EX(1,"Form 8332","Normally the parent the child lives with claims the child. With Form 8332 that parent lets the other parent claim the child. Only the child tax credit and the dependency claim move. Head of Household, the earned income credit and the dependent care credit always stay with the parent the child lives with.");
EX(1,"Custodial parent definition","For divorced or separated parents, the custodial parent is the one the child slept at for more nights in the year. It is counted in nights, not decided by the divorce decree. If the nights are equal, it is the parent with the higher AGI.");
EX(1,"Tie-breaker: two parents who don't file jointly both qualify","When both parents could claim the same child and they do not file together, the child belongs to the parent the child lived with longer. If the time is exactly equal, the parent with the higher AGI wins.");
EX(1,"Tie-breaker: parent vs. non-parent","A parent always has the first right to claim their own child. Someone else, like a grandparent, can claim the child only if no parent does and the other person's AGI is higher than the AGI of either parent.");
EX(1,"Citizenship test for dependents","A dependent must be a U.S. citizen, national or resident, or live in Canada or Mexico. A child living in another country, such as a nephew in Lithuania, cannot be claimed even if you pay all of the support.");
EX(1,"Can a dependent claim another dependent?","A person who can be claimed as someone's dependent cannot claim dependents of their own. Example: a 19-year-old mother who is her parents' dependent cannot claim her baby; her parents may claim both.");
EX(1,"Nonresident alien spouse: how to file jointly","A nonresident alien normally cannot file a joint return. A U.S. citizen or resident married to one can elect to treat the spouse as a resident. They can then file jointly, but the price is that both spouses' income from the whole world becomes taxable here.");
EX(1,"Form W-7","An ITIN is a tax number for someone who must file or be listed on a return but cannot get a Social Security number. Form W-7 is the application, usually sent with the tax return and identity documents.");
EX(1,"Form W-7A","During an adoption the child may not have a Social Security number yet. Form W-7A gets a temporary number, the ATIN, so the adopting parents can claim the child. An ATIN does not work for the earned income credit.");
EX(1,"Form 8867 covers which credits/status?","Some benefits are claimed wrongly so often that paid preparers must ask extra questions and keep records. Form 8867 is the checklist. It covers the earned income credit, the child tax credit family, the American Opportunity credit, and Head of Household status. The penalty is charged per failure.");
EX(1,"Temporary absences and the residency test","A child or relative who is away for a temporary reason is still treated as living with you. School, hospital stays, military service, vacation and juvenile detention all count as time at home. So a college student in a dorm still meets the residency test.");
EX(1,"Child born or died during the year: residency","A baby born in December, or a child who died during the year, is treated as living with you the whole year, as long as your home was the child's home for the time the child was alive.");
EX(1,"2025 gross income filing threshold: Single","A single person under 65 must file when gross income reaches the standard deduction, $15,750. At 65 the extra $2,000 standard deduction raises the threshold to $17,750. The idea: below your standard deduction, no tax can be due.");
EX(1,"2025 gross income filing threshold: MFJ","For a joint return the threshold is the joint standard deduction, $31,500, plus $1,600 for each spouse who is 65 or older: $33,100 with one, $34,700 with both.");
EX(1,"2025 gross income filing threshold: HOH","For Head of Household the threshold equals the standard deduction: $23,625, or $25,625 if 65 or older (adding $2,000).");
EX(1,"2025 gross income filing threshold: QSS","A qualifying surviving spouse uses the joint amount, $31,500. At 65 only one extra amount of $1,600 is added, giving $33,100, because there is only one person.");
EX(1,"Blindness and the filing threshold","Being blind raises the standard deduction but does not change the gross income level at which you must file. Only being 65 or older raises the filing threshold. This is a common trick question.");
EX(1,"2025 filing requirement for a single dependent under 65","A dependent has lower filing thresholds because of the smaller standard deduction. A single dependent must file with more than $1,350 of unearned income (interest, dividends), or more than $15,750 of wages. With both kinds, compare total income with the larger of $1,350 or earned income plus $450.");
EX(1,"Must file even if income is under the threshold when…","Some people must file even with very little income, because they owe a tax that is not income tax or must reconcile something. Main cases: self-employment profit of $400 or more, the 10% penalty on a retirement withdrawal, HSA distributions, and advance premium tax credit payments received.");
EX(1,"Should file (to get money back) even if not required","Not being required to file is different from not benefiting from filing. A person with small wages should still file to get back withheld tax or to claim refundable credits like the earned income credit.");
EX(1,"Age 65 rule","The tax law treats you as reaching an age on the day before your birthday. So a person born January 1, 1961 is considered 65 on December 31, 2025 and gets the age-65 amounts for 2025.");
EX(1,"Prior-year returns: why review","Last year's return shows amounts that carry into this year: unused capital losses, charitable gifts over the limit, passive losses, depreciation taken so far, and overpayments applied to this year. It also shows whether a state refund is taxable.");
EX(1,"Form 8822","Form 8822 tells the IRS a new home address, so notices reach you. Form 8822-B is the business version; it also reports a change in the responsible person for an EIN.");
EX(1,"Form 14039","If someone used your Social Security number to file a false return, you send Form 14039 to report the identity theft. The IRS then investigates and usually issues an Identity Protection PIN for later years.");
EX(1,"Identity Protection PIN","An IP PIN is a 6-digit number the IRS gives to protect a person from identity theft. If anyone on the return has one, it must be entered or an e-filed return is rejected. A new number is issued every year.");
EX(1,"SSN vs ITIN for credits","Credits have different ID rules. The earned income credit needs valid Social Security numbers for everyone. The child tax credit needs a Social Security number for the child and, from 2025, for at least one parent. The $500 credit for other dependents accepts an ITIN.");
EX(1,"Qualifying child relationship test","The child must be your own child, stepchild, foster child, brother or sister (including half and step), or a descendant of one of them. So a grandchild, niece or nephew can be your qualifying child. A cousin cannot.");
EX(1,"Qualifying relative: relatives who need not live with you","Close relatives can be your qualifying relative without living with you: children, parents, grandparents, siblings, aunts, uncles, nieces, nephews and in-laws. A cousin or an unrelated person, such as a girlfriend, must live in your home the entire year.");
EX(1,"Support test: what counts","Support means what was spent on the person's living needs: food, housing (at its rental value), clothing, education, medical care and transportation. Scholarships received by a student who is your child are ignored. Compare what you paid with the total from all sources, including the person's own money.");
EX(1,"Kidnapped child","A child kidnapped by someone outside the family is still treated as living with the parent for the tests, if the child lived with the parent more than half of the part of the year before the kidnapping.");
EX(1,"HOH: parent who doesn't live with you","A parent is the one exception to the rule that the qualifying person must live with you for Head of Household. If you pay more than half the cost of your parent's home, or nursing home, and can claim the parent as a dependent, you qualify.");
EX(1,"HOH: qualifying person must be your dependent?","For Head of Household the qualifying person normally must be your dependent. Exception: your unmarried qualifying child counts even if you cannot claim the child, for example because you gave the claim to the other parent with Form 8332.");
EX(1,"Married to a nonresident alien","A citizen married to a nonresident alien is treated as unmarried for Head of Household purposes if they do not elect to file jointly. With a qualifying child or relative, they can file as Head of Household. The nonresident spouse cannot be the qualifying person.");
EX(1,"Green card test","A person with a green card is a U.S. tax resident, taxed on income from the whole world, even if they spend little time here. Residency begins on the first day they are in the U.S. as a permanent resident.");
EX(1,"First-year choice","Someone who arrives late in the year may not meet the 183-day test until next year. The first-year choice lets them be treated as a resident from their arrival this year, if they were here at least 31 days in a row. This can allow deductions and a joint return sooner.");
EX(1,"Who is 'married' for federal tax","Federal tax law follows state law on who is married. A marriage valid where it was performed counts, including same-sex and common-law marriages. Registered domestic partnerships and civil unions are not marriages for tax, so those partners file as single or Head of Household.");
EX(1,"Annulment","A divorce ends a marriage; an annulment says it never existed. So after an annulment you were never married for tax purposes and must amend the open years that were filed as married, usually the last 3 years.");
EX(1,"Joint return test exception","A married person who files a joint return usually cannot be claimed as a dependent. Exception: the couple filed jointly only to get a refund of withheld tax, and neither would owe tax on separate returns.");
EX(1,"Eligible foster child","A foster child counts as your child for the relationship test only if placed with you by an authorized agency or a court. A child you informally take in does not qualify under this rule.");
EX(1,"Social Security used for support","When a person uses their own Social Security benefits to pay for living costs, that money counts as support they provided themselves. It can cause you to fail the more-than-half support test for a parent.");
EX(1,"Form 1040-C","An alien who is leaving the United States may need a certificate showing their tax is paid, called a sailing permit. Form 1040-C is used to get it. It is not the final return; the person still files a regular return after year end.");
EX(1,"Bona fide residents of Puerto Rico","A full-year resident of Puerto Rico does not pay U.S. income tax on income from Puerto Rico sources. Income from outside Puerto Rico, such as U.S. wages or U.S. dividends, is still reported on Form 1040.");
EX(1,"Form 8843","Certain people do not count their days in the U.S. toward the 183-day test, such as students and teachers on certain visas. Form 8843 is how they tell the IRS they are excluding those days. It is filed even when there is no income.");
EX(1,"EIC qualifying child vs CTC qualifying child","Two credits use different child rules. For the earned income credit the child can be up to 18 (23 if a student) and must live with you in the U.S. over half the year. For the child tax credit the child must be under 17 at year end. A 17-year-old can give you the earned income credit but not the child tax credit.");
EX(1,"Form 1040","Form 1040 is the main yearly income tax return for U.S. citizens and residents. Income, deductions, tax and credits are summarized on it, with details on attached schedules.");
EX(1,"Form 1040-SR","Form 1040-SR is the same return as Form 1040 with larger print and a standard deduction chart, for people 65 or older. Using it is optional and the tax is identical.");
EX(1,"Form W-9","A business asks for Form W-9 to get your correct name and tax ID number so it can send you a Form 1099. The form goes to the payer, not the IRS. Refusing can lead to backup withholding of 24%.");
EX(1,"Form 2120","When several people together support someone and no one pays over half, they can agree that one of them claims the dependent. That person must have paid more than 10%. Form 2120 is filed by the one who claims the dependent.");
EX(1,"AGI","AGI is your total income minus certain adjustments such as IRA contributions, student loan interest and half of self-employment tax. Many limits are based on it: for example, medical expenses count only above 7.5% of AGI.");
EX(1,"MAGI","MAGI is AGI with some items added back, such as the foreign earned income exclusion or tax-exempt interest. There is no single definition: each rule (Roth IRA, premium tax credit, education credits) has its own list of add-backs.");
EX(1,"MFJ","Married Filing Jointly: both spouses report all income and deductions on one return. It usually gives the lowest tax, but each spouse is responsible for the whole tax bill.");
EX(1,"MFS","Married Filing Separately: each spouse files their own return. Rates are less favorable and many benefits are lost, such as education credits, and usually the earned income credit. If one spouse itemizes, the other must too.");
EX(1,"HOH","Head of Household: for an unmarried person who pays more than half the cost of a home for a qualifying person. It gives a bigger standard deduction and wider tax brackets than Single.");
EX(1,"QSS","Qualifying Surviving Spouse: for the 2 years after the year a spouse died, a survivor with a dependent child at home keeps the joint return rates and standard deduction.");
EX(1,"SSN","The Social Security number is the tax ID for individuals. A valid one is required for the earned income credit and for a child to count for the child tax credit.");
EX(1,"ITIN","An ITIN is a tax number from the IRS for someone who needs to file or be listed on a return but cannot get a Social Security number. It is for tax only: it does not allow work and does not qualify for the earned income credit.");
EX(1,"ATIN","An ATIN is a temporary number for a child who is being adopted and has no Social Security number yet. It allows the dependency claim and child tax credit items that accept it, but not the earned income credit.");
EX(1,"EIN","An EIN is the tax ID number of a business or other entity, such as an estate or trust. It is needed to have employees and to file business returns. Applied for on Form SS-4.");
EX(1,"PTIN","Anyone paid to prepare federal tax returns must have a PTIN and put it on each return. It is renewed every year.");
EX(1,"Gross income","The starting rule of income tax: everything you receive is income unless the law specifically excludes it. That is why items like gambling winnings, found money and illegal income are taxable, while gifts and life insurance proceeds are not.");
EX(1,"Taxable income","Taxable income is what the tax rates are applied to. Start with AGI and subtract the standard deduction or itemized deductions, and the 20% qualified business income deduction if any.");
EX(1,"Earned income","Earned income is pay for work. It matters because several benefits require it: IRA contributions, the earned income credit, the dependent care credit and the refundable part of the child tax credit.");
EX(1,"Unearned income","Unearned income comes from owning something or from a benefit, not from working: interest, dividends, capital gains, pensions. It matters for the kiddie tax, the dependent's standard deduction, and the 3.8% net investment income tax.");
EX(1,"Exclusion","An exclusion is income the law leaves out completely. It never appears in gross income, so it differs from a deduction, which is subtracted after income is counted. Examples: gifts, inheritances, municipal bond interest.");
EX(1,"Dependent","A dependent is a qualifying child or a qualifying relative. Having one opens the door to Head of Household status, the child tax credit or the $500 credit for other dependents, and other benefits.");
EX(1,"Resident alien","A resident alien is a non-citizen who has a green card or was in the U.S. long enough to meet the substantial presence test. For tax they are treated like a citizen: worldwide income is taxed and Form 1040 is filed.");
EX(1,"Nonresident alien","A nonresident alien is a non-citizen who has no green card and did not meet the substantial presence test. Only U.S.-source income is taxed, and it is reported on Form 1040-NR.");
EX(1,"Social Security base amounts","Social Security is taxed only when your other income is high enough. The base amount is the first line: below it nothing is taxable. $25,000 for a single person, $32,000 for a joint return. A married person filing separately who lived with the spouse has a base of $0, so benefits are almost always taxable.");
EX(1,"Maximum % of Social Security benefits that can be taxable","However high the income, at most 85% of Social Security benefits is included in income. At least 15% is always tax-free. Example: $20,000 of benefits: no more than $17,000 can be taxable.");
EX(1,"Second-tier Social Security thresholds (85% tier)","There are two steps. Above the first base amount, up to 50% of benefits is taxable. Above the second line ($34,000 single, $44,000 joint) up to 85% is taxable. These amounts are not adjusted for inflation.");
EX(1,"Alimony: pre-2019 vs post-2018 divorce agreements","The date the divorce was signed decides the rule. Before 2019: the payer deducts alimony and the receiver reports it as income. From 2019 on: no deduction and no income. An old agreement keeps the old rule unless it is changed and says the new rule applies.");
EX(1,"Child support","Child support is for the child, not income to the parent who receives it. It is never deducted and never reported. If a payer falls behind, payments count as child support first, then alimony.");
EX(1,"Life insurance proceeds paid because of death (§101)","Money paid by a life insurance policy because the insured person died is not income to the beneficiary. If the insurer holds the money and pays it later with interest, only the interest is taxable.");
EX(1,"Gifts and inheritances (§102)","Receiving a gift or inheritance is not income. But what the property earns afterward is taxable to the new owner. Example: inherit $100,000: not taxed. Put it in the bank and earn $4,000 of interest: taxed.");
EX(1,"Scholarships: what is tax-free?","A scholarship is tax-free only for costs of the education itself: tuition, fees, and required books and supplies, and only for a student working toward a degree. The part that pays for room, food or travel is taxable income.");
EX(1,"Unemployment compensation (2025)","Unemployment benefits replace wages, so they are fully taxable. The state reports them on Form 1099-G. No Social Security tax is due on them, and they are not earned income.");
EX(1,"State income tax refund: taxable?","A state refund is taxable only if deducting that state tax lowered your federal tax last year. If you took the standard deduction last year, the refund is not taxable. If your deduction was cut off by the SALT cap, part or all of the refund may also be non-taxable.");
EX(1,"Gambling winnings and losses (2025)","All gambling winnings are income, reported in full. Losses are deducted separately, only if you itemize, and only up to the amount of winnings. You cannot show just the net. Example: won $5,000, lost $8,000: report $5,000 income and deduct at most $5,000.");
EX(1,"Qualified dividend holding period","Qualified dividends get the lower capital gain rates, but only if you really held the stock. You must hold it more than 60 days during the 121 days around the ex-dividend date. Buying just before a dividend and selling just after gives an ordinary dividend.");
EX(1,"2025 0% long-term capital gain rate applies up to","Long-term gains and qualified dividends are taxed at 0% while taxable income stays under a threshold: $48,350 single, $96,700 joint. Example: a single person with taxable income of $40,000 including $5,000 of long-term gain pays no tax on that gain.");
EX(1,"2025 20% long-term capital gain rate starts above","The top long-term rate, 20%, applies only to the part of taxable income above these high thresholds, such as $533,400 single and $600,050 joint. Between the 0% and 20% lines the rate is 15%.");
EX(1,"Maximum rates on collectibles gain and unrecaptured §1250 gain","Two kinds of long-term gain have higher maximum rates. Gain on collectibles such as coins, art and stamps: up to 28%. Gain on real estate that comes from depreciation previously deducted (unrecaptured Section 1250 gain): up to 25%.");
EX(1,"Capital loss deduction limit","Capital losses first offset capital gains without limit. If losses are larger, only $3,000 a year ($1,500 married filing separately) can reduce other income like wages. The rest carries forward with no time limit. Example: net loss $10,000: deduct $3,000, carry $7,000.");
EX(1,"Long-term holding period","Hold an asset more than one year and the gain is long-term, taxed at lower rates. Exactly one year is still short-term. Count from the day after you bought it. Bought March 1, 2024: long-term starting March 2, 2025.");
EX(1,"Home sale exclusion (§121) amounts","Gain on selling your main home can be left out of income: up to $250,000, or $500,000 on a joint return. For the $500,000, one spouse must have owned the home for 2 years and both must have lived in it for 2 years.");
EX(1,"Home sale exclusion tests","To exclude the gain you must have owned the home and lived in it as your main home for at least 2 of the 5 years before the sale. The 2 years need not be continuous. And you cannot have used the exclusion on another home in the last 2 years.");
EX(1,"Home sale gain that can't be excluded","Two parts of home sale gain are always taxed. First, gain equal to depreciation claimed after May 6, 1997, for example for a rental period or home office, taxed at up to 25%. Second, gain for periods after 2008 when the home was not your main home before you moved in.");
EX(1,"Rental of a home for fewer than 15 days","If you rent out your home for 14 days or fewer in the year, the rent is tax-free and you do not report it. You also deduct no rental expenses. Mortgage interest and property tax stay on Schedule A as usual.");
EX(1,"Vacation home: personal use test","A property used both for rent and personally is treated as a residence if you use it more than 14 days, or more than 10% of the days it was rented, whichever is greater. Then rental expenses cannot create a loss. Example: rented 200 days: more than 20 personal days triggers the limit.");
EX(1,"Real estate professional test","Rental losses are normally passive. A real estate professional escapes that rule by spending more than 750 hours a year, and more than half of all working time, in real estate businesses. A full-time employee in another field almost never qualifies.");
EX(1,"Suspended passive losses","Passive losses you cannot use are not lost: they carry forward. When you sell the entire activity to an unrelated buyer in a taxable sale, all the stored losses are released and can offset any income.");
EX(1,"Excluded from income: personal physical injury damages","Money that compensates for a physical injury or physical sickness is not income, including lost wages in that claim. Punitive damages, which punish the wrongdoer, and interest are always taxable. Damages for emotional distress alone, with no physical injury, are taxable.");
EX(1,"Group-term life insurance","An employer can give up to $50,000 of group life insurance tax-free. The cost of coverage above $50,000, from an IRS table, is added to the employee's wages on Form W-2, even though no cash was received.");
EX(1,"2025 qualified transportation fringe limit","An employer can pay for commuting tax-free up to a monthly limit: $325 for parking and another $325 for transit passes or vanpools in 2025. Amounts above the limit are wages.");
EX(1,"2025 health FSA salary reduction limit","A health flexible spending account lets an employee set aside pre-tax pay for medical costs: up to $3,300 in 2025. It is use-it-or-lose-it, but the plan may allow up to $660 to carry into the next year.");
EX(1,"2025 dependent care assistance exclusion","An employer plan can pay or reimburse child care tax-free up to $5,000 a year ($2,500 married filing separately) for 2025. The amount shows in box 10 of Form W-2 and reduces the expenses available for the dependent care credit.");
EX(1,"Employer educational assistance exclusion","An employer can pay up to $5,250 a year for an employee's education tax-free, whether or not it relates to the job. It can also be used to pay the employee's student loans. Amounts above $5,250 are wages unless another rule applies.");
EX(1,"Clergy housing allowance","A minister's housing allowance is not subject to income tax, up to the fair rental value of the home plus utilities. But it is included when figuring self-employment tax. So it shows on Schedule SE and not as wages for income tax.");
EX(1,"Eligible designated beneficiaries (may stretch)","Five kinds of heirs are exempt from the 10-year rule and can spread payments over their life expectancy: the surviving spouse, the owner's minor child (until 21), a disabled person, a chronically ill person, and someone not more than 10 years younger than the owner, such as a sibling.");
EX(1,"2025 HSA contribution limits","A health savings account goes with a high-deductible health plan. For 2025 you can put in $4,300 with self-only coverage or $8,550 with family coverage, plus $1,000 if 55 or older. Employer contributions count toward the limit.");
EX(1,"2025 HDHP minimum deductible","To contribute to an HSA your health plan must have a high deductible: at least $1,650 for self-only or $3,300 for family coverage in 2025. You also cannot be on Medicare or be claimed as a dependent.");
EX(1,"529 plan: K-12 tuition limit for 2025","A 529 college savings plan can also pay tuition for kindergarten through 12th grade, but only up to $10,000 a year per student for 2025. The exam window tests 2025 law, so $20,000 is the wrong answer.");
EX(1,"Coverdell ESA contribution limit","A Coverdell account is a small education savings account: at most $2,000 a year for each child from all contributors combined. Contributions are not deductible, but growth is tax-free when used for education, including grade school costs.");
EX(1,"Savings bond interest exclusion (§135)","Interest on Series EE or I savings bonds can be tax-free if you cash them in a year you pay college tuition. The bond owner must have been at least 24 when the bond was issued, and income must be under the limits. Not allowed if married filing separately.");
EX(1,"Net Investment Income Tax (NIIT) rate and thresholds","High earners pay an extra 3.8% on investment income such as interest, dividends, capital gains and rents. The tax applies to the smaller of the investment income or the amount of MAGI over the threshold. Example: single, MAGI $230,000, investment income $50,000: 3.8% × $30,000 = $1,140.");
EX(1,"NIIT does NOT apply to","The 3.8% tax is only for investment income. It does not apply to pay from work, retirement account distributions or tax-exempt interest. But a retirement distribution can raise MAGI and push other investment income over the threshold.");
EX(1,"2025 Form 1099-K reporting threshold","Payment apps and card processors send Form 1099-K when a seller got more than $20,000 and had more than 200 transactions. The income is taxable whether or not a form arrives.");
EX(1,"2025 foreign earned income exclusion","A U.S. citizen or resident who lives and works abroad can exclude up to $130,000 of foreign wages or self-employment income for 2025. Each spouse who qualifies gets their own exclusion. It does not cover pensions, interest or dividends.");
EX(1,"FEIE qualifying tests","Two conditions. Your tax home, meaning your main place of work, must be in a foreign country. And you must either be a real resident there for a full calendar year, or be physically outside the U.S. for 330 full days in a 12-month period.");
EX(1,"Bartering income","Trading goods or services without money is still income. Each person reports the market value of what they received. Example: a painter paints a dentist's office in exchange for $800 of dental work: both report $800.");
EX(1,"Illegal income and embezzled funds","Income is taxable no matter how it was obtained. Money from theft, bribes or drug sales must be reported. A thief who returns the money in the same year has no income from it.");
EX(1,"Jury duty pay","Jury pay is taxable as other income. Some employers keep paying wages and require the employee to hand over the jury pay. Then the employee still reports it, and deducts the same amount as an adjustment to income.");
EX(1,"Foster care payments","Payments from a state or licensed agency for caring for a foster child in your home reimburse the cost of care, so they are not income. Payments for more than 5 foster individuals over age 18 can be taxable.");
EX(1,"Workers' compensation","Workers' compensation for a job injury or illness is fully tax-free. If the person goes back to light work, the wages for that work are taxable as usual.");
EX(1,"Prizes and awards","A prize is income at its market value, even if it is a car or a trip and not cash. Example: win a $30,000 car on a game show: $30,000 of income. Narrow exception: certain awards given directly to charity.");
EX(1,"Schedule B required when","Schedule B lists interest and dividends by payer. It is required when either total is over $1,500. It is also required at any amount if you had a foreign account or received interest on a loan you gave to the buyer of your home.");
EX(1,"Tips: employee reporting","Tips are wages. An employee who gets $20 or more in cash tips in a month must report them to the employer by the 10th of the next month, so tax can be withheld. Tips not reported to the employer are still income, and the employee pays the Social Security and Medicare tax on Form 4137.");
EX(1,"Allocated tips (W-2 box 8)","Large restaurants must assign extra tips to employees when reported tips look too low. These allocated tips are in box 8 of Form W-2, not in box 1. The employee adds them to income unless good records prove a smaller amount.");
EX(1,"Section 199A dividends (1099-DIV box 5)","Dividends from real estate investment trusts are ordinary dividends, not qualified. But they get the 20% qualified business income deduction. Example: $1,000 of these dividends gives a $200 deduction.");
EX(1,"Seller-financed mortgage interest received","When you sell your home and lend the buyer the money, the interest the buyer pays you is taxable. You list it on Schedule B with the buyer's name, address and Social Security number, because the buyer is deducting it.");
EX(1,"Accrued interest paid when buying a bond between interest dates","If you buy a bond between payment dates, you pay the seller the interest earned so far. When you get the full interest payment, part of it is just your own money coming back. So you subtract that accrued interest from the interest you report.");
EX(1,"Market discount on bonds","A bond bought on the market for less than face value has a market discount. That discount is really extra interest. So when you sell or redeem the bond, the gain up to the discount earned is ordinary interest income, not capital gain.");
EX(1,"Bond premium on taxable bonds","If you pay more than face value for a bond, the premium lowers your real interest return. On a taxable bond you may choose to deduct a part each year against the interest. On a tax-exempt bond you must reduce basis each year, with no deduction.");
EX(1,"Disability benefits","The rule follows who paid for the policy. If the employer paid the premiums, or the employee paid with pre-tax pay, the benefits are taxable. If the employee paid with after-tax money, the benefits are tax-free.");
EX(1,"Long-term care insurance benefits","Benefits from a long-term care policy are generally tax-free. A policy that pays a flat daily amount is tax-free up to $420 a day in 2025, or the actual cost of care if that is higher.");
EX(1,"Life insurance surrender for cash","If you cancel a life insurance policy and take the cash value, you have income only to the extent the cash is more than the premiums you paid. That gain is ordinary income, not capital gain. Example: paid $20,000, receive $26,000: $6,000 ordinary income.");
EX(1,"Accelerated death benefits","A life insurance policy can pay out before death when the insured is very sick. For a terminally ill person, expected to die within 24 months, the payment is tax-free. For a chronically ill person it is tax-free up to the cost of care or the daily limit.");
EX(1,"Employer adoption assistance (2025)","An employer can pay up to $17,280 of an employee's adoption costs in 2025 without income tax. Social Security and Medicare tax still apply to it. The same expenses cannot also be used for the adoption credit.");
EX(1,"Qualified Medicaid waiver payments","A state Medicaid program may pay a person to care for a disabled individual who lives in the caregiver's home. These payments are treated like foster care payments and are not income. The caregiver may still choose to count them as earned income for the earned income credit.");
EX(1,"Meals and lodging (§119)","Meals an employer provides at the workplace for the employer's own business reasons are not wages. Housing is tax-free only if the employee must live there to do the job, like a hotel manager who has to live at the hotel.");
EX(1,"Accountable plan reimbursements","Under an accountable plan the employee shows the business purpose, gives receipts, and returns any extra advance. Then reimbursements are not wages and do not appear on Form W-2. If those rules are not met, the whole payment is wages.");
EX(1,"Rebates from a dealer or manufacturer on a purchase","A cash rebate on something you buy is just a lower price, not income. You reduce the basis of the item. Example: a $30,000 car with a $2,000 rebate has a basis of $28,000.");
EX(1,"Hobby income","Income from an activity not run for profit must be reported as other income. But hobby expenses cannot be deducted at all for 2025. So a hobby is taxed on its gross income.");
EX(1,"Alaska Permanent Fund dividend","Alaska pays its residents a yearly dividend from oil revenue. It is taxable as other income. It often appears on a child's return and can trigger the kiddie tax.");
EX(1,"Royalties","Royalties from owning rights, like an oil lease or an inherited book copyright, go on Schedule E with no self-employment tax. A person who creates the work as a business, such as an active author, reports royalties on Schedule C and pays self-employment tax.");
EX(1,"Supplemental Security Income (SSI) and welfare","Payments based on need, such as Supplemental Security Income and welfare, are not income. Do not confuse SSI with regular Social Security retirement or disability benefits, which can be taxable.");
EX(1,"VA disability benefits","Disability payments from the Department of Veterans Affairs are tax-free. A military pension based on years of service is taxable like any pension.");
EX(1,"Taxable scholarship and the filing threshold","The part of a scholarship that pays for room and board is taxable. For a dependent student it is treated as earned income when figuring the standard deduction and whether a return is needed, which often removes the need to file.");
EX(1,"Digital assets (cryptocurrency)","Cryptocurrency is treated as property, not money. Selling it, trading it for another coin or spending it creates a capital gain or loss. Coins received as pay, or from mining or staking, are ordinary income at their value on the day received. Everyone must answer the digital asset question on Form 1040.");
EX(1,"Form 1099-DA","Starting with 2025 sales, crypto brokers report sales to the IRS on the new Form 1099-DA. For 2025 it shows only the sale proceeds. Cost basis is added for sales in 2026 and later, so for 2025 the taxpayer must supply the basis.");
EX(1,"Wash sale rule and crypto (2025)","The wash sale rule blocks a loss when you buy back the same stock within 30 days. It is written for stock and securities. Crypto is property, so for 2025 you can sell at a loss and buy back right away and still claim the loss.");
EX(1,"Simplified Method table: single life","Under the Simplified Method you divide your after-tax cost in the pension by a number of expected monthly payments. The number depends on your age when payments start: the older you are, the fewer payments. Example: start at 62 with $26,000 of cost: $26,000 ÷ 260 = $100 of each payment is tax-free.");
EX(1,"Simplified Method table: joint and survivor","When a pension will continue to a survivor, the table uses the combined ages of both people when payments start. More expected payments means a smaller tax-free amount in each one. Example: ages 64 and 60 total 124, so 310 payments.");
EX(1,"Repayment of Social Security over $3,000 of prior-year benefits","If you must give back Social Security benefits that you were taxed on in an earlier year, and the amount is over $3,000, you can take a deduction or a credit for the earlier tax, whichever helps more. Repayments of $3,000 or less give no deduction for 2025.");
EX(1,"Lump-sum Social Security for prior years","A back payment covering earlier years arrives in one year and can make more benefits taxable. The election lets you figure the taxable part as if each year's benefits had been received in its own year. You do not amend the old returns; all tax is still reported this year.");
EX(1,"Medicare premiums deducted from Social Security","Form SSA-1099 shows total benefits before Medicare premiums are taken out. You start from that full amount when figuring taxable benefits. The premiums withheld count as a medical expense if you itemize.");
EX(1,"Capital asset definition (§1221)","Almost everything you own for personal use or investment is a capital asset: stocks, a home, a car. The law lists what is not: inventory, business equipment and buildings, money customers owe you, and art or writing in the hands of the person who created it.");
EX(1,"Holding period start","The holding period starts the day after you acquire the asset. For a gift you add the giver's time to yours. Inherited property is always long-term, even if sold the day after the death.");
EX(1,"Amount realized","The amount realized is everything you get from a sale: cash, the value of other property, and any of your debt the buyer takes over, minus selling costs like commissions. Gain or loss is this amount minus your adjusted basis.");
EX(1,"Gain on sale of a main home","Gain on your home is the selling price less selling costs, minus your basis (cost plus improvements). Up to $250,000 or $500,000 is excluded if you pass the tests. A loss on your personal home can never be deducted.");
EX(1,"Home office and §121","If your home office was a room inside the house, the whole gain can still be excluded. Only the depreciation you claimed for the office is taxed, at up to 25%. An office in a separate building is treated as a separate sale.");
EX(1,"Rental: personal use days","A day counts as personal use if the home is used by you or your family, by anyone paying less than a fair rent, or under a swap arrangement. Renting to a family member at a fair price as their main home is not personal use. Days spent mainly doing repairs do not count.");
EX(1,"Rental expenses: allocation","Costs are split between rental and personal days. If personal use is over the 14-day or 10% limit, rental deductions cannot be more than rental income. They are taken in a fixed order: mortgage interest and taxes first, then operating costs, and depreciation last.");
EX(1,"Depreciation on rental real estate","A residential rental building is deducted evenly over 27.5 years. Land is never depreciated. In the first year you get a part-year amount based on the month it was placed in service. Basis goes down by depreciation you could have taken, even if you forgot to claim it.");
EX(1,"Nonqualified stock options (NSO)","With a nonqualified option you have wage income on the day you exercise: the stock's value minus the price you pay. It is on your Form W-2. Your basis in the shares is their value that day. Example: exercise at $10 when worth $30: $20 of wages per share, basis $30.");
EX(1,"Employee stock purchase plan (§423 ESPP)","An employee plan lets you buy company stock at a discount with no tax at purchase. If you hold the shares 2 years from the offering and 1 year from purchase, only a small part of the profit is ordinary income (the discount) and the rest is long-term capital gain.");
EX(1,"Restricted stock units (RSUs)","Restricted stock units are a promise of shares later. Nothing is taxed at grant. When they vest, the value of the shares is wages. That value becomes your basis, so selling right away gives almost no gain.");
EX(1,"Form 8949 and Schedule D","Every sale of stock or other capital asset is listed on Form 8949, short-term in Part I and long-term in Part II. The totals move to Schedule D, where gains and losses are netted and the tax rate is decided.");
EX(1,"Gain on personal-use casualty (insurance exceeds basis)","A gain can arise when insurance pays more than your basis in destroyed property. You can postpone the gain by buying replacement property within 2 years after the year of the gain. For a main home in a federal disaster area you get 4 years.");
EX(1,"W-2 box 12 codes: other","Box 12 uses letter codes for special items. C is the taxable cost of life insurance over $50,000. DD is the cost of health coverage and is only for information. W is employer money put in your HSA. Q is tax-free combat pay.");
EX(1,"Form 1099-INT boxes","Each box has a different tax result. Box 1 is ordinary taxable interest. Box 3 is interest on U.S. Treasury debt, taxable federally but not by states. Box 8 is tax-exempt municipal interest. Box 2 is a penalty for cashing a certificate early, which you deduct as an adjustment.");
EX(1,"Insolvency exclusion amount","Insolvent means you owe more than everything you own is worth. Cancelled debt is not income up to that shortfall. Example: debts $100,000, assets $70,000, so insolvent by $30,000. If $40,000 is cancelled, $30,000 is excluded and $10,000 is income.");
EX(1,"Foreclosure on nonrecourse debt","With nonrecourse debt the lender can take only the property and cannot pursue you. So a foreclosure is treated as selling the property for the full amount of the debt. You have a gain or loss, and never cancellation of debt income.");
EX(1,"Advance rent","Rent paid in advance is income in the year you receive it, for both cash and accrual landlords. Example: in December 2025 a tenant pays January 2026 rent: it is 2025 income.");
EX(1,"Security deposits","A security deposit you must give back is not income. It becomes income in the year you keep it, for example to cover damage. A deposit meant to be the last month's rent is advance rent and is income when received.");
EX(1,"Tenant pays landlord's expenses or provides services instead of rent","If a tenant pays one of your bills or does work in place of rent, you have rental income equal to that amount. Example: the tenant pays a $300 plumber and deducts it from rent: you report the full rent and deduct the $300 repair.");
EX(1,"Renting to a relative","Renting to a family member counts as real rental only if they pay a fair market rent and use it as their main home. If the rent is below market, every day is personal use, so you report the income but deductions are limited.");
EX(1,"Points on a loan for rental property","Points are prepaid interest. On a rental property loan you cannot deduct them all in the year paid. You spread them evenly over the life of the loan. Example: $3,000 of points on a 30-year loan gives $100 a year.");
EX(1,"Stock dividends","A stock dividend gives you more shares, not money, so your share of the company is unchanged and nothing is taxed. It becomes taxable if shareholders could have chosen cash instead.");
EX(1,"§1202 qualified small business stock (individuals)","To encourage investment in small companies, gain on qualified small business stock can be excluded. The stock must be from a C corporation, bought at original issue, and held more than 5 years. Any part of the gain that is not excluded is taxed at up to 28%.");
EX(1,"HSA and Medicare","Medicare is not a high-deductible plan, so once you enroll in any part of Medicare you can no longer contribute to an HSA. For the year you enroll, the limit is prorated by month. Money already in the HSA can still be spent tax-free on medical costs.");
EX(1,"Coverdell ESA age limits","Coverdell contributions stop when the child turns 18, and the account must be used or moved to a younger family member by age 30. Money left after 30 is paid out, with tax and a 10% extra tax on the earnings. The age limits do not apply to a special needs beneficiary.");
EX(1,"Employer health coverage","Health insurance paid by an employer is the largest tax-free fringe benefit. The amount shown with code DD in box 12 of Form W-2 is only for information and is never added to income.");
EX(1,"Basis of stock received in a non-taxable stock split or stock dividend","In a stock split you own more shares but paid nothing more. So you divide your original total basis by the new number of shares. Example: 100 shares bought for $6,000 split 2-for-1: 200 shares at $30 each. The new shares keep the original purchase date.");
EX(1,"Reinvested dividends","Dividends used to buy more shares of a fund are taxed the same as dividends paid in cash. Because you paid tax on them, they are added to your basis. Forgetting this means paying tax twice when you sell.");
EX(1,"Return of capital distribution","A return of capital is the company giving back part of what you invested. It is not income; it lowers your basis. After basis reaches zero, anything more is capital gain.");
EX(1,"Mutual fund basis methods","When you sell only some shares of a fund bought at different prices, you need a rule for which shares were sold. You can pick specific shares, use first-in first-out (the default), or for mutual funds use the average cost of all shares.");
EX(1,"Personal residence converted to rental: depreciable basis","When a home becomes a rental, depreciation starts from the smaller of two numbers: your adjusted basis or the home's value on the day it became a rental. This stops a drop in value during personal use from becoming a deduction. Land is taken out first.");
EX(1,"Loss on sale of personal-use property (car, home)","The rule is one-sided for personal things. If you sell your car, furniture or home at a loss, there is no deduction. If you sell personal property at a gain, the gain is taxable (a home may qualify for the exclusion).");
EX(1,"Related-party sale at a loss (§267)","If you sell property at a loss to a close relative or a company you control, you cannot deduct the loss. The loss is not wasted: if the buyer later sells at a gain, the buyer's gain is reduced by your disallowed loss. Related means siblings, spouse, ancestors and descendants; in-laws are not included.");
EX(1,"Worthless securities","When stock becomes completely worthless, the law treats it as sold for $0 on the last day of that year. That date decides whether the loss is long-term. Because the exact year can be hard to know, you get 7 years to claim a refund, not the usual 3.");
EX(1,"Holding period: gifted property with loss basis (FMV)","When a gift's value was below the giver's basis and you sell at a loss, your basis is that lower value and your holding period starts on the gift date. You do not add the giver's time. When you use the giver's basis, you do add the giver's holding period.");
EX(1,"Basis of property received for services","Property received as pay is income at its market value, and that same value becomes your basis. Example: a consultant is paid with stock worth $5,000: $5,000 of income, and a $5,000 basis in the stock.");
EX(1,"Section 1244 small business stock (individuals)","A loss on stock is normally a capital loss, limited to $3,000 a year. Section 1244 lets the original investor in a small corporation treat the loss as ordinary: up to $50,000 a year, or $100,000 on a joint return. Any loss above that is a capital loss.");
EX(1,"Adjusted basis of a home","Basis in a home starts with the purchase price and closing costs like title fees. Add improvements that add value or extend life, such as a new roof. Subtract depreciation and insurance payments for casualty losses. Ordinary repairs like painting do not change basis.");
EX(1,"Combat zone pay","Military pay earned in a combat zone is tax-free: all of it for enlisted members, and up to a limit for officers. Because it is not taxed, it is normally not earned income for the earned income credit, but the member can choose to count it if that gives a bigger credit.");
EX(1,"U.S. Treasury interest","Interest on Treasury bills, notes and bonds is taxed by the federal government but cannot be taxed by states. This is the mirror image of municipal bond interest.");
EX(1,"Municipal bond interest","Interest on state and local government bonds is free of federal income tax. Two catches: it still must be reported, and it counts when figuring how much Social Security is taxable. Interest on private activity bonds is added back for the AMT.");
EX(1,"Series EE/I bond interest timing","Savings bond interest builds up inside the bond. A cash-basis person can wait and report all of it when the bond is cashed or matures, or choose to report the increase each year. The choice applies to all their savings bonds.");
EX(1,"HSA last-month rule","Normally the HSA limit is prorated by months of coverage. Under the last-month rule, if you are covered on December 1 you may contribute the full year's amount. The condition: you must stay covered through the end of the next year, or the extra amount becomes income with a 10% extra tax.");
EX(1,"ABLE account annual contribution limit (2025)","An ABLE account is a savings account for a person whose disability began before age 26. Total contributions from everyone are limited to the gift tax annual exclusion, $19,000 for 2025. Growth is tax-free when spent on disability expenses.");
EX(1,"Schedule B","Schedule B lists each payer of interest and dividends when either total is over $1,500. Part III asks about foreign accounts and trusts, which is where a taxpayer first tells the IRS that an FBAR may be required.");
EX(1,"Schedule C","Schedule C reports the income and expenses of a business run by one person. The net profit goes to Form 1040 as income and to Schedule SE for self-employment tax.");
EX(1,"Schedule D","Schedule D brings together all capital gains and losses from Form 8949, capital gain distributions and carryovers. It nets short-term against long-term and applies the $3,000 loss limit.");
EX(1,"Schedule E","Schedule E reports income that passes to you from owning something: rental real estate and royalties in Part I, and Schedule K-1 income from partnerships, S corporations, estates and trusts in the other parts.");
EX(1,"Schedule F","Schedule F is the farm version of Schedule C. Net farm profit is subject to self-employment tax, and farmers have special rules such as income averaging and a single estimated tax payment date.");
EX(1,"Schedule K-1","Partnerships, S corporations, estates and trusts usually do not pay the tax themselves. They send each owner or beneficiary a Schedule K-1 showing that person's share, and the person reports it on their own return, whether or not any cash was paid out.");
EX(1,"Form W-2","Form W-2 is the yearly statement from an employer. Box 1 is taxable wages, box 2 is federal tax withheld, boxes 3 to 6 are Social Security and Medicare, and box 12 has coded items like retirement contributions. Due to employees by January 31.");
EX(1,"Form W-2G","Form W-2G reports gambling winnings above certain amounts and any tax withheld. All winnings are taxable even when no form is issued.");
EX(1,"Form SSA-1099","Form SSA-1099 shows Social Security benefits for the year. Box 5 is net benefits (paid minus repaid), the number used to figure the taxable part. It also shows Medicare premiums and any tax withheld.");
EX(1,"Form 1099-INT","Form 1099-INT reports interest of $10 or more from a bank or other payer, with separate boxes for Treasury interest and tax-exempt interest.");
EX(1,"Form 1099-DIV","Form 1099-DIV reports dividends. Box 1a is total ordinary dividends, box 1b is the part that is qualified (lower rate), and box 2a is capital gain distributions. Box 1b is included in box 1a, not added to it.");
EX(1,"Form 1099-B","Form 1099-B reports sales of stocks and other securities by a broker: the proceeds, the date, and usually the cost basis. The amounts go onto Form 8949.");
EX(1,"Form 1099-R","Form 1099-R reports money paid out of pensions, annuities and IRAs. Box 1 is the gross amount, box 2a the taxable amount, and box 7 the code that tells why it was paid.");
EX(1,"Form 1099-G","Form 1099-G reports payments from a government: mainly unemployment benefits (taxable) and state income tax refunds (taxable only if you got a benefit from deducting the tax).");
EX(1,"Form 1099-NEC","Form 1099-NEC reports $600 or more paid to a person who is not an employee for services. The worker reports it on Schedule C and pays self-employment tax. Due January 31.");
EX(1,"Form 1099-MISC","Form 1099-MISC reports other payments of $600 or more such as rent, prizes and awards, and royalties of $10 or more. Payments for services by a contractor moved to Form 1099-NEC.");
EX(1,"Form 1099-K","Form 1099-K reports payments received through credit cards and payment apps. It shows gross amounts, before fees and refunds, so the seller must subtract those to find real income.");
EX(1,"Form 1099-S","Form 1099-S reports the gross proceeds from a real estate sale. It may not be issued for a main home when the seller certifies the gain is fully excluded.");
EX(1,"Form 1099-SA","Form 1099-SA reports money taken out of an HSA. The taxpayer then shows on Form 8889 how much was used for medical costs, which is tax-free.");
EX(1,"Form 1099-Q","Form 1099-Q reports distributions from a 529 plan or Coverdell account, split into earnings and basis. Earnings are tax-free if the total was used for qualified education costs.");
EX(1,"Form 1099-C","A lender sends Form 1099-C when it forgives $600 or more of debt. The amount is income unless an exclusion applies, such as bankruptcy or insolvency, which is claimed on Form 982.");
EX(1,"Form 1099-OID","Form 1099-OID reports the part of a bond's discount that counts as interest for the year. You report it as interest even though you received no cash.");
EX(1,"Form 1099-LTC","Form 1099-LTC reports payments from a long-term care policy or early payments from a life insurance policy. They are usually tax-free within the daily limit; Form 8853 is used to figure any taxable part.");
EX(1,"Form 2555","Form 2555 is used to claim the foreign earned income exclusion and the foreign housing exclusion. It shows which test you meet: bona fide residence or 330 days of physical presence.");
EX(1,"Form 8949","Form 8949 lists every sale of a capital asset: description, dates, proceeds, basis and any adjustment such as a wash sale. The totals carry to Schedule D.");
EX(1,"Form 4797","Form 4797 reports sales of business property, such as equipment or a rental building. It separates the part of the gain that is ordinary because of past depreciation from the part treated as Section 1231 gain.");
EX(1,"Form 8824","Form 8824 reports a like-kind exchange of real estate held for business or investment. It figures the gain that is postponed and the basis of the new property. Since 2018 only real property qualifies.");
EX(1,"Form 6252","Form 6252 reports a sale where at least one payment comes after the year of sale. Each year it applies the gross profit percentage to the payments received to find the gain to report.");
EX(1,"Form 8889","Form 8889 is required for anyone with HSA activity. It figures the deduction for contributions, reports employer contributions, and shows whether withdrawals were for medical costs. Non-medical withdrawals are taxed plus 20%.");
EX(1,"Form 8582","Form 8582 figures how much passive loss can be deducted this year. It applies the rule that passive losses offset only passive income, plus the $25,000 allowance for rental real estate, and tracks the losses carried forward.");
EX(1,"Form 6198","You can deduct a loss only up to the amount you could actually lose: your cash and property invested plus debts you are personally liable for. Form 6198 figures that limit. It is applied before the passive loss rules.");
EX(1,"Form 982","Form 982 is filed to exclude cancelled debt from income, for example because of bankruptcy or insolvency. The price of the exclusion: you must reduce tax benefits such as loss carryovers and the basis of your property.");
EX(1,"Form 8815","Form 8815 figures how much savings bond interest can be excluded when the bonds were cashed to pay for college. The exclusion shrinks as income rises.");
EX(1,"Form 4137","An employee who did not report all tips to the employer uses Form 4137 to figure the Social Security and Medicare tax on those tips and pay it with the return.");
EX(1,"Form 8919","A worker who should have been an employee but was paid as a contractor uses Form 8919. The worker pays only the employee's half of Social Security and Medicare tax, not full self-employment tax.");
EX(1,"FMV","Fair market value is the price a buyer and seller would agree on when neither is forced to act and both know the facts. It is used to value gifts, inheritances, donations and property received as pay.");
EX(1,"COD","When a lender forgives a debt, you got the benefit of money you never repay, so the forgiven amount is income. Exceptions include bankruptcy, insolvency and certain main home mortgage debt.");
EX(1,"QPRI","Mortgage debt used to buy, build or improve your main home. When this kind of debt was forgiven, for example in a short sale, the cancelled amount could be excluded from income under a special rule; the basis of the home is reduced.");
EX(1,"IRA","An IRA is a personal retirement account you open yourself, not through an employer. A traditional IRA may give a deduction now and is taxed on withdrawal; a Roth IRA gives no deduction and is tax-free later.");
EX(1,"NUA","Net unrealized appreciation is the growth in value of employer stock held inside a retirement plan. With a lump-sum distribution, only the plan's cost is taxed as ordinary income now, and the growth is taxed as long-term capital gain when the stock is sold.");
EX(1,"HSA","A health savings account is a personal account for medical costs, available only with a high-deductible health plan. It has three tax benefits: contributions are deducted, growth is not taxed, and withdrawals for medical costs are tax-free.");
EX(1,"HDHP","A high-deductible health plan is insurance with a deductible of at least $1,650 for one person or $3,300 for a family in 2025. Having one, and no other general coverage, is the requirement for contributing to an HSA.");
EX(1,"FSA","A flexible spending arrangement is an employer account funded with pre-tax pay, for health costs or dependent care. Unlike an HSA it belongs to the employer plan, and unused money is mostly lost at year end.");
EX(1,"ESA","A Coverdell education savings account takes up to $2,000 a year per child. Contributions are not deductible; growth is tax-free when spent on education, including elementary and high school costs.");
EX(1,"QTP","A qualified tuition program, better known as a 529 plan, is a state-sponsored education savings plan. There is no federal deduction for contributions, but growth is tax-free when used for qualified education expenses.");
EX(1,"ISO","An incentive stock option is an employee option with special tax treatment. There is no regular tax at exercise, but the bargain element is added for the AMT. Holding the stock 2 years from grant and 1 year from exercise makes all the profit long-term capital gain.");
EX(1,"NSO","A nonqualified stock option has no special treatment. On exercise, the difference between the stock's value and the price paid is wages, with withholding. Later growth is capital gain.");
EX(1,"ESPP","An employee stock purchase plan lets workers buy company stock, usually at a discount of up to 15%. There is no tax when the shares are bought; tax comes when they are sold, partly as ordinary income and partly as capital gain.");
EX(1,"RSU","A restricted stock unit is a promise to deliver shares after a waiting period. There is no tax at grant. When it vests, the value of the shares is wages and becomes the basis.");
EX(1,"OID","Original issue discount arises when a bond is first sold for less than its face value. The discount is interest that you report a little each year as it builds up, even though it is paid only at maturity. Your basis rises by the amount reported.");
EX(1,"NIIT","The net investment income tax is an extra 3.8% on interest, dividends, capital gains, rents and other investment income for people with MAGI over $200,000 single or $250,000 joint. Figured on Form 8960.");
EX(1,"FEIE","The foreign earned income exclusion lets a citizen or resident working abroad leave up to $130,000 of foreign pay out of income for 2025. It is elected on Form 2555 and covers only pay for work, not investment income or pensions.");
EX(1,"PAL","A passive activity loss comes from a business in which you do not materially participate, or from most rentals. It can be deducted only against passive income; the unused part is carried forward until there is passive income or the activity is sold.");
EX(1,"NOL","A net operating loss happens when business deductions are larger than all income for the year. It is carried forward to later years without a time limit, where it can offset up to 80% of taxable income.");
EX(1,"Basis","Basis is the amount you have invested in property for tax purposes. It usually starts as what you paid. When you sell, gain or loss is the amount you receive minus your basis, so a higher basis means less tax.");
EX(1,"Adjusted basis","Adjusted basis is the original basis after changes during ownership. Improvements raise it. Depreciation, insurance payments for losses and returns of capital lower it. Gain or loss on a sale is measured from adjusted basis.");
EX(1,"Carryover basis","With a gift, the giver's basis carries over to the person who receives it, along with the giver's holding period. So the gain that built up while the giver owned the property is taxed when the receiver sells.");
EX(1,"Stepped-up basis","Property inherited from someone who died gets a new basis equal to its value at the date of death. Gain that built up during the owner's life is never taxed. It can also be a step down if the value fell.");
EX(1,"Realized vs recognized gain","A gain is realized when you sell or exchange property for more than your basis. It is recognized when the law actually taxes it. Some realized gains are not recognized now, such as in a like-kind exchange or the excluded part of a home sale.");
EX(1,"Boot","In a like-kind exchange, boot is anything you receive that is not like-kind property: cash, debt relief or other property. You are taxed on gain up to the amount of boot. Example: realized gain $50,000 and $10,000 cash received: $10,000 is taxed now.");
EX(1,"Qualified dividend","A qualified dividend is an ordinary dividend from a U.S. or qualifying foreign company on stock you held long enough. It is taxed at the long-term capital gain rates of 0%, 15% or 20% in place of ordinary rates.");
EX(1,"Material participation","Material participation means you really work in the business, regularly and substantially. The best-known test is more than 500 hours in the year. If you materially participate, the activity is not passive and its losses are not limited by the passive loss rules.");
EX(1,"Active participation","Active participation is an easier test that applies only to rental real estate. You own at least 10% and make the real decisions, such as approving tenants and setting rent, even through a manager. It allows up to $25,000 of rental loss against other income.");
EX(1,"Fringe benefit","A fringe benefit is pay in a form other than money, like a company car or health insurance. The rule is that it is taxable unless the law specifically excludes it, as it does for health coverage, small de minimis items and qualified retirement plans.");
EX(1,"Insolvency","A person is insolvent when total debts are more than the market value of everything they own, counting retirement accounts. Debt cancelled while insolvent is excluded from income up to the amount of the insolvency.");
EX(1,"Recourse vs nonrecourse debt","With recourse debt the borrower is personally responsible, so the lender can pursue other assets if the collateral is not enough. With nonrecourse debt the lender can only take the collateral. The difference decides whether a foreclosure creates cancellation of debt income.");
EX(1,"Educator expense deduction (2025)","Teachers often buy classroom supplies with their own money. A K-12 teacher, aide, counselor or principal who works at least 900 hours in the school year can deduct up to $300 without itemizing. Two married teachers get up to $300 each, not $600 for one.");
EX(1,"Student loan interest deduction","Interest paid on a loan for college can be deducted up to $2,500 a year without itemizing. The deduction shrinks as income rises and is gone above $100,000 single or $200,000 joint. Not allowed if married filing separately or if someone can claim you as a dependent.");
EX(1,"Self-employed adjustments to income","A self-employed person gets three main deductions before AGI: half of the self-employment tax (like the half an employer would pay), health insurance premiums, and contributions to their own retirement plan. They reduce income tax but not self-employment tax.");
EX(1,"Moving expenses deduction","Moving costs are no longer deductible for most people. The only ones who can deduct them are active-duty military members who move because of a permanent change of station order. Claimed on Form 3903.");
EX(1,"Penalty on early withdrawal of savings","If you cash a certificate of deposit early, the bank charges a penalty. You report the full interest and deduct the penalty as an adjustment to income, so you do not need to itemize. The penalty is in box 2 of Form 1099-INT.");
EX(1,"New OBBBA deductions: where reported and who can claim","The four new deductions (tips, overtime, car loan interest, seniors) go on the new Schedule 1-A. They come after AGI, so they do not lower AGI, but you get them whether you itemize or take the standard deduction. A married person must file a joint return to claim the tips, overtime and senior deductions. The car loan interest deduction has no joint-return requirement.");
EX(1,"Senior deduction (2025–2028)","An extra $6,000 deduction for each person who is 65 or older by the end of the year, on top of the standard deduction. It shrinks by 6 cents for each dollar of MAGI above $75,000 single or $150,000 joint. Example: single, MAGI $95,000: reduction 6% × $20,000 = $1,200, deduction $4,800.");
EX(1,"Qualified tips deduction (2025–2028)","Workers in jobs that normally get tips, such as servers and barbers, can deduct up to $25,000 of tips from income tax. The tips must be voluntary, not a required service charge. The deduction shrinks above $150,000 of MAGI. Social Security and Medicare tax still apply to the tips.");
EX(1,"Qualified overtime deduction (2025–2028)","Only the extra half of time-and-a-half is deductible, not all overtime pay. Example: regular pay $20 an hour, overtime $30: the deductible part is $10 an hour. The limit is $12,500, or $25,000 on a joint return. The overtime must be required by federal labor law.");
EX(1,"Car loan interest deduction (2025–2028)","Interest on a loan to buy a new car for personal use can be deducted up to $10,000 a year. The car must be new, finished in a U.S. factory, and bought with a loan made after 2024. Used cars and leases do not count. It shrinks above $100,000 of MAGI ($200,000 joint).");
EX(1,"Medical expense deduction floor","Medical costs are deductible only above 7.5% of AGI, and only if you itemize. Example: AGI $60,000, medical costs $7,000. The floor is $4,500, so $2,500 is deductible.");
EX(1,"2025 standard mileage rates","In place of actual car costs you can use a flat rate per mile. For 2025: 70 cents for business, 21 cents for medical trips and military moves, and 14 cents for charity work. The charity rate is set by law and does not change.");
EX(1,"SALT: income tax vs sales tax","You deduct either state income tax or state sales tax, whichever is larger, never both. Sales tax helps people in states with no income tax. Property tax is added to either one, and the total is subject to the SALT cap.");
EX(1,"Mortgage interest: acquisition debt limit","Mortgage interest is deductible on up to $750,000 of debt used to buy, build or improve your home. Loans taken before December 16, 2017 keep the older $1,000,000 limit. Example: a 2022 mortgage of $1,000,000: only three-quarters of the interest is deductible.");
EX(1,"Home equity loan interest","What the money was used for decides the deduction, not what the loan is called. A home equity loan used to add a room is deductible. The same loan used to pay credit cards or buy a car is not.");
EX(1,"Points on a home purchase loan","Points paid to get a mortgage when buying your main home can be deducted in full that year. Points paid to refinance are spread over the life of the new loan. Example: $3,000 of refinance points on a 30-year loan gives $100 a year.");
EX(1,"Mortgage insurance premiums (2025)","Premiums for private mortgage insurance are not deductible on a 2025 return. The law brings the deduction back for 2026. The exam tests 2025, so the answer here is not deductible.");
EX(1,"Investment interest expense","Interest on money borrowed to buy investments, such as a margin loan, is deductible only up to your net investment income for the year. Any extra carries forward with no time limit. Example: $5,000 of interest and $3,000 of investment income: deduct $3,000, carry $2,000.");
EX(1,"Charitable cash to public charities: AGI limit","Cash gifts to public charities such as churches and schools can be deducted up to 60% of AGI. Anything above that carries forward for up to 5 years.");
EX(1,"Charitable: appreciated long-term capital gain property","If you give stock or other property held more than a year that has gone up in value, you deduct its full market value and never pay tax on the gain. In exchange the limit is lower: 30% of AGI. Example: stock bought for $2,000, now worth $10,000: deduct $10,000.");
EX(1,"Charitable substantiation thresholds","The larger the gift, the more proof is needed. $250 or more: a written receipt from the charity. Property over $500: Form 8283. Property over $5,000: a qualified appraisal. If you get something back for a payment over $75, the charity must tell you how much is deductible.");
EX(1,"Value of volunteer services","You cannot deduct the value of your time or services given to a charity. You can deduct what you actually spent while volunteering, such as supplies and uniforms, and 14 cents a mile for driving.");
EX(1,"Personal casualty and theft losses (2025)","Loss of personal property from fire, storm or theft is deductible only if it happened in an area the President declared a federal disaster. Then subtract $100 from each event, and deduct only the total above 10% of AGI. Example: loss $20,000, AGI $80,000: $20,000 − $100 − $8,000 = $11,900.");
EX(1,"Qualified disaster loss","For certain major disasters named by Congress the rules are easier. The floor per event is $500, there is no 10% of AGI reduction, and people who do not itemize can add the loss to their standard deduction.");
EX(1,"Miscellaneous itemized deductions subject to 2% (tax prep fees, unreimbursed employee expenses)","Before 2018 employees could deduct job expenses, tax preparation fees and investment fees above 2% of AGI. These deductions were suspended and are now gone permanently. An employee with unreimbursed work costs gets no deduction.");
EX(1,"2025 Child Tax Credit","For 2025 the child tax credit is $2,200 for each qualifying child under 17. If the credit is more than your tax, up to $1,700 per child can be refunded as the Additional Child Tax Credit.");
EX(1,"CTC phase-out","The credit shrinks by $50 for every $1,000, or part of $1,000, that MAGI is over $200,000 ($400,000 joint). Example: joint MAGI $410,500 is $10,500 over, which rounds up to 11 steps: the credit drops by $550.");
EX(1,"CTC SSN rules (2025)","New for 2025: the child needs a Social Security number that allows work, issued before the return is due. In addition, the taxpayer, or at least one spouse on a joint return, must also have a Social Security number. An ITIN is not enough for this credit.");
EX(1,"Credit for Other Dependents","A $500 credit for a dependent who does not qualify for the child tax credit: a child aged 17 or older, a dependent parent, or a child with an ITIN. It can reduce tax to zero but is never refunded.");
EX(1,"AOTC eligibility","The American Opportunity credit is for a student in the first 4 years of college, enrolled at least half-time in a degree program. It can be claimed for only 4 tax years per student, and not by a student with a felony drug conviction.");
EX(1,"Lifetime Learning Credit","The Lifetime Learning credit is 20% of up to $10,000 of tuition, so at most $2,000 per return, not per student. There is no limit on years and no half-time rule, so it covers graduate school and single job-skill courses. It is not refundable.");
EX(1,"Education credit MAGI phase-out (AOTC and LLC)","Both education credits shrink between $80,000 and $90,000 of MAGI for a single filer, and between $160,000 and $180,000 on a joint return. Above the top number there is no credit.");
EX(1,"Education credits and MFS","A married person who files a separate return cannot claim either education credit. The couple must file jointly to get them.");
EX(1,"AOTC: are books counted?","For the American Opportunity credit, required books and supplies count even when bought at a regular bookstore. For the Lifetime Learning credit they count only if the school requires that they be paid to the school.");
EX(1,"2025 EITC maximum credit","The earned income credit is a refundable credit for workers with low income. The maximum grows with the number of children, up to three: $649 with none, $4,328 with one, $7,152 with two, and $8,046 with three or more.");
EX(1,"2025 EITC investment income limit","The earned income credit is for people who live on work income. If investment income such as interest, dividends, rent and capital gains is more than $11,950 for 2025, no credit is allowed.");
EX(1,"EITC with no qualifying child: age","A worker with no qualifying child can get a small credit only if they are at least 25 and under 65 at the end of the year, are not someone's dependent, and lived in the U.S. more than half the year.");
EX(1,"EITC after disallowance","If the IRS denied the credit in an earlier year (not for a math error), you must attach Form 8862 to claim it again. If the claim was reckless you are banned for 2 years; if it was fraud, for 10 years.");
EX(1,"EITC and MFS","A married person filing separately can now get the earned income credit, but only if they have a qualifying child living with them more than half the year and they lived apart from their spouse for the last 6 months, or are legally separated.");
EX(1,"Child and dependent care credit (2025)","The credit helps pay for care so you can work. For 2025 it is 20% to 35% of care costs, counting at most $3,000 for one person or $6,000 for two or more. So the largest credit is $1,050 or $2,100. It is not refundable for 2025.");
EX(1,"Dependent care: qualifying person","The care must be for a child under 13 who is your dependent, or for a spouse or dependent who cannot care for themselves and lives with you more than half the year. The day a child turns 13, later costs stop counting.");
EX(1,"Dependent care: earned income limit","Care costs cannot be more than what the lower-earning spouse earned, because the credit is for working. If one spouse is a full-time student or disabled, that spouse is treated as earning $250 a month with one child, or $500 with two or more.");
EX(1,"Dependent care: payments to relatives","Paying a relative for care can qualify, but not these: your spouse, the child's other parent, anyone you claim as a dependent, or your own child under 19. Paying a grandmother who is not your dependent qualifies.");
EX(1,"2025 saver's credit AGI limits","The saver's credit rewards lower-income workers who put money into a retirement account. It is 50%, 20% or 10% of up to $2,000 of contributions per person. No credit above AGI of $39,500 single, $59,250 Head of Household, or $79,000 joint.");
EX(1,"Premium tax credit: forms","The Marketplace sends Form 1095-A showing premiums and the advance credit paid to the insurer. The taxpayer uses those numbers on Form 8962 to compare the advance with the credit really earned. Form 1095-A itself is kept, not attached.");
EX(1,"Premium tax credit: MFS","Married people must file jointly to get the premium tax credit. The exception is a victim of domestic abuse or abandonment who cannot file with the spouse.");
EX(1,"2025 adoption credit","Adoption costs such as agency fees, court costs and travel earn a credit up to $17,280 per child for 2025. New for 2025, up to $5,000 of it is refundable. The credit starts to shrink at MAGI of $259,190.");
EX(1,"Residential clean energy credit (§25D)","A 30% credit for solar panels, solar water heaters, wind, geothermal and battery storage at your home. There is no dollar cap and unused credit carries forward. The law ends it for spending after December 31, 2025, so it still applies on 2025 returns.");
EX(1,"Energy efficient home improvement credit (§25C)","A 30% credit for making a home more efficient: insulation, windows, doors and efficient heating. The yearly cap is $1,200, with a separate $2,000 for heat pumps. It ends for property placed in service after December 31, 2025.");
EX(1,"Clean vehicle credit (2025)","The credit for electric vehicles is up to $7,500 for a new one and $4,000 for a used one. The new law ended it early: it does not apply to a vehicle acquired after September 30, 2025.");
EX(1,"Foreign tax credit without Form 1116","Small amounts of foreign tax, typically withheld on mutual fund dividends, can be claimed directly without Form 1116. The total must be $300 or less ($600 joint) and all of it must be on passive income shown on a Form 1099.");
EX(1,"Credit for the elderly or disabled","A small credit for people 65 or older, or under 65 and retired on total disability, whose income is very low. It is figured on Schedule R and is not refundable. Few people qualify because the income limits have never been raised.");
EX(1,"Excess Social Security withheld","Each employer withholds Social Security tax up to the wage base, $176,100 for 2025. With two jobs the total withheld can go over the yearly maximum. The extra is claimed as a refundable credit on the return. If one employer alone withheld too much, that employer must fix it.");
EX(1,"Self-employed health insurance deduction","A self-employed person can deduct health insurance premiums for themselves, their spouse, dependents and children under 27, without itemizing. Two limits: not more than the business's net profit, and no deduction for any month they could have joined an employer plan, including a spouse's.");
EX(1,"Armed Forces reservists travel","Members of the National Guard or reserves who travel more than 100 miles from home for duty can deduct the unreimbursed travel costs as an adjustment to income. This is one of the few employee expense deductions still allowed.");
EX(1,"Attorney fees as an adjustment","Normally legal fees for a personal lawsuit are not deductible. An exception: fees in an unlawful discrimination or whistleblower case can be deducted before AGI, but not more than the award included in income that year.");
EX(1,"HSA deduction","HSA contributions you make yourself are deducted as an adjustment on Form 8889. Contributions taken from pay through an employer plan were never in your taxable wages, so they are not deducted again.");
EX(1,"Alimony deduction","Alimony is deductible only under a divorce or separation agreement signed before 2019. The payer must give the recipient's Social Security number. For agreements from 2019 on there is no deduction and no income.");
EX(1,"2025 long-term care premium limits (by age at year end)","Premiums for long-term care insurance count as a medical expense, but only up to a limit that rises with age: from $480 at age 40 or under to $6,020 at 71 or older for 2025.");
EX(1,"Medical lodging","When you travel away from home mainly for medical care, lodging counts as a medical expense up to $50 per night for each person. A parent traveling with a sick child counts too, so $100 a night. Meals are not included.");
EX(1,"Capital improvements for medical reasons","A home improvement made for medical reasons is a medical expense only to the extent it does not raise the home's value. Example: an elevator costs $20,000 and raises value by $12,000: $8,000 counts. Ramps and widened doors usually add no value, so the full cost counts.");
EX(1,"Medical: whose expenses count","You can deduct medical costs you paid for yourself, your spouse and your dependents. You can also include someone who would be your dependent except that their income was too high or they filed a joint return, such as a parent you support.");
EX(1,"Taxes NOT deductible","Some payments feel like taxes but are not deductible: federal income tax, Social Security tax, homeowner association fees, and charges for local improvements like sidewalks. Transfer taxes on buying a home are added to basis. Property tax on foreign real estate is not deductible.");
EX(1,"Personal property tax","A state or local tax on personal property, such as a car, is deductible only if it is based on the item's value and charged every year. A flat registration fee or one based on weight is not deductible.");
EX(1,"Real estate taxes in the year of a sale","In the year a home is sold, the property tax is split by the number of days each party owned it. The seller deducts the tax up to the day before the sale and the buyer from the sale date, no matter who actually wrote the check.");
EX(1,"Qualified home for mortgage interest","Mortgage interest is deductible on your main home and one other home you choose. A boat or motor home can count as a home if it has a place to sleep, cook and a toilet.");
EX(1,"Charitable: not deductible","Gifts are deductible only to qualified organizations. Money given directly to a needy person, to a political campaign or to most foreign charities is not. Raffle tickets, the value of your time, and donated blood are also not deductible.");
EX(1,"Donated car worth over $500","Charities usually sell donated cars. If the claimed value is over $500, your deduction is the amount the charity sold it for, shown on Form 1098-C, not the book value. You may use market value if the charity keeps and uses the car.");
EX(1,"Donated clothing and household items","Used clothing and household goods are deductible only if in good used condition or better. The amount is what a thrift shop would charge, usually far below the original price.");
EX(1,"Ordinary income property donated","If selling the property would have produced ordinary income or short-term gain, the deduction is limited to your basis, not the market value. Examples: inventory, art given by the artist, and stock held one year or less.");
EX(1,"Other itemized deductions (not subject to 2%)","These itemized deductions survived when the 2% deductions ended. The most tested are gambling losses up to winnings, the deduction for estate tax paid on income in respect of a decedent, and repayments over $3,000 under claim of right.");
EX(1,"Limit on total itemized deductions (2025)","For 2025 there is no overall limit that cuts total itemized deductions for high earners. A new limit for taxpayers in the 37% bracket begins in 2026, which is outside this exam.");
EX(1,"What is NOT qualified business income","The 20% deduction is for business profit, not for investment income or pay for work. So capital gains, dividends, interest, wages as an employee, an S corporation owner's salary and a partner's guaranteed payments are all left out.");
EX(1,"QBI loss","If your qualified businesses have a combined loss, there is no deduction this year, and the loss carries to next year, where it reduces that year's qualified business income.");
EX(1,"QBI and rental real estate","Rental income gets the 20% deduction only if the rental is a real business. A safe harbor treats it as one if you or your workers spend at least 250 hours a year on rental services and keep separate books.");
EX(1,"Clean vehicle credit income limits (new vehicles, 2025)","High earners cannot claim the new clean vehicle credit: MAGI must be at or below $300,000 joint, $225,000 Head of Household or $150,000 others. The price must be at or below $80,000 for vans, SUVs and pickups, or $55,000 for other cars.");
EX(1,"Used clean vehicle credit (2025)","A used electric vehicle bought from a dealer earns a credit of 30% of the price, up to $4,000. The price must be $25,000 or less and income limits are half those for new cars. Not allowed for vehicles acquired after September 30, 2025.");
EX(1,"Premium tax credit eligibility (2025)","The premium tax credit lowers the cost of insurance bought on the Marketplace. You must not be eligible for affordable employer coverage or a government plan such as Medicare, and cannot be a dependent. For 2025 there is still no upper income cutoff at 400% of the poverty line.");
EX(1,"Excess advance PTC repayment (2025)","If the advance credit paid to the insurer was more than you were entitled to, you repay the difference on the return. For 2025, people with income under 400% of the poverty line repay only up to a capped amount.");
EX(1,"Adoption credit: special needs child","For a child with special needs, as determined by a state, the full credit is allowed in the year the adoption becomes final, even if the parents spent little or nothing.");
EX(1,"Adoption credit: timing (domestic)","For a U.S. adoption, costs paid before the adoption is final are claimed one year later. Costs paid in the year it becomes final, or after, are claimed that year. For a foreign adoption nothing is claimed until it is final.");
EX(1,"Credit for prior-year minimum tax","Some AMT comes only from timing, such as exercising incentive stock options. That AMT is not lost. It becomes a credit, figured on Form 8801, that reduces regular tax in later years when regular tax is higher than the tentative minimum tax.");
EX(1,"Education credits and tax-free aid","You cannot claim a credit for costs that were paid with tax-free money. Subtract tax-free scholarships, Pell grants and employer tuition help from tuition first. The same expense also cannot be used for both a credit and a tax-free 529 withdrawal.");
EX(1,"Who claims the education credit for a dependent student","When a student is claimed as a dependent, only the parent can claim the education credit, and tuition the student paid counts as paid by the parent. If the parents could claim the student but do not, the student may claim the credit.");
EX(1,"Dependent care credit rate (2025)","The credit percentage depends on AGI. It is 35% at AGI of $15,000 or less and falls by 1 point for each $2,000 until it reaches 20% above $43,000. Most taxpayers get 20%.");
EX(1,"Dependent care and employer benefits","Care costs paid through a tax-free employer plan cannot also earn the credit. Subtract the excluded benefits from the $3,000 or $6,000 limit. Example: two children and $5,000 excluded: only $1,000 of expenses is left for the credit.");
EX(1,"Investment interest: election","Qualified dividends and long-term gains are taxed at low rates, so they do not count as investment income for this deduction. You may elect to count them, but then they are taxed at ordinary rates. It is worth it only when the extra deduction saves more.");
EX(1,"Investment interest doesn't include","Investment interest covers only loans for taxable investments. Interest on a loan to buy tax-exempt bonds is never deductible. Interest tied to a passive activity follows the passive loss rules, and home mortgage interest has its own rules.");
EX(1,"Personal casualty gains and losses netting","Add up personal casualty gains and losses for the year. Losses can offset gains even if not from a disaster. Only if losses are larger does the remaining net loss need to be from a federal disaster and exceed 10% of AGI.");
EX(1,"Seller-paid points","If the seller pays the buyer's points, the buyer still gets to deduct them, as if the buyer had paid. In return the buyer lowers the basis of the home by that amount. For the seller they are a selling expense.");
EX(1,"Charitable timing","A gift counts in the year it is actually given. A check counts when mailed. A credit card gift counts when charged, even if the bill is paid next year. A promise to give counts only when paid.");
EX(1,"Medical: qualifying vs not","An expense is medical if it treats or prevents a specific illness. A weight-loss program for diagnosed obesity, a stop-smoking program and a guide dog qualify. Things that are merely good for general health, like vitamins or a gym membership, do not.");
EX(1,"Student loan interest: whose loan","You can deduct student loan interest only if you are legally required to repay the loan and it was for you, your spouse, or a person who was your dependent when you borrowed. A parent who voluntarily pays a child's loan gets no deduction.");
EX(1,"EIC: earned income includes","For the earned income credit, earned income is pay for working: wages, tips and self-employment profit. It also includes employer disability benefits received before minimum retirement age, and combat pay if the person elects to count it.");
EX(1,"EIC: earned income excludes","Income that does not come from current work does not count for the earned income credit: pensions, unemployment, Social Security, alimony, child support and investment income. Pay earned by a prison inmate does not count either.");
EX(1,"EIC qualifying child residency","For the earned income credit the child must live with you in the United States for more than half the year. There is no support test here, unlike the dependency rules. Military duty abroad counts as living in the U.S.");
EX(1,"Dependent care: what counts","Care counts if its main purpose is the person's well-being and protection while you work: day care, a nanny, before and after-school programs and day camp. Overnight camp, school tuition from kindergarten up, and care while you are not working do not count.");
EX(1,"Dependent care provider information","To claim the credit you must report who was paid: the provider's name, address and tax ID number on Form 2441. Form W-10 can be used to ask the provider. If the provider refuses, you can still claim the credit by showing you tried to get the information.");
EX(1,"Child tax credit: qualifying child","For the child tax credit the child must be under 17 at the end of the year, be your dependent, live with you more than half the year, be a U.S. citizen, national or resident, and have a Social Security number valid for work.");
EX(1,"Carryover cheat sheet: individuals","Unused amounts carry to later years for different lengths of time. Capital losses, investment interest and passive losses carry forward with no limit. Charitable gifts above the AGI limit carry forward only 5 years, so they can expire.");
EX(1,"Credit carryovers","Credits you cannot use may move to other years. The foreign tax credit goes back 1 year and forward 10. The general business credit goes back 1 and forward 20. The AMT credit carries forward forever. Refundable credits never carry over because they are paid out.");
EX(1,"Long-term care premiums","Long-term care insurance premiums are a medical expense, but only up to a yearly limit based on your age. Anything paid above the limit is not deductible.");
EX(1,"Medical: not deductible","Not medical: cosmetic surgery done only to improve appearance, over-the-counter medicine (insulin is the exception), gym dues for general health, and funeral costs. Surgery to correct a deformity from birth, injury or disease does qualify.");
EX(1,"Reservists, performing artists, fee-basis officials","Most employees cannot deduct job costs. Four groups still can, as an adjustment on Form 2106: reservists traveling over 100 miles, qualified performing artists, state or local officials paid by fees, and disabled employees with impairment-related work costs.");
EX(1,"Credit for the elderly or disabled income limits","The credit for the elderly or disabled disappears at very low income levels, and nontaxable Social Security reduces it further. So although many people are old enough, very few actually receive it.");
EX(1,"Schedule 1","Schedule 1 has two parts. Part I adds income that has no line on Form 1040, such as business income, unemployment and rental income. Part II lists adjustments that are subtracted to reach AGI, such as the IRA deduction and student loan interest.");
EX(1,"Schedule 1-A","Schedule 1-A is new for 2025. It holds the four new deductions: tips, overtime, car loan interest and the senior deduction. They are subtracted after AGI and are available whether or not you itemize.");
EX(1,"Schedule 3","Schedule 3 collects nonrefundable credits other than the child credits (foreign tax, education, dependent care, saver's, energy) and other payments, such as tax paid with an extension and excess Social Security withheld.");
EX(1,"Schedule A","Schedule A lists itemized deductions: medical costs above 7.5% of AGI, state and local taxes up to the cap, home mortgage and investment interest, gifts to charity, and disaster losses. You use it only when the total beats your standard deduction.");
EX(1,"Schedule EIC","Schedule EIC is attached when the earned income credit is claimed with a qualifying child. It gives each child's name, Social Security number, age, relationship and months lived with the taxpayer.");
EX(1,"Schedule R","Schedule R figures the credit for the elderly or the disabled. It starts with a base amount for the filing status and reduces it for nontaxable pensions and for AGI above a low limit.");
EX(1,"Schedule 8812","Schedule 8812 figures the child tax credit, the $500 credit for other dependents, and the refundable Additional Child Tax Credit, which is based on earned income over $2,500.");
EX(1,"Form 1098","The mortgage lender sends Form 1098 showing the mortgage interest and points you paid in the year, and often property tax paid from escrow. It supports the home mortgage interest deduction on Schedule A.");
EX(1,"Form 1098-T","A college sends Form 1098-T showing tuition paid and scholarships received. It is the starting point for the education credits, but you must adjust for books and for which year amounts were really paid.");
EX(1,"Form 1098-E","The loan servicer sends Form 1098-E when you paid $600 or more of student loan interest. It supports the deduction of up to $2,500.");
EX(1,"Form 1098-C","A charity that receives a car, boat or plane worth more than $500 sends Form 1098-C. It shows what the charity sold the vehicle for, which is normally the limit of your deduction.");
EX(1,"Form 1095-A","Anyone who bought health insurance through the Marketplace gets Form 1095-A. It shows the monthly premium, the benchmark plan premium and any advance credit, which are the numbers needed for Form 8962.");
EX(1,"Form 2441","Form 2441 is used for the child and dependent care credit. It also reports tax-free dependent care benefits from an employer, so it is required even when you only received employer benefits and claim no credit.");
EX(1,"Form 8863","Form 8863 figures both education credits. Part III is filled in for each student; Part I gives the refundable part of the American Opportunity credit and Part II the nonrefundable credits.");
EX(1,"Form 8962","Form 8962 compares the premium tax credit you are entitled to, based on actual income, with the advance payments sent to your insurer. Too little advance gives extra credit; too much means repayment. It is required whenever advance payments were made.");
EX(1,"Form 5695","Form 5695 figures the two home energy credits: the residential clean energy credit (solar and similar) and the energy efficient home improvement credit (insulation, windows, heat pumps).");
EX(1,"Form 8936","Form 8936 figures the credits for new and used clean vehicles. It asks for the vehicle identification number and applies the income and price limits.");
EX(1,"Form 8839","Form 8839 figures the adoption credit and also the amount of employer adoption assistance that can be excluded from income. It tracks expenses by year and by child.");
EX(1,"Form 8862","After the IRS denies the earned income credit, child tax credit or American Opportunity credit, Form 8862 must be attached the next time you claim it, to show you now qualify. It is not needed if the denial was only a math error.");
EX(1,"Form 8801","Form 8801 figures the credit for AMT paid in earlier years on timing items. The credit can be used in a year when regular tax is higher than the tentative minimum tax, and the rest carries forward.");
EX(1,"Form 1116","Form 1116 figures the foreign tax credit. It limits the credit to the share of U.S. tax that relates to foreign income, by category of income, and tracks carryovers.");
EX(1,"Form 4684","Form 4684 reports losses from casualties and thefts. Section A is for personal property, where the $100 and 10% of AGI reductions apply; Section B is for business and income-producing property.");
EX(1,"Form 8283","Form 8283 is required when total gifts of property are over $500. Section A covers items up to $5,000. Section B is for items over $5,000 and needs an appraiser's signature and the charity's acknowledgment.");
EX(1,"Form 4952","Form 4952 figures how much investment interest can be deducted this year, limited to net investment income, and how much carries forward. It is also where you elect to treat qualified dividends or capital gains as investment income.");
EX(1,"Form 2106","Form 2106 is now used only by the few employees who can still deduct work expenses: reservists, qualified performing artists, fee-basis government officials and employees with impairment-related work expenses.");
EX(1,"Form 8995","Form 8995 is the simple form for the 20% qualified business income deduction. It is used when taxable income is at or below the threshold, so the wage and property limits do not apply. Higher incomes use Form 8995-A.");
EX(1,"What is the Child Tax Credit (CTC)?","The child tax credit lowers tax by up to $2,200 for each qualifying child under 17 who has a Social Security number. It shrinks at high incomes. If it is larger than the tax, part can be refunded through the Additional Child Tax Credit.");
EX(1,"What is the Additional Child Tax Credit (ACTC)?","When the child tax credit is more than your tax, the unused part can be refunded, up to $1,700 per child. The refund is limited to 15% of earned income above $2,500, so a family with no earned income gets none.");
EX(1,"What is the Credit for Other Dependents (ODC)?","A $500 credit for each dependent who cannot be counted for the child tax credit, for example a 17-year-old, a college student, or an elderly parent. It only reduces tax; nothing is refunded.");
EX(1,"What is the Earned Income Credit (EIC or EITC)?","The earned income credit is a refundable credit meant to reward work at low pay. It rises with earnings up to a point, then phases out. It is larger with children and is denied when investment income is over the yearly limit.");
EX(1,"What is the American Opportunity Tax Credit (AOTC)?","The American Opportunity credit covers 100% of the first $2,000 and 25% of the next $2,000 of college costs, so up to $2,500 per student, for the first 4 years. Up to $1,000 of it is refundable.");
EX(1,"What is the Lifetime Learning Credit (LLC)?","The Lifetime Learning credit is 20% of up to $10,000 of tuition for everyone on the return together. It has no limit on years and covers graduate study and courses to improve job skills. It is not refundable.");
EX(1,"What is the Child and Dependent Care Credit?","A credit for part of what you pay for care of a child under 13, or a disabled spouse or dependent, so that you (and your spouse) can work or look for work. Both spouses need earned income.");
EX(1,"What is the Premium Tax Credit (PTC)?","The premium tax credit helps pay for health insurance bought through the Marketplace. Most people get it in advance, paid to the insurer. On the return, Form 8962 compares the advance with the credit based on actual income.");
EX(1,"What is the Adoption Credit?","The adoption credit covers reasonable costs of adopting a child, such as fees, court costs and travel, up to $17,280 per child for 2025. Up to $5,000 can be refunded; the rest carries forward up to 5 years.");
EX(1,"What is the Foreign Tax Credit (FTC)?","When another country taxes your income and the U.S. taxes it too, the foreign tax credit lowers U.S. tax by the foreign tax paid, up to the U.S. tax on that foreign income.");
EX(1,"What is the Residential Clean Energy Credit?","A 30% credit for the cost of solar electric, solar water heating, wind, geothermal and battery storage on a home. It is not refundable, but unused credit carries to later years. It ends for costs after 2025.");
EX(1,"What is the Energy Efficient Home Improvement Credit?","A 30% credit for energy-saving improvements to an existing home, with yearly caps: $1,200 in general and $2,000 for heat pumps. It does not carry forward. It ends after 2025.");
EX(1,"What is the Credit for Prior Year Minimum Tax?","If you paid AMT because of a timing item, you paid tax early. This credit gives it back in a later year by reducing regular tax down to the level of that year's tentative minimum tax.");
EX(1,"Refundable credits (2025)","Refundable credits are paid even when you owe no tax. For 2025 the list is: the earned income credit, the Additional Child Tax Credit, 40% of the American Opportunity credit, the premium tax credit, up to $5,000 of the adoption credit, and excess Social Security withheld.");
EX(1,"Nonrefundable credits (2025)","Nonrefundable credits can take your tax down to zero, and anything left over is lost unless that credit has a carryover. Common ones: the credit for other dependents, Lifetime Learning, dependent care, saver's, foreign tax and energy credits.");
EX(1,"Refundable vs nonrefundable credit","The difference shows when the credit is bigger than the tax. Example: tax of $800 and a $1,000 credit. If nonrefundable, tax becomes zero and $200 is lost. If refundable, the IRS pays you the $200.");
EX(1,"Credit vs deduction","A deduction reduces the income that is taxed, so its value depends on your tax rate. A credit reduces the tax itself. Example in the 22% bracket: a $1,000 deduction saves $220; a $1,000 credit saves $1,000.");
EX(1,"What is the standard deduction?","The standard deduction is a set amount you may subtract without proving any expenses. It depends on filing status and is higher for people who are 65 or older or blind. You choose it or itemized deductions, whichever is larger.");
EX(1,"What are itemized deductions?","Itemized deductions are specific personal expenses the law allows, listed on Schedule A. You itemize only when their total is more than your standard deduction. A married person filing separately must itemize if the spouse itemizes.");
EX(1,"What is an adjustment to income (above-the-line deduction)?","An adjustment is subtracted from total income to arrive at AGI. Everyone gets it, even with the standard deduction. Because it lowers AGI, it can also help with limits based on AGI, such as the medical expense floor.");
EX(1,"What is the SALT deduction?","The SALT deduction is for state and local taxes: income tax or sales tax, plus property taxes. The total is capped at $40,000 for 2025 ($20,000 if married filing separately), and the cap shrinks for MAGI above $500,000.");
EX(1,"What is the home mortgage interest deduction?","Interest on a loan used to buy, build or improve your main home or a second home is an itemized deduction. It is allowed on up to $750,000 of such debt, and the loan must be secured by the home.");
EX(1,"What is the charitable contribution deduction?","Gifts of money or property to qualified charities are an itemized deduction. The amount you can deduct in one year is limited to a percentage of AGI (60% for cash to public charities), and the extra carries forward 5 years.");
EX(1,"What is the medical expense deduction?","Unreimbursed costs of medical and dental care for you, your spouse and dependents are an itemized deduction, but only the part above 7.5% of AGI. Health insurance paid with after-tax money counts.");
EX(1,"What is the casualty loss deduction?","A loss from a sudden event such as a fire, flood or storm. For personal property it is deductible only in a federally declared disaster area, after subtracting $100 per event and 10% of AGI.");
EX(1,"What is the QBI deduction?","Owners of pass-through businesses can deduct up to 20% of the business's net income. It is taken after AGI and is available with either the standard deduction or itemized deductions. At higher incomes it is limited by wages paid and by the type of business.");
EX(1,"What is the student loan interest deduction?","Interest paid on a loan used only for higher education can be deducted up to $2,500 a year as an adjustment to income, so no itemizing is needed. It phases out at higher incomes.");
EX(1,"What is the educator expense deduction?","K-12 teachers, aides, counselors and principals who work at least 900 hours can deduct up to $300 of unreimbursed classroom costs as an adjustment to income.");
EX(1,"What is the HSA deduction?","Money you put into your own HSA outside of payroll is deducted as an adjustment to income. You must be covered by a high-deductible health plan and not be on Medicare.");
EX(1,"What is the self-employed health insurance deduction?","A self-employed person has no employer to provide tax-free health insurance. This adjustment gives a similar result: premiums are deducted before AGI, limited to the profit of the business.");
EX(1,"What is the deduction for half of self-employment tax?","An employee pays half of Social Security and Medicare tax and the employer pays half and deducts it. A self-employed person pays both halves, so the law lets them deduct one half as an adjustment to income.");
EX(1,"What is the senior deduction?","A new temporary deduction of $6,000 for each taxpayer who is 65 or older. It is in addition to the standard deduction and its age add-on, and it phases out as MAGI rises above $75,000, or $150,000 on a joint return.");
EX(1,"What is the 'no tax on tips' deduction?","A new temporary deduction that takes up to $25,000 of qualified tips out of taxable income for workers in jobs that customarily receive tips. The tips must still be reported, and payroll taxes still apply.");
EX(1,"What is the 'no tax on overtime' deduction?","A new temporary deduction for the extra pay earned for overtime, meaning the half in time-and-a-half, up to $12,500 or $25,000 joint. Only overtime required by the Fair Labor Standards Act counts.");
EX(1,"What is the car loan interest deduction?","A new temporary deduction for interest on a loan taken after 2024 to buy a new personal vehicle that had its final assembly in the U.S. The limit is $10,000 a year, and it is available without itemizing.");
EX(1,"What is the investment interest deduction?","Interest on money borrowed to buy taxable investments is an itemized deduction, but only up to the net investment income you report. Unused interest carries forward.");
EX(1,"QBI","Qualified business income is the net profit from a U.S. business run as a sole proprietorship, partnership or S corporation. It does not include investment income or wages. The deduction is 20% of it, subject to limits.");
EX(1,"SALT","SALT means state and local taxes. On Schedule A it covers income tax or sales tax plus real estate and personal property taxes, subject to the cap ($40,000 for 2025).");
EX(1,"FLSA","The Fair Labor Standards Act is the federal law that requires time-and-a-half for hours over 40 in a week. Only overtime required by this law qualifies for the new overtime deduction; overtime owed only under a state law or a union contract does not.");
EX(1,"OBBBA","The One Big Beautiful Bill Act is the tax law signed on July 4, 2025. It made the 2017 tax cuts permanent and added new items such as the tips, overtime, car loan interest and senior deductions. Its 2025 changes are tested in this exam window.");
EX(1,"TCJA","The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 nearly doubled the standard deduction, removed personal exemptions, capped the SALT deduction and created the 20% business income deduction. Most of its individual rules were set to end after 2025 before the new law extended them.");
EX(1,"ACA","The Affordable Care Act created the health insurance Marketplace and the premium tax credit, and also the 3.8% net investment income tax and the 0.9% Additional Medicare Tax.");
EX(1,"APTC","The advance premium tax credit is the premium tax credit paid during the year straight to the insurance company to lower monthly premiums. Anyone who received it must file a return with Form 8962 to reconcile it.");
EX(1,"Phase-out","A phase-out reduces a tax benefit step by step as income rises through a range. Below the range you get the full benefit; above it, none. Example: the IRA deduction shrinks evenly across its $10,000 range.");
EX(1,"Carryforward and carryback","When a loss, deduction or credit cannot be used this year, the law may let you use it in another year. Forward means a later year; back means an earlier year, by amending or claiming a refund. Each item has its own time limit.");
EX(1,"Acquisition debt","Acquisition debt is a loan used to buy, build or substantially improve your main or second home and secured by that home. Only interest on this kind of debt qualifies for the home mortgage interest deduction.");
EX(1,"Points","Points are a fee paid to the lender at closing, each point equal to 1% of the loan, in exchange for a lower rate. They are treated as interest paid in advance.");
EX(1,"Qualified higher education expenses","For education credits, qualified expenses are tuition and fees the school requires for enrollment. The American Opportunity credit also counts required books and supplies. Living costs such as room, board and transportation never count.");
EX(1,"2025 tax brackets: Single","Income is taxed in layers. Each rate applies only to the income inside its layer, not to all your income. Example for a single person with taxable income of $60,000: 10% on the first $11,925, 12% on the next $36,550, and 22% only on the last $11,525.");
EX(1,"2025 tax brackets: MFJ","The joint brackets for the lower rates are exactly double the single brackets: the 10% bracket ends at $23,850 and the 12% bracket at $96,950. Only income above each line is taxed at the next rate.");
EX(1,"Kiddie tax applies to","The kiddie tax covers children under 18, and also 18-year-olds and full-time students aged 19 to 23 who do not earn more than half of their own support. At least one parent must be alive, and the child must not file a joint return.");
EX(1,"Form 8615","Form 8615 is attached to the child's own return. It figures the tax on the child's unearned income above $2,700 using the parents' tax rate.");
EX(1,"Form 8814","In place of a separate return for the child, parents may add the child's income to their own return with Form 8814. It is allowed only if the child had just interest and dividends, under $13,500, with no tax withheld or estimated payments. It is simpler but raises the parents' AGI.");
EX(1,"2025 AMT exemption","The AMT is a second way of figuring tax that allows fewer deductions. The exemption is an amount of AMT income that is free of AMT, which keeps most people out of it: $88,100 single and $137,000 joint for 2025.");
EX(1,"2025 AMT exemption phase-out starts","The AMT exemption is taken away from very high earners. For each dollar of AMT income above $626,350 single or $1,252,700 joint, the exemption drops by 25 cents.");
EX(1,"AMT rates","The AMT has two rates. 26% applies to the first $239,100 of AMT income after the exemption, and 28% to the rest. The result is compared with regular tax, and you pay the higher one.");
EX(1,"Common AMT adjustments and preferences","To figure the AMT you add back items the regular tax allows. State and local taxes and the standard deduction are not allowed. The bargain on exercising incentive stock options is added as income, and so is interest on private activity municipal bonds.");
EX(1,"Deduction for SE tax","A self-employed person pays both halves of Social Security and Medicare tax. The half that an employer would have paid is deducted as an adjustment to income. It lowers income tax, not the self-employment tax itself.");
EX(1,"Additional Medicare Tax","High earners pay an extra 0.9% Medicare tax on wages and self-employment income above $200,000 single or $250,000 joint. An employer starts withholding once wages pass $200,000 no matter the filing status, so the right amount is settled on Form 8959.");
EX(1,"Who must pay estimated tax","Tax must be paid as income is earned. If you expect to owe at least $1,000 after subtracting withholding and credits, you must make quarterly estimated payments. This mostly affects the self-employed, landlords and investors.");
EX(1,"2025 estimated tax due dates","Estimated payments are due four times, and the periods are not equal: April 15, June 15, September 15 and January 15 of the next year. When a date falls on a weekend it moves to the next business day, which is why June 16 applied in 2025.");
EX(1,"Farmers and fishermen estimated tax","Farm and fishing income often arrives once a year. If at least two-thirds of gross income comes from farming or fishing, only one estimated payment is needed, by January 15. It can be skipped completely by filing the return and paying all tax by March 1.");
EX(1,"Form 2210","Form 2210 figures the penalty for paying too little estimated tax. Most people can let the IRS compute it. You file it yourself to ask for a waiver or to use the annualized income method.");
EX(1,"Failure-to-file penalty","Filing late costs 5% of the unpaid tax for each month or part of a month, up to 25%. It is based on tax not paid, so a late return with a refund has no penalty. If the delay is fraudulent the rate is 15% a month, up to 75%.");
EX(1,"Failure-to-pay penalty","Paying late costs 0.5% of the unpaid tax per month, up to 25%. It is ten times smaller than the late filing penalty, so always file on time even if you cannot pay. The rate drops to 0.25% during an installment agreement and rises to 1% after a levy notice.");
EX(1,"When both FTF and FTP apply in the same month","When a return is both late and unpaid, the two penalties are not simply added. The late filing penalty is reduced by the late payment penalty for that month, so the total is 5% a month: 4.5% for filing and 0.5% for paying.");
EX(1,"Accuracy-related penalty","When a return understates tax because of carelessness or a large error, the penalty is 20% of the underpaid tax. For an individual, large means the understatement is more than the greater of 10% of the correct tax or $5,000.");
EX(1,"Household employer: when Schedule H is needed (2025)","A person who pays a nanny, housekeeper or caregiver can be an employer. If cash wages to one worker reach $2,800 in 2025, Social Security and Medicare taxes are due. If total wages reach $1,000 in any quarter, federal unemployment tax is due. Both are reported on Schedule H with Form 1040.");
EX(1,"Underpayment interest rate (individuals)","Interest on unpaid tax is set each quarter: the federal short-term rate plus 3 percentage points, compounded daily. It runs from the original due date, even during an extension, and normally cannot be waived.");
EX(1,"How long to keep tax records","Keep records as long as the IRS can still question the return. Usually that is 3 years from filing. It is 6 years if more than 25% of income was left off, and there is no limit if no return was filed or it was fraudulent. Keep property records until 3 years after you sell the property.");
EX(1,"Employment tax records","Employers must keep payroll tax records, such as wages paid and deposits made, for at least 4 years after the tax is due or paid, whichever is later.");
EX(1,"Refund claim deadline","To get money back you must claim it within 3 years from the date the return was filed, or 2 years from the date the tax was paid, whichever is later. A return filed early counts as filed on the due date.");
EX(1,"Amended individual return","To correct a return already filed, an individual files Form 1040-X. It shows the original amounts, the changes and the corrected amounts, with an explanation. It can be filed electronically.");
EX(1,"Property transfers between spouses or incident to divorce (§1041)","Property moved between spouses, or to a former spouse as part of a divorce, is treated like a gift for income tax: no gain or loss, and the receiver takes over the giver's basis. So the built-in gain moves to the one who receives the property.");
EX(1,"Name change after marriage","The IRS matches the name and Social Security number on a return against Social Security records. After a name change, tell the Social Security Administration first; if not, an e-filed return is rejected or the refund is delayed.");
EX(1,"Withholding adjustment form","Withholding is set by a form given to the payer. Employees use Form W-4. People receiving regular pension payments use Form W-4P, and Form W-4R is for one-time retirement payments.");
EX(1,"Divorce in 2025: filing status","Marital status is decided on December 31. A person whose divorce is final by that date is unmarried for the entire year, and files as Single, or as Head of Household if they qualify.");
EX(1,"Individual mandate penalty","The law still says people should have health coverage, but the federal penalty for not having it has been $0 since 2019. Nothing is owed on the federal return. Some states have their own penalty.");
EX(1,"Uncollected Social Security/Medicare on tips","Sometimes an employer cannot collect the employee's share of Social Security and Medicare tax on tips because wages were too small. The unpaid amount appears in box 12 with codes A and B, and the employee pays it on the return through Schedule 2.");
EX(1,"Form 4868 and payment","Form 4868 gives 6 more months to file, to October 15. It does not give more time to pay. Tax not paid by April 15 is charged interest and the late payment penalty, so you estimate what you owe and pay with the extension.");
EX(1,"Backup withholding","Normally no tax is withheld from interest or dividends. If you do not give the payer a correct tax ID number, or the IRS says you underreported, the payer must hold back 24% and send it to the IRS. You claim it as a payment on your return.");
EX(1,"Supplemental wage withholding","Bonuses, commissions and similar extra pay can have income tax withheld at a flat 22%. Once extra pay passes $1 million in a year, the part above that must be withheld at 37%.");
EX(1,"Additional taxes reported on Schedule 2","Schedule 2 collects taxes that are added on top of the regular income tax: the AMT, repayment of excess advance premium credit, self-employment tax, the 10% tax on early retirement distributions, household employment tax, Additional Medicare Tax and the net investment income tax.");
EX(1,"Marriage penalty and bonus","Two people with similar high incomes can owe more tax married than they would as two single people: a marriage penalty. When one spouse earns most of the income, the joint brackets usually lower the total: a marriage bonus.");
EX(1,"When MFS can be better","Filing separately usually costs more, but it can help in a few cases: one spouse does not want to be liable for the other's tax, one spouse has large medical bills measured against a smaller AGI, or student loan payments are based on separate income.");
EX(1,"Withholding check","If too little or too much tax is being taken from pay, the fix is a new Form W-4 given to the employer. The IRS online estimator helps choose the entries. People with income that has no withholding make estimated payments with Form 1040-ES.");
EX(1,"Tax consequences of a home purchase","Buying a home changes the return. Mortgage interest and property tax may now exceed the standard deduction, and points on the purchase may be deductible. The closing statement and records of improvements should be kept because they set the basis for a later sale.");
EX(1,"Tax consequences of a death in the family","A death creates several filings: a final Form 1040, perhaps Form 1041 for the estate's income and Form 706 for a large estate. The survivor may file jointly that year. Heirs take inherited property at its date-of-death value, but income the decedent had earned is taxed to whoever receives it.");
EX(1,"Education planning options","Several tax benefits help pay for education: 529 plans, Coverdell accounts, the savings bond interest exclusion, the two education credits and the student loan interest deduction. The same dollar of expense can be used for only one benefit, so they must be planned together.");
EX(1,"Annualized income installment method","The normal penalty calculation assumes income came in evenly through the year. If most income came late in the year, for example a December bonus or a business with a busy season, Schedule AI figures required payments from actual income in each period, which lowers or removes the penalty.");
EX(1,"Minimum failure-to-file penalty","If a return is more than 60 days late, there is a minimum late filing penalty: for 2025 returns (due in 2026) it is $525, or 100% of the unpaid tax if that is smaller. So even a small balance can bring a noticeable penalty.");
EX(1,"Ministers: dual status","A minister is an employee for income tax and gets a Form W-2, but for Social Security the minister is treated as self-employed. No Social Security or Medicare tax is withheld; the minister pays self-employment tax on salary plus housing allowance. A minister opposed on religious grounds can ask for exemption with Form 4361.");
EX(1,"Form 1040-X","Form 1040-X corrects a return that was already filed, for example to add income, change filing status or claim a missed deduction. To get a refund it must be filed within 3 years of the original filing or 2 years of payment.");
EX(1,"Form 1040-ES","Form 1040-ES contains a worksheet to estimate the year's tax and vouchers for the four quarterly payments. It is used by people whose income has no withholding.");
EX(1,"Schedule 2","Schedule 2 has two parts. Part I has the AMT and repayment of excess advance premium tax credit. Part II has other taxes such as self-employment tax, the additional tax on retirement accounts, household employment tax, Additional Medicare Tax and the 3.8% net investment income tax.");
EX(1,"Schedule H","Schedule H is attached to Form 1040 by someone who employs household help. It reports Social Security, Medicare and federal unemployment taxes on the worker's wages, so the employer pays them with the personal return.");
EX(1,"Schedule J","Farm and fishing income can swing from year to year. Schedule J lets the farmer spread part of this year's farm income over the 3 earlier years' tax rates, which lowers tax in a good year. The earlier returns are not amended.");
EX(1,"Schedule SE","Schedule SE figures self-employment tax: 15.3% of 92.35% of net profit (12.4% Social Security up to the wage base and 2.9% Medicare). It is required when net self-employment earnings are $400 or more.");
EX(1,"Form W-4","An employee fills in Form W-4 so the employer knows how much income tax to take from each paycheck. It reflects filing status, other jobs, dependents and other adjustments. A new one can be given at any time.");
EX(1,"Form 6251","Form 6251 figures the alternative minimum tax. It starts with taxable income, adds back the AMT adjustments, subtracts the exemption and applies the 26% and 28% rates. You pay AMT only if the result is more than regular tax.");
EX(1,"Form 8959","Form 8959 figures the 0.9% Additional Medicare Tax on wages and self-employment income above the threshold, and compares it with what employers withheld.");
EX(1,"Form 8960","Form 8960 figures the 3.8% net investment income tax. It totals investment income, subtracts related expenses, and applies the tax to the smaller of that amount or MAGI above the threshold.");
EX(1,"Form 4868","Form 4868 gives an automatic 6-month extension to file Form 1040, until October 15. No reason is needed. It must be filed by the original due date, and it does not extend the time to pay.");
EX(1,"Form 9465","Form 9465 asks the IRS for a monthly payment plan when the tax cannot be paid in full. Interest and a reduced late payment penalty keep running until the balance is paid.");
EX(1,"Form 8857","Form 8857 asks the IRS to relieve one spouse from tax on a joint return that was caused by the other spouse's errors. It is generally due within 2 years after the IRS first tries to collect from the requesting spouse.");
EX(1,"Form 8379","Form 8379 is filed by a spouse whose share of a joint refund was, or will be, taken to pay the other spouse's past debts, such as child support or a student loan. It splits the refund so the injured spouse gets their part.");
EX(1,"AMT","The alternative minimum tax makes sure people with many deductions still pay some tax. You figure tax a second way, with fewer deductions and an exemption, at 26% and 28%. If this is more than the regular tax, you pay the difference as AMT.");
EX(1,"AMTI","Alternative minimum taxable income is taxable income after adding back the items the AMT does not allow, such as state and local taxes and the bargain on incentive stock options. The exemption is subtracted from it before applying the AMT rates.");
EX(1,"FICA","FICA is the payroll tax law. The employee pays 6.2% Social Security (up to the wage base, $176,100 for 2025) and 1.45% Medicare, and the employer pays the same. Self-employed people pay both halves as self-employment tax.");
EX(1,"Marginal tax rate","The marginal rate is the rate on your next dollar of income, meaning the top bracket you reach. It tells you how much a deduction saves. Example: in the 22% bracket, a $1,000 deduction saves $220.");
EX(1,"Effective tax rate","The effective rate is your average rate: total tax divided by income. Because lower layers of income are taxed at lower rates, it is always below the marginal rate. Example: $9,000 of tax on $75,000 of income is 12%.");
EX(1,"Withholding","Withholding is tax taken out of a payment before you receive it, mainly from wages. It is treated as paid evenly through the year, whenever it was actually withheld, which helps avoid the estimated tax penalty.");
EX(1,"Safe harbor","A safe harbor is a clear test that protects you from a penalty if you meet it, no matter how things turn out. The best known: no estimated tax penalty if you paid at least 100% of last year's tax (110% for higher incomes) or 90% of this year's.");
EX(1,"Statute of limitations","The statute of limitations is a deadline in the law. The IRS generally has 3 years from filing to assess more tax, and a taxpayer has 3 years from filing or 2 years from payment to claim a refund. After that, the year is closed.");
EX(1,"Joint and several liability","On a joint return each spouse is fully responsible for all the tax, interest and penalties, even on income the other spouse earned, and even after a divorce. Innocent spouse relief is the exception to this rule.");
EX(1,"What is the Credit for the Elderly or the Disabled?","A small credit for people who are 65 or older, or who retired early because of permanent and total disability, and whose income is very low. It is figured on Schedule R and can only reduce tax to zero.");
EX(1,"What is the Clean Vehicle Credit?","A credit for buying a qualifying electric or plug-in vehicle: up to $7,500 new or $4,000 used, with income and price limits. The new law ended it for vehicles acquired after September 30, 2025.");
EX(1,"Capital gain distribution","A mutual fund sells investments during the year and pays its net long-term gains to shareholders. You report the payment as long-term capital gain no matter how long you have owned the fund shares.");
EX(2,"Carryover cheat sheet: C corporations","A C corporation's unused items follow different rules from an individual's. Capital losses go back 3 years and forward 5, and only against capital gains. Charitable gifts over the limit carry forward 5 years. Net operating losses carry forward without limit but can offset only 80% of a later year's income.");
EX(2,"S corp election termination","S status ends in two ways. The owners can choose to end it, if holders of more than half the shares agree. Or the corporation stops qualifying, for example by getting a 101st shareholder. After it ends, the corporation normally must wait 5 years to elect again.");
EX(2,"Corporate liquidation","When a corporation closes and hands out its assets, there are two levels of tax. The corporation is taxed as if it sold every asset at market value. Each shareholder then has gain or loss: the value received minus their stock basis. A parent closing a subsidiary it owns 80% or more pays no tax.");
EX(2,"Stock redemption (§302)","When a corporation buys back its own stock, the question is whether the shareholder really reduced their ownership. If yes (all shares redeemed, or a large enough drop), it is a sale with capital gain. If the shareholder's percentage barely changes, the payment is treated as a dividend.");
EX(2,"Earnings and profits (E&P)","Earnings and profits is a tax-law measure of how much a corporation could pay out without returning capital. It starts from taxable income and adjusts toward economic reality: add tax-exempt income, subtract federal income tax paid, and use slower depreciation.");
EX(2,"Constructive dividends","A payment does not need to be called a dividend to be one. If a corporation gives a shareholder a benefit with no business reason, such as paying personal bills, paying too much salary, or lending money at no interest, the IRS treats the benefit as a dividend: taxable to the shareholder and not deductible by the corporation.");
EX(2,"Limited partner self-employment tax","A limited partner is an investor, not a worker, so the share of partnership profit is not subject to self-employment tax. If the limited partner is paid a guaranteed payment for actual services, that payment is subject to self-employment tax.");
EX(2,"Partnership: Schedule K-1 due","A partnership must give each partner a Schedule K-1 by the due date of Form 1065: March 15 for a calendar year, or September 15 with an extension. Partners need it to file their own returns.");
EX(2,"Liquidating distribution: when does a partner recognize gain?","When a partner leaves and the partnership pays them out, gain arises only if the money received is more than the partner's outside basis. Being relieved of a share of partnership debt counts as money. Receiving property alone does not trigger gain; the basis moves to the property.");
EX(2,"Liquidating distribution: when does a partner recognize a loss?","A loss on leaving a partnership is allowed only in a narrow case: the partner receives only cash, unrealized receivables and inventory, and their total is less than the partner's outside basis. If any other property is received, there is no loss; the leftover basis attaches to that property.");
EX(2,"Liquidating distribution: basis of property received","In a final payout the partner's whole outside basis must go somewhere. First subtract cash. Then receivables and inventory take the partnership's own basis. Whatever basis remains is assigned to the other property. Example: outside basis $50,000, cash $10,000, inventory with basis $15,000, and land: the land takes $25,000.");
EX(2,"Sale of a partnership interest: amount realized","When a partner sells their interest, the price includes more than cash. The partner's share of partnership debt that the buyer takes over is added. Example: $40,000 cash and $10,000 of debt relief gives an amount realized of $50,000.");
EX(2,"Sale of a partnership interest: character","A partnership interest is a capital asset, so the sale normally gives capital gain or loss. But the part of the price paid for the partner's share of unrealized receivables and inventory (hot assets) is ordinary income, so a partner cannot convert ordinary income to capital gain by selling.");
EX(2,"Partner's share of liabilities","Partnership debt increases the partners' basis, so it must be divided among them. Recourse debt goes to the partner who would have to pay it if the partnership could not. Nonrecourse debt, where no partner is personally liable, is generally shared by profit ratio.");
EX(2,"Partnership tax year for a partner who sells the entire interest","When a partner sells their entire interest, the partnership's year ends for that partner on the sale date. The partner reports their share of income up to that day. The partnership itself continues with its normal year for everyone else.");
EX(2,"When a partnership terminates","A partnership ends for tax only when no partner continues any part of its business in partnership form. Since 2018 a sale of 50% or more of the interests no longer ends it.");
EX(2,"Converting a partnership to a corporation","Partners can move their business into a corporation tax-free under Section 351, because together they own at least 80% of the new corporation right after the transfer. Their basis and holding period carry over into the stock.");
EX(2,"LLC electing S corporation status","An LLC that wants S corporation treatment files only Form 2553. By filing it, the LLC is treated as having also chosen to be taxed as a corporation, so a separate Form 8832 is not needed.");
EX(2,"Late S election relief","If the S election was filed late, relief is available when the corporation meant to be an S corporation from that date, has reasonable cause, and asks within 3 years and 75 days of the intended start date. Form 2553 is filed with a statement explaining the delay.");
EX(2,"S election revocation","To give up S status voluntarily, shareholders owning more than half the shares must consent. If the revocation is filed by the 15th day of the 3rd month, it applies for the whole year. Filed later, it takes effect the next year, unless a specific future date is named.");
EX(2,"S termination by a disqualifying event","If the corporation breaks an S rule, such as gaining a nonresident alien shareholder, the election ends on that very day. The year is split in two short years: an S corporation year up to the day before, and a C corporation year from the event.");
EX(2,"Re-electing S after termination","After S status ends, the corporation generally cannot elect S again for 5 tax years unless the IRS agrees. If the termination was accidental and is fixed quickly, the IRS can treat the election as never having ended.");
EX(2,"LIFO recapture on C to S conversion","LIFO inventory holds old, low costs, so it hides profit. A C corporation that becomes an S corporation must add its LIFO reserve to income in its last C corporation year. The extra tax is paid in 4 equal yearly installments.");
EX(2,"S corp debt basis restoration","When losses have reduced a shareholder's loan basis, later profit rebuilds the loan basis first and stock basis second. If the corporation repays the loan while its basis is still reduced, part of the repayment is gain to the shareholder.");
EX(2,"Suspended S corp losses","Losses that exceed a shareholder's basis are not lost; they wait until basis is restored. They stay with that shareholder and cannot be passed on by sale. If S status ends, they can still be used during a transition period of about one year if the shareholder adds basis.");
EX(2,"C corporation complete liquidation","Closing a C corporation is taxed twice. First the corporation pays tax as if it sold its assets at market value. Then each shareholder pays tax on what they receive minus their stock basis.");
EX(2,"Late filing penalty: Form 1065 and 1120-S (2025 returns)","A partnership or S corporation return usually shows no tax, so the penalty for filing late is based on the number of owners: $255 for each partner or shareholder for each month late, up to 12 months, for 2025 returns. Example: 4 partners, 3 months late: $3,060.");
EX(2,"Late filing penalty: Form 1120","A C corporation pays its own tax, so its late filing penalty works like an individual's: 5% of the unpaid tax per month up to 25%. Paying too little estimated tax is figured separately on Form 2220.");
EX(2,"Default classification of a single-member LLC","An LLC with one owner is ignored for income tax unless it elects otherwise. An individual owner reports the business on Schedule C, E or F, as if the LLC did not exist. It can choose to be taxed as a corporation with Form 8832.");
EX(2,"Default classification of a multi-member LLC","An LLC with two or more owners is taxed as a partnership by default and files Form 1065. It can elect to be taxed as a corporation.");
EX(2,"Form 8832","Form 8832 is how an eligible entity such as an LLC chooses its tax classification: corporation, partnership or disregarded entity. Once changed, it generally cannot be changed again for 60 months.");
EX(2,"Form 2553","Form 2553 elects S corporation status. To apply for the current year it must be filed within 2 months and 15 days after the year starts (March 15 for a calendar year), or any time during the year before. Every shareholder must sign.");
EX(2,"S corporation eligibility","Only a small, simple U.S. corporation can be an S corporation: no more than 100 shareholders, all of them eligible (U.S. individuals, estates and certain trusts), and only one class of stock.");
EX(2,"Who cannot be an S corp shareholder","Certain owners make a corporation ineligible for S status: nonresident aliens, partnerships and other corporations. The idea is that the income must be taxed once to a U.S. individual. If one of them gets even one share, the election ends.");
EX(2,"S corp shareholder count: family","For the 100-shareholder limit, a married couple counts as one shareholder, and a whole family (up to six generations from a common ancestor) can be treated as one. So an S corporation can have well over 100 actual people as owners.");
EX(2,"S corp: one class of stock","All shares must give the same rights to distributions and to assets on liquidation. Shares may differ in voting power, so voting and nonvoting common stock is allowed. Preferred stock is not.");
EX(2,"Due date: Form 1065 and Form 1120-S (calendar year)","Partnerships and S corporations file a month before individuals, so owners get their Schedule K-1 in time. For a calendar year the due date is March 15, and Form 7004 extends it 6 months to September 15.");
EX(2,"Due date: Form 1120 (calendar year)","A calendar-year C corporation files Form 1120 by April 15, the 15th day of the 4th month after year end. Form 7004 gives 6 more months, to October 15.");
EX(2,"C corporation tax rate","A C corporation pays one flat rate of 21% on all taxable income, with no brackets. The owners are taxed again when profit is paid out as dividends.");
EX(2,"Personal holding company tax","A personal holding company is a closely held corporation that earns mostly investment income. To stop people from using a corporation as a low-tax pocket for investments, a 20% tax is added on the income it does not pay out.");
EX(2,"Section 351 transfer","Starting a corporation should not be a taxable event. If the people transferring property own at least 80% of the stock right afterward, no gain or loss is recognized. If they also receive cash or other property (boot), gain is taxed up to that amount.");
EX(2,"Section 721 contribution","Putting property into a partnership in exchange for an interest is tax-free, with no 80% control requirement. The partnership takes over the partner's basis in the property, and the partner's interest gets that same basis.");
EX(2,"Partner's outside basis: increases","Outside basis is the partner's running investment. It goes up for money and property contributed, for the partner's share of income (even tax-exempt income), and for any increase in their share of partnership debt.");
EX(2,"Partner's outside basis: decreases","Outside basis goes down for distributions, for the partner's share of losses and nondeductible expenses, and for any decrease in their share of partnership debt. It can never go below zero.");
EX(2,"Partnership loss limits (in order)","A partner's loss must pass four tests in order. First, it cannot exceed outside basis. Second, the amount at risk. Third, the passive activity rules. Fourth, the excess business loss limit. A loss stopped at any step is carried forward under that step's rules.");
EX(2,"Cash distribution greater than partner's basis","A distribution of money is tax-free until it exceeds the partner's outside basis. The extra is capital gain. Example: basis $10,000, cash distribution $14,000: $4,000 capital gain and basis becomes zero.");
EX(2,"Basis of property distributed to a partner (nonliquidating)","When a partnership distributes property to a continuing partner, the partner normally takes the partnership's basis in it. But it cannot be more than the partner's outside basis after subtracting any cash received in the same distribution.");
EX(2,"Section 751 'hot assets'","Hot assets are the partnership items that would produce ordinary income: unrealized receivables (including depreciation recapture) and inventory. When a partner sells an interest, the gain tied to them is ordinary income, not capital gain.");
EX(2,"Partnership tax year rules (order)","A partnership cannot freely pick its tax year. It must use the year of partners owning more than 50%. If there is none, the year of all the principal partners (5% or more). If still none, the year that defers the least income.");
EX(2,"Centralized partnership audit regime (BBA)","Since 2018 the IRS audits a partnership at the partnership level and collects any tax from the partnership itself. The partnership names a partnership representative with full authority to act. A partnership with 100 or fewer eligible partners can elect out each year on its return.");
EX(2,"S corp shareholder basis: ordering","An S corporation shareholder adjusts stock basis each year in a set order: first add income, then subtract distributions, then nondeductible expenses, then losses. Because distributions come before losses, a distribution is more likely to be tax-free.");
EX(2,"S corp shareholder loss limit","A shareholder can deduct losses only up to stock basis plus loans the shareholder personally made to the corporation. Guaranteeing a bank loan to the corporation creates no basis, unlike a partner, who gets basis from partnership debt.");
EX(2,"S corp distributions (no C corp E&P)","If the S corporation never had C corporation earnings, a distribution is simply a return of the shareholder's investment: tax-free up to stock basis, and capital gain above it.");
EX(2,"S corp distributions with accumulated E&P","An S corporation that was once a C corporation may still hold old earnings. Distributions come first from the account of income already taxed to shareholders (AAA), which is tax-free. Next they come from the old earnings, taxed as dividends. After that they reduce basis.");
EX(2,"S corp reasonable compensation","S corporation profit passed through to owners is not subject to payroll tax, so owners are tempted to take no salary. The IRS requires an owner who works in the business to be paid a reasonable wage, with payroll taxes, before taking distributions.");
EX(2,"2% S corp shareholder health insurance","Health insurance the S corporation pays for an owner of more than 2% is added to that owner's wages for income tax, but not for Social Security and Medicare. The owner can then deduct it as self-employed health insurance.");
EX(2,"S corp excess passive investment income","This applies only to an S corporation that has old C corporation earnings. If more than 25% of its gross receipts are passive investment income, it pays a corporate-level tax on the excess. If that happens 3 years in a row, the S election ends.");
EX(2,"Corporate charitable contribution limit (2025)","A C corporation can deduct charitable gifts only up to 10% of its taxable income, figured before the gift deduction and the dividends-received deduction. Any excess carries forward 5 years.");
EX(2,"Corporate capital losses","A corporation gets no $3,000 allowance. Its capital losses can be used only against capital gains. Unused losses go back 3 years and then forward 5 years, treated as short-term.");
EX(2,"Net operating loss (post-2020)","A net operating loss from recent years cannot be carried back, except for farm losses (2 years). It carries forward with no time limit, but in any later year it can offset only 80% of taxable income.");
EX(2,"Corporate estimated tax","A corporation must pay estimated tax if it expects to owe $500 or more. Payments are due on the 15th day of the 4th, 6th, 9th and 12th months of its year. A large corporation cannot rely on last year's tax as a safe harbor, except for the first payment.");
EX(2,"Schedule M-1 vs M-3","Income on the financial books differs from taxable income. Schedule M-1 explains the difference, for example federal tax expense, meals and tax-exempt interest. Corporations with total assets of $10 million or more must use the more detailed Schedule M-3.");
EX(2,"Schedule M-2 (Form 1120)","Schedule M-2 shows how retained earnings changed during the year: start with the opening balance, add net income per books, subtract dividends, and arrive at the closing balance.");
EX(2,"Corporate distributions","A distribution to a shareholder is taxed in three layers. It is a dividend up to the corporation's earnings and profits, current year first, then accumulated. Next it is a tax-free return of capital that lowers stock basis. Anything beyond basis is capital gain.");
EX(2,"Below-market loan from corporation to shareholder","If a corporation lends money to a shareholder at little or no interest, the tax law creates the missing interest. The unpaid interest is treated as a dividend to the shareholder. If the borrower is an employee, it is treated as pay.");
EX(2,"Section 1244 stock","Stock losses are normally capital losses. Section 1244 lets the first owner of stock in a small corporation deduct a loss as ordinary, up to $50,000 a year or $100,000 on a joint return, which is far more useful.");
EX(2,"Qualified business income (QBI) deduction","Owners of sole proprietorships, partnerships and S corporations can deduct 20% of their qualified business income. It was created to balance the 21% corporate rate, and the new law made it permanent. C corporations do not get it.");
EX(2,"2025 QBI threshold","Below the taxable income threshold ($197,300, or $394,600 joint, for 2025) the 20% deduction is simple. Above it, limits phase in across the next $50,000 ($100,000 joint): the wage and property limit, and the loss of the deduction for service businesses.");
EX(2,"QBI W-2 wage/UBIA limit","For higher-income owners the deduction cannot exceed the greater of two amounts: 50% of the W-2 wages the business paid, or 25% of wages plus 2.5% of the original cost of its depreciable property. A business with no employees and no property gets nothing above the range.");
EX(2,"Form 8995 vs 8995-A","Form 8995 is the short version for taxpayers at or below the income threshold. Above it, Form 8995-A is required because the wage and property limits and the service business rules must be applied.");
EX(2,"Specified service trade or business (SSTB)","A specified service business sells the skill of its people: health, law, accounting, consulting, performing arts, athletics, financial services. Once taxable income is above the phase-in range, these businesses get no 20% deduction. Engineers and architects are not on the list.");
EX(2,"Hobby loss rule","A business can deduct losses; a hobby cannot. If the activity made a profit in 3 of the last 5 years (2 of 7 for horse breeding), it is presumed to be a business. A hobby must report its income and cannot deduct its expenses.");
EX(2,"Cash method restriction","The cash method is simple but lets income be shifted between years. So C corporations, and partnerships with a C corporation partner, must use accrual unless their average gross receipts are $31 million or less.");
EX(2,"2025 small business gross receipts test","A business with average yearly gross receipts of $31 million or less over the prior 3 years counts as small. It may use the cash method, skip inventory capitalization rules, and is not subject to the business interest limit.");
EX(2,"Form 3115 / Form 1128","A business cannot simply switch how it reports. Changing an accounting method, for example cash to accrual, requires Form 3115. Changing the tax year requires Form 1128.");
EX(2,"Business interest expense limit (§163(j))","A larger business can deduct interest only up to its interest income plus 30% of adjusted taxable income. For 2025 that income is figured before depreciation and amortization, which raises the limit. Interest not allowed carries forward. Small businesses under the gross receipts test are exempt.");
EX(2,"2025 excess business loss limit (noncorporate)","An individual cannot use unlimited business losses against wages and investment income. For 2025 the net business loss allowed is $313,000, or $626,000 on a joint return. The excess becomes a net operating loss carried to later years.");
EX(2,"Qualified small business stock (§1202)","Gain on stock of a qualifying small C corporation, bought at original issue, can be excluded from income. The classic rule requires holding more than 5 years. The new law added partial exclusions at 3 and 4 years and a larger cap for stock issued after its enactment.");
EX(2,"Form 1065","Form 1065 is the partnership's information return. The partnership pays no income tax; the form reports its income and deductions and gives each partner a Schedule K-1 with their share.");
EX(2,"Form 1120","Form 1120 is the income tax return of a C corporation. The corporation figures and pays its own tax at 21%.");
EX(2,"Form 1120-S","Form 1120-S is the return of an S corporation. Like a partnership it generally pays no tax; income and deductions pass to shareholders on Schedule K-1 in proportion to their shares.");
EX(2,"Form 7004","Form 7004 gives an automatic extension to file business returns, usually 6 months. It does not extend the time to pay any tax due.");
EX(2,"Form SS-4","Form SS-4 is the application for an employer identification number. A business needs one to hire employees, open a business bank account or file business returns.");
EX(2,"Form 3115","Form 3115 asks the IRS for permission to change an accounting method, such as cash to accrual or a change in depreciation. Many changes are automatic if the form is filed correctly.");
EX(2,"Form 1128","Form 1128 is used to adopt, change or keep a tax year, for example to move from a calendar year to a fiscal year.");
EX(2,"Form 8990","Form 8990 figures how much business interest expense can be deducted under the 30% limit and how much carries forward. Small businesses under the gross receipts test do not file it.");
EX(2,"Form 461","Form 461 figures the excess business loss of an individual: the amount of net business loss above the yearly limit, which is carried forward as a net operating loss.");
EX(2,"Form 1125-A","Form 1125-A figures cost of goods sold: opening inventory plus purchases and production costs, minus closing inventory. It is attached to Forms 1065, 1120 and 1120-S.");
EX(2,"Form 2220","A corporation uses Form 2220 to figure the penalty for paying too little estimated tax during the year.");
EX(2,"Form 1120-W","Form 1120-W is a worksheet a corporation uses to estimate its tax and the four required installments. It is not filed with the IRS.");
EX(2,"Form 7203","An S corporation shareholder uses Form 7203 to track stock basis and loan basis. It must be attached when the shareholder claims a loss, receives a distribution, sells stock or is repaid a loan.");
EX(2,"LLC","A limited liability company is formed under state law to protect its owners from the business's debts. It has no tax classification of its own: one owner means it is ignored, two or more means partnership, unless it elects to be taxed as a corporation.");
EX(2,"PSC","A personal service corporation is a C corporation whose main activity is services by its employee-owners in fields like health, law, engineering or accounting. It generally must use a calendar year and has a lower accumulated earnings credit.");
EX(2,"AAA","The accumulated adjustments account tracks S corporation income that has already been taxed to shareholders but not yet paid out. It matters when the corporation also has old C corporation earnings: distributions from this account come out first and are tax-free.");
EX(2,"DRD","The dividends-received deduction prevents the same profit from being taxed at three levels. A C corporation that receives a dividend from another U.S. corporation deducts 50% of it (owning under 20%), 65% (20% up to 80%) or 100% (80% or more).");
EX(2,"AET","The accumulated earnings tax is a 20% penalty on a C corporation that keeps profits beyond what the business reasonably needs, in order to spare shareholders the tax on dividends. The first $250,000 ($150,000 for service corporations) is protected.");
EX(2,"PHC","A personal holding company is a corporation in which 5 or fewer people own more than half the stock and at least 60% of its income is passive, such as dividends, interest and rents. It pays an extra 20% tax on income it does not distribute.");
EX(2,"BIG tax","When a C corporation becomes an S corporation, appreciation that built up during the C years is tracked. If those assets are sold within 5 years, the S corporation itself pays tax at 21% on that built-in gain.");
EX(2,"SSTB","A specified service trade or business is one based on personal skill, such as medicine, law, accounting or consulting. Its owners lose the 20% business income deduction once taxable income exceeds the phase-in range.");
EX(2,"UBIA","UBIA is the original cost of depreciable property the business still uses, without subtracting depreciation. 2.5% of it is part of the alternative limit on the 20% deduction, which helps businesses with much property and few employees, like real estate.");
EX(2,"ATI","Adjusted taxable income is the base for the business interest limit: 30% of it, plus interest income, is the most interest a business can deduct. For 2025 it is figured before interest, depreciation and amortization.");
EX(2,"QSub","A qualified subchapter S subsidiary is a corporation owned 100% by an S corporation that elects this status. For tax it does not exist separately: its income and assets are treated as the parent's.");
EX(2,"Earnings and profits","Earnings and profits measure how much a corporation can distribute as dividends. A distribution is a taxable dividend only up to this amount; beyond it, the payment is a return of capital.");
EX(2,"Pass-through entity","A pass-through entity does not pay income tax itself. Its income, deductions and credits pass to the owners, who report them on their own returns. Partnerships and S corporations are the main examples.");
EX(2,"Separately stated items","Some items must be shown separately on Schedule K-1 because their tax treatment depends on each owner's own situation: capital gains and losses, Section 179 deduction, charitable gifts, interest and dividends. Everything else is combined into ordinary business income.");
EX(2,"Outside basis vs inside basis","Outside basis is each partner's basis in their partnership interest. Inside basis is the partnership's basis in the assets it owns. They start out equal but can differ after someone buys an interest, which is why a Section 754 election exists.");
EX(2,"Distributive share","The distributive share is the part of partnership income, loss and credits assigned to a partner by the agreement. The partner is taxed on it when the partnership earns it, even if no money is paid out.");
EX(2,"Constructive receipt (business)","Under constructive receipt, income belongs to the year it was made available to you without real restrictions. A cash-method business cannot delay income by leaving a check uncashed in December.");
EX(2,"Fiscal year","A fiscal year is a tax year of 12 months ending on the last day of any month other than December, for example July 1 to June 30. C corporations can choose one freely; pass-through entities are restricted.");
EX(2,"Cash method","Under the cash method, income counts when money is actually or constructively received and expenses count when paid. It is simple and is allowed for most small businesses.");
EX(2,"Accrual method","Under the accrual method, income counts when you have the right to it and expenses when you owe them, regardless of when cash moves. Example: an invoice sent in December and paid in January is December income.");
EX(2,"Section 197 intangibles","When a business is bought, part of the price is often for things you cannot touch: goodwill, customer lists, a promise not to compete, licenses. These are deducted evenly over 15 years (180 months), starting the month of purchase, no matter how long they really last.");
EX(2,"Depletion","Depletion is depreciation for natural resources like oil, gas, minerals and timber. Cost depletion spreads the basis over the units expected and deducts for units sold. Percentage depletion is a fixed percent of gross income from the property and can continue even after basis is used up. Timber must use cost depletion.");
EX(2,"General business credit","Many business credits are combined into one general business credit on Form 3800, with one overall limit based on tax liability. Unused credit goes back 1 year and forward 20.");
EX(2,"Small employer health insurance credit","A small employer that pays at least half of its workers' health premiums through the SHOP Marketplace can get a credit of up to 50% of the premiums it pays. It must have fewer than 25 full-time equivalent employees with modest average wages, and can claim it for only 2 years in a row.");
EX(2,"Retirement plan startup credit","To encourage small businesses to start retirement plans, an employer with 100 or fewer employees gets a credit for the cost of setting up and running a new plan for its first 3 years, plus a credit for part of its contributions.");
EX(2,"Employer-provided snacks and on-premises meals (2025)","Meals an employer provides at the workplace for its own convenience, and snacks, are 50% deductible for 2025 and become nondeductible in 2026. Entertainment is never deductible. A party for all employees is fully deductible.");
EX(2,"Business use of a vehicle: actual expenses","With the actual expense method you total the real costs of the car, including depreciation, and multiply by the business-use percentage. Example: $9,000 of costs and 60% business use gives $5,400. Driving between home and a regular workplace is commuting and never counts.");
EX(2,"Repossession of personal property sold on installment","If a buyer stops paying on an installment sale of personal property and you take it back, you compare its market value with your remaining basis in the note plus repossession costs. The difference is gain or loss. Your basis in the returned property is its market value.");
EX(2,"Repossession of real property (§1038)","Taking back real estate you sold has a special rule so that you are not taxed on value you never got in cash. Gain is limited to the money you received before the repossession minus the gain you already reported. No loss is allowed.");
EX(2,"Basis in an installment obligation","Part of each dollar still owed to you is profit not yet taxed. Your basis in the note is the unpaid balance times the part that is not profit. Example: $60,000 still owed and a 40% gross profit percentage: basis is $36,000.");
EX(2,"MACRS first-year rates (half-year convention)","MACRS tables assume equipment was bought in the middle of the year, so the first year gets half a year of deduction. For 5-year property the first-year rate is 20% and the second 32%. For 7-year property it is 14.29%, then 24.49%.");
EX(2,"MACRS real property first-year rate (placed in service in January)","Buildings are depreciated straight-line starting from the middle of the month placed in service. A building placed in service in January gets 11.5 months in year one: 3.485% for a residential rental (27.5 years) and 2.461% for a commercial building (39 years).");
EX(2,"Mid-quarter convention first-year rate, 5-year property placed in service in Q4","If more than 40% of the year's equipment is placed in service in the last 3 months, each asset is treated as placed in service in the middle of its quarter. A fourth-quarter asset gets only 1.5 months: 5% in the first year for 5-year property.");
EX(2,"Accrual method: when is income included?","An accrual business reports income when it has earned the right to it and can measure the amount, even if not yet paid. It also cannot report income later for tax than it does on its audited financial statements.");
EX(2,"Gross receipts of an accrual business include","Gross receipts are the income from the ordinary operations of the business: sales, service fees, and interest it charges customers on unpaid accounts. Gain from selling equipment is not part of gross receipts; it is reported separately.");
EX(2,"Bad debts recovered","If you deducted a debt as uncollectible and the customer later pays, the amount recovered is income in the year received, but only up to the deduction that actually reduced your tax.");
EX(2,"Business bad debt deduction","A business can deduct a debt that becomes uncollectible, in whole or in part, as an ordinary loss. The amount must already have been reported as income. So a cash-method business cannot deduct unpaid invoices, since it never reported that income.");
EX(2,"Nonbusiness bad debt","A personal loan that is not repaid, such as a loan to a friend, can be deducted only when it is completely worthless. It is treated as a short-term capital loss, subject to the $3,000 yearly limit.");
EX(2,"1099-NEC: who and threshold (payments in 2025)","A business that pays a non-employee $600 or more in a year for services must report it on Form 1099-NEC. This applies to payments made in 2025; the higher $2,000 threshold is for 2026 payments.");
EX(2,"1099 for payments to corporations","Payments to corporations usually need no Form 1099. Two exceptions are always reported even when the payee is a corporation: fees paid to attorneys and payments for medical or health care services.");
EX(2,"1099-MISC: common boxes","Form 1099-MISC covers payments that are not for services by a contractor: rent of $600 or more, royalties of $10 or more, prizes, medical and health care payments, and the gross amount paid to an attorney in a settlement.");
EX(2,"1099-NEC due date","Form 1099-NEC must be given to the worker and filed with the IRS by January 31. The early date lets the IRS match income before paying refunds.");
EX(2,"Electronic filing threshold for information returns","A business that files 10 or more information returns in a year, counting all types together (W-2, 1099 and others), must file them electronically.");
EX(2,"Backup withholding rate","If a payee does not provide a correct tax ID number, the payer must withhold 24% from payments and send it to the IRS. The payee claims it as tax paid on their return.");
EX(2,"Business meals (2025)","A business meal with a client or while traveling is 50% deductible if it is not extravagant and the owner or an employee is present. Entertainment such as game tickets is not deductible. Food at a party for all staff is 100% deductible.");
EX(2,"Business gifts","Gifts to customers and business contacts are deductible only up to $25 per person per year. Small extra costs such as wrapping and engraving do not count toward the $25. Items under $4 with the company name on them are not treated as gifts.");
EX(2,"2025 business standard mileage rate","In place of actual costs, a business can deduct 70 cents per business mile in 2025, plus parking and tolls. To use it you must choose it in the first year the car is used for business. It cannot be used by a fleet of 5 or more vehicles used at the same time.");
EX(2,"Business travel","Travel is deductible when business takes you away from your tax home long enough to need sleep. Transportation and lodging are fully deductible and meals 50%. A spouse's costs count only if the spouse is an employee with a real business reason to come.");
EX(2,"Home office requirements","A home office is deductible only if a specific area is used regularly and only for business, and it is the main place of business, or a place to meet clients, or a separate structure. Employees cannot claim it, even when working from home.");
EX(2,"Home office simplified method","The simple way: deduct $5 per square foot of the office, up to 300 square feet, so at most $1,500. No depreciation is taken. The regular method uses actual costs times the business percentage on Form 8829.");
EX(2,"Startup and organizational costs","Costs of investigating and starting a business, and of legally forming it, are capital costs. You may deduct up to $5,000 of each kind in the first year. That $5,000 shrinks dollar for dollar once the total passes $50,000. The rest is spread over 180 months.");
EX(2,"2025 Section 179 limits (OBBBA)","Section 179 lets a business deduct the cost of equipment in the year it is placed in service. For 2025 the limit is $2,500,000. It is reduced dollar for dollar when purchases exceed $4,000,000, and it cannot be more than the business's taxable income; the extra carries forward.");
EX(2,"2025 §179 limit for heavy SUVs","Vehicles over 6,000 pounds escape the luxury car depreciation caps, so the law puts a separate limit on Section 179 for heavy SUVs: $31,300 for 2025. Bonus depreciation can still apply to the rest.");
EX(2,"Bonus depreciation in 2025","Bonus depreciation lets a business deduct the cost of qualifying property in the first year with no dollar limit. For property acquired after January 19, 2025 it is 100%, made permanent. Property acquired before that date and placed in service in 2025 gets 40%.");
EX(2,"MACRS 5-year property","Five-year property covers things that wear out or go out of date quickly: cars, light trucks, computers, copiers and research equipment.");
EX(2,"MACRS 7-year property","Seven-year property is the default class: office furniture and fixtures, farm machinery, and any property that has no class life assigned.");
EX(2,"MACRS 15-year property","Fifteen-year property includes improvements to land, such as fences, roads, sidewalks and parking lots, and qualified improvement property inside commercial buildings.");
EX(2,"MACRS real property lives","Buildings are depreciated straight-line: 27.5 years for residential rental and 39 years for commercial property, with a mid-month convention. The land under the building is never depreciated, so the price must be divided between land and building.");
EX(2,"Listed property: business use test","Listed property, mainly vehicles, must be used more than 50% for business to get accelerated depreciation, Section 179 or bonus. If business use later falls to 50% or less, the extra depreciation taken is added back to income.");
EX(2,"Unrecaptured §1250 gain","Buildings are depreciated straight-line, so there is no ordinary recapture for an individual. But the gain that comes from that depreciation is taxed at up to 25%, not the usual 15% or 20%. Gain above the original cost gets the normal capital gain rates.");
EX(2,"Section 291 recapture","C corporations have an extra rule for real property. 20% of the straight-line depreciation taken, or 20% of the gain if less, is treated as ordinary income. With a flat 21% rate it mainly affects capital loss use.");
EX(2,"Section 1231 netting","Section 1231 gives the best of both: add up gains and losses on business property held over a year. A net gain is long-term capital gain; a net loss is an ordinary loss. To prevent abuse, a net gain is ordinary to the extent of net Section 1231 losses in the past 5 years.");
EX(2,"Form 4797","Form 4797 reports sales of property used in a business. It separates ordinary gain from depreciation recapture, Section 1231 gains and losses, and ordinary gains on property held one year or less.");
EX(2,"Like-kind exchange (§1031) rules","You can swap business or investment real estate for other real estate and postpone the gain. You must identify the new property within 45 days and receive it within 180 days. Cash or other property received (boot) is taxed up to the gain. Equipment and vehicles no longer qualify.");
EX(2,"Involuntary conversion (§1033) replacement period","When property is destroyed, stolen or condemned and the payment exceeds basis, the gain can be postponed by buying similar property in time: generally 2 years after the end of the year the gain was realized, 3 years for condemned business real estate, and 4 years for a main home in a federal disaster.");
EX(2,"Installment sale","When payments arrive over more than one year, gain is reported as the money comes in, using the gross profit percentage. Depreciation recapture is the exception: all of it is taxed in the year of sale. Inventory and publicly traded stock cannot use this method.");
EX(2,"Capitalize vs repair","A cost that only keeps property in normal working condition is a repair and is deducted now. A cost that makes the property better, restores it, or adapts it to a new use is an improvement and must be capitalized and depreciated.");
EX(2,"De minimis safe harbor","A business may elect to deduct small purchases right away, not depreciate them: up to $2,500 per item or invoice, or $5,000 if it has audited financial statements. The election is made each year on the return.");
EX(2,"Inventory includes","Inventory is what the business holds to sell: goods bought for resale, raw materials, partly finished goods and finished products. Supplies that do not become part of the product are not inventory.");
EX(2,"Inventory methods","Inventory methods decide which costs are treated as sold. FIFO assumes the oldest goods are sold first; LIFO assumes the newest. LIFO needs an election on Form 970 and cannot be combined with lower of cost or market. Small businesses may treat inventory as supplies.");
EX(2,"Domestic research and experimental costs (2025)","From 2022 through 2024, U.S. research costs had to be spread over 5 years. The new law lets them be deducted immediately again for tax years beginning after 2024. Research done outside the U.S. is still spread over 15 years.");
EX(2,"FICA rates 2025","Payroll tax has two parts, each paid by both the employee and the employer. Social Security is 6.2% each on wages up to $176,100 for 2025. Medicare is 1.45% each on all wages. The employee alone pays an extra 0.9% on wages over $200,000.");
EX(2,"FUTA","Federal unemployment tax is paid only by the employer: 6.0% on the first $7,000 of each worker's wages. Employers who pay state unemployment tax on time get a credit of up to 5.4%, so the usual federal cost is 0.6%, or $42 per employee.");
EX(2,"Employment tax return due dates","Form 941 is filed each quarter, by the last day of the month after the quarter ends: April 30, July 31, October 31 and January 31. Form 940 for unemployment tax is yearly, due January 31. Very small employers may be told to file the yearly Form 944.");
EX(2,"W-2 and W-3 due date","Employers must give Form W-2 to each employee and file the forms with the Social Security Administration, with Form W-3, by January 31.");
EX(2,"Deposit schedule (lookback period)","How often an employer deposits payroll taxes depends on its history. Look at total taxes in the 12 months ending June 30 of the prior year. $50,000 or less: deposit monthly, by the 15th of the next month. More than $50,000: deposit semiweekly.");
EX(2,"$100,000 next-day deposit rule","If an employer builds up $100,000 or more of payroll tax on any single day, it must deposit by the next business day. From then on it is a semiweekly depositor for the rest of that year and the next.");
EX(2,"Small employment tax liability","A very small employer can skip deposits: if total payroll tax for the quarter is under $2,500, it can be paid with Form 941. Unemployment tax must be deposited once the amount owed exceeds $500.");
EX(2,"Worker classification: common-law factors","Whether a worker is an employee depends on who controls the work. Three groups of facts: behavioral control (how the work is done), financial control (who pays expenses and takes the risk), and the relationship (contracts, benefits, permanence). Either side can ask the IRS to decide with Form SS-8.");
EX(2,"Statutory employees","A statutory employee is an independent contractor by normal rules whom the law treats as an employee for payroll tax. They get a Form W-2 with the box 13 statutory employee box checked, report income and expenses on Schedule C, and owe no self-employment tax.");
EX(2,"Statutory nonemployees","Some workers are treated as self-employed by law: direct sellers and licensed real estate agents, when paid by sales and under a contract saying they are not employees. No taxes are withheld and they pay self-employment tax.");
EX(2,"Tax-free fringe benefits","Certain benefits can be given to employees without tax: services the employer sells that cost it nothing extra (an empty airline seat), employee discounts, items needed for the job, small occasional items, commuting benefits up to $325 a month, and the first $50,000 of group life insurance.");
EX(2,"Employee achievement awards","An award of a physical item for long service or safety can be tax-free to the employee and deductible by the employer, up to $400, or $1,600 under a written plan. Cash, gift cards, vacations and meals do not qualify.");
EX(2,"Moving expense reimbursements","If an employer pays or reimburses an employee's moving costs, the amount is taxable wages. The only exception is for active-duty military moving under orders.");
EX(2,"2025 SEP-IRA limit","A SEP is a simple employer-funded plan. The employer can put in up to 25% of pay, with a $70,000 cap for 2025. For a self-employed owner it works out to about 20% of net earnings. It can be opened and funded as late as the extended due date of the return.");
EX(2,"2025 SIMPLE IRA limits","A SIMPLE IRA is for employers with 100 or fewer employees. Employees can defer up to $16,500 in 2025, plus $3,500 if 50 or older. The employer must either match up to 3% of pay or give 2% to everyone. A new plan must be set up by October 1.");
EX(2,"2025 401(k) limits","For 2025 an employee can defer $23,500 into a 401(k), plus $7,500 at age 50 or older, or $11,250 at ages 60 to 63. Employee and employer contributions together cannot exceed $70,000, not counting catch-up amounts.");
EX(2,"2025 defined benefit limit","A defined benefit plan promises a pension amount, not an account balance. For 2025 the yearly pension that can be funded is limited to $280,000.");
EX(2,"2025 highly compensated employee threshold","Retirement plans must not favor the highest paid. For testing, an employee is highly compensated if they earned more than $160,000 in the prior year, or own more than 5% of the business.");
EX(2,"Form 5500-EZ","A retirement plan covering only the owner, or the owner and spouse, files Form 5500-EZ, and only when plan assets are over $250,000 at year end or it is the plan's final year.");
EX(2,"Where a self-employed person deducts own retirement contributions","Contributions for employees are a business expense on Schedule C. But the owner's own retirement contribution is not a business expense: it is deducted as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1, so it does not reduce self-employment tax.");
EX(2,"Direct seller income is reported on","A direct seller, such as someone selling products at home parties, is self-employed. Income and expenses go on Schedule C and net profit is subject to self-employment tax.");
EX(2,"2025 luxury auto first-year depreciation cap","Depreciation on passenger cars is capped each year, however expensive the car. For a car placed in service in 2025 the first-year limit is $20,200 if bonus depreciation is used and $12,200 if not. Heavy vehicles over 6,000 pounds are not subject to these caps.");
EX(2,"Form 4562","Form 4562 reports depreciation and amortization for property placed in service this year, the Section 179 election, and information on vehicles and other listed property.");
EX(2,"Form 8829","A self-employed person uses Form 8829 to figure the home office deduction under the regular method. It splits home costs by the business percentage and limits the deduction to the income of the business.");
EX(2,"Form 3800","Form 3800 combines the various business credits into one general business credit and applies the limit based on tax. It also tracks carrybacks and carryforwards.");
EX(2,"Form 8941","Form 8941 figures the credit for small employers that pay for employee health insurance. The result goes to Form 3800.");
EX(2,"Form 941","Form 941 is the employer's quarterly payroll return. It reports wages paid, income tax withheld, and both halves of Social Security and Medicare tax.");
EX(2,"Form 940","Form 940 is the employer's yearly return for federal unemployment tax. Only the employer pays this tax; nothing is withheld from workers.");
EX(2,"Form 944","Form 944 replaces the four quarterly Forms 941 for the smallest employers, those with yearly payroll tax of $1,000 or less. The IRS must notify the employer to use it.");
EX(2,"Form 943","Form 943 is the yearly payroll return for employers of farm workers, used in place of Form 941.");
EX(2,"Form W-3","Form W-3 is the cover sheet that totals all the Forms W-2 an employer sends to the Social Security Administration.");
EX(2,"Form 1096","Form 1096 is the cover sheet sent with paper Forms 1099 and similar information returns. It is not used when filing electronically.");
EX(2,"Form SS-8","When it is unclear whether a worker is an employee or a contractor, the business or the worker can file Form SS-8 and the IRS will decide.");
EX(2,"Form 8300","A business that receives more than $10,000 in cash in one transaction, or in related transactions, must report it on Form 8300 within 15 days. The rule exists to detect money laundering.");
EX(2,"Form 970","A business files Form 970 with its return for the first year it wants to use LIFO to value inventory.");
EX(2,"Form 8594","When a business is sold, buyer and seller both file Form 8594 showing how the price was divided among 7 classes of assets. Whatever is left after the other classes is goodwill.");
EX(2,"MACRS","MACRS is the system used to depreciate most business property for tax. Each asset is put in a class (5-year, 7-year and so on) with a set method and convention, so the deduction comes from tables, not from estimates of useful life.");
EX(2,"GDS and ADS","The General Depreciation System is the normal one, with shorter lives and faster methods. The Alternative Depreciation System uses straight-line over longer lives. It is required for some property, such as listed property used 50% or less for business, and can be elected.");
EX(2,"UNICAP","The uniform capitalization rules require producers, and resellers above the gross receipts test, to add a share of indirect costs such as storage, purchasing and handling to inventory. Those costs are deducted when the goods are sold, not when paid.");
EX(2,"COGS","Cost of goods sold is the cost of the items actually sold in the year. Formula: beginning inventory, plus purchases and production costs, minus ending inventory. It is subtracted from sales to get gross profit.");
EX(2,"FIFO and LIFO","FIFO treats the first items bought as the first sold, so ending inventory holds the newest costs. LIFO treats the last items bought as the first sold. When prices rise, LIFO gives higher cost of goods sold and lower taxable income.");
EX(2,"QIP","Qualified improvement property is an improvement to the inside of a commercial building made after the building was first placed in service. It is 15-year property and qualifies for bonus depreciation. Enlargements, elevators and structural framework are excluded.");
EX(2,"Listed property","Listed property is property that is easily used for personal purposes too, mainly cars and trucks. It needs detailed records of use and must be used more than 50% for business to get accelerated depreciation, Section 179 or bonus.");
EX(2,"Depreciation recapture","Depreciation lowered ordinary income in past years. When the asset is sold at a gain, depreciation recapture taxes that part of the gain as ordinary income. It is full for equipment (Section 1245) and limited for buildings.");
EX(2,"Amortization","Amortization is the same idea as depreciation, for intangible assets. The cost is deducted in equal amounts over a fixed period: 15 years for purchased goodwill and similar intangibles.");
EX(2,"Bonus depreciation","Bonus depreciation is an extra deduction in the year property is placed in service, taken before regular depreciation. It has no dollar limit and can create a loss. It is 100% for qualifying property acquired after January 19, 2025.");
EX(2,"EFTPS","The Electronic Federal Tax Payment System is the Treasury's free system for paying federal taxes online or by phone. Employers must make payroll tax deposits electronically.");
EX(2,"Accountable plan","Under an accountable plan, employees must show a business purpose, document the expense, and return any excess advance. Reimbursements made under these rules are not wages and are not reported on Form W-2.");
EX(2,"Statutory employee","A statutory employee is one of four kinds of workers, such as certain drivers and full-time life insurance salespeople, treated as employees for Social Security and Medicare. They still deduct their business expenses on Schedule C.");
EX(2,"Ordinary and necessary","A business expense is deductible if it is ordinary, meaning common and accepted in that kind of business, and necessary, meaning helpful and appropriate. It does not have to be indispensable.");
EX(2,"Capital expenditure","A capital expenditure buys or improves something that will last beyond the year, such as equipment or a building. It is not deducted at once: the cost is recovered over time through depreciation, unless Section 179 or bonus depreciation applies.");
EX(2,"Employer deduction limit: profit-sharing and SEP","An employer can deduct contributions to a profit-sharing plan or SEP up to 25% of the total pay of the employees covered by the plan. Example: covered payroll of $400,000 allows up to $100,000.");
EX(2,"Vesting of employer contributions","Vesting is when employer money in a retirement plan becomes the employee's to keep. Two allowed schedules: cliff, where nothing is vested until 3 years and then 100%; or graded, 20% a year from year 2 until 100% at year 6. An employee's own deferrals are always fully theirs.");
EX(2,"Form 5500 due date","Form 5500, the yearly report of a benefit plan, is due by the last day of the 7th month after the plan year ends: July 31 for a calendar-year plan. Form 5558 extends it by 2½ months.");
EX(2,"Top-heavy plan","A plan is top-heavy when key employees, such as owners and officers, hold more than 60% of the benefits. To protect the other workers, the employer must then make a minimum contribution for them, generally 3% of pay.");
EX(2,"Deadline to adopt a new SEP or profit-sharing plan","An employer can create a new SEP or profit-sharing plan for a year as late as the due date of its tax return for that year, including extensions. So a plan for 2025 can be set up in 2026 before filing.");
EX(2,"Prohibited transactions (IRAs and plans)","A retirement account must not be used for the owner's personal benefit now. Borrowing from your IRA, selling property to it, or pledging it for a loan is a prohibited transaction. For an IRA the result is harsh: the entire account is treated as paid out on January 1 of that year.");
EX(2,"Form 1041 filing requirement","An estate files Form 1041 when it has gross income of $600 or more in the year. A trust files with any taxable income, or gross income of $600 or more. Either must file if any beneficiary is a nonresident alien.");
EX(2,"Form 1041 due date","Form 1041 is due on the 15th day of the 4th month after the year ends, April 15 for a calendar year. Form 7004 gives an extension of 5½ months, shorter than the 6 months for most returns.");
EX(2,"Trust and estate tax year","An estate may pick any year-end, starting on the date of death, which can delay tax for the heirs. A trust generally must use the calendar year. A revocable trust can elect to be treated as part of the estate and share its fiscal year.");
EX(2,"1041 exemption amounts","Estates and trusts get a small exemption in place of a standard deduction: $600 for an estate, $300 for a simple trust that must pay out all income, and $100 for a complex trust.");
EX(2,"2025 trust and estate brackets","Trust and estate brackets are very narrow. The top 37% rate begins at just $15,650 of taxable income for 2025, compared with over $600,000 for an individual. That is why income is usually distributed to beneficiaries, who are taxed at their own lower rates.");
EX(2,"Simple trust","A simple trust has three features in a given year: its terms require all income to be paid out currently, it makes no gifts to charity, and it distributes no principal. The beneficiaries are taxed on the income even if it was not actually paid.");
EX(2,"Complex trust","A complex trust is any trust that is not simple that year: it may keep some income, pay out principal, or give to charity. A trust can be simple one year and complex the next.");
EX(2,"Distributable net income (DNI)","Distributable net income is the limit that makes sure trust income is taxed only once. The trust deducts what it distributes, up to DNI, and the beneficiaries are taxed on the same amount. The income keeps its character, so tax-exempt interest stays tax-exempt to the beneficiary.");
EX(2,"65-day rule","A trustee often does not know the year's income until after year end. The 65-day rule allows distributions made in the first 65 days of the new year to be treated as made on the last day of the prior year, moving income from the trust's high brackets to the beneficiaries.");
EX(2,"Grantor trust","If the person who created a trust keeps control over it, such as the power to revoke it or to take the income, the trust is ignored for income tax. All of its income, deductions and credits are reported on the grantor's own return. A revocable living trust is the usual example.");
EX(2,"Final year of an estate or trust","In its last year an estate or trust passes its unused tax benefits to the beneficiaries who receive the property: capital loss carryovers, net operating loss carryovers, and deductions greater than income in the final year.");
EX(2,"Income in respect of a decedent (IRD)","Some income was earned by the person before death but received afterward, like a last paycheck or a traditional IRA. It gets no step-up in basis. Whoever receives it reports it, with the same character it would have had for the decedent.");
EX(2,"IRD deduction (§691(c))","Income in respect of a decedent can be hit by both estate tax and income tax. To ease this, the person who reports the income can deduct the part of the federal estate tax that was caused by it. It is an itemized deduction.");
EX(2,"501(c)(3) purposes","To qualify under section 501(c)(3) an organization must be organized and operated only for listed purposes such as religious, charitable, scientific, literary or educational work, and no profit may benefit private individuals.");
EX(2,"501(c)(3) political activity","A 501(c)(3) organization may never support or oppose a candidate for public office. Lobbying on legislation is allowed only if it is not a substantial part of its activities; an organization can elect a clear spending test for lobbying under section 501(h).");
EX(2,"Form 1023 / 1023-EZ","An organization applies for 501(c)(3) status with Form 1023. Small ones, with expected gross receipts of $50,000 or less and assets of $250,000 or less, may use the shorter Form 1023-EZ. Filing within 27 months of formation makes the exemption go back to the start.");
EX(2,"Form 990 series","Most exempt organizations file a yearly Form 990, and its size determines which one: the electronic postcard 990-N for gross receipts normally $50,000 or less, 990-EZ for mid-size, and the full 990 for larger ones. Private foundations always file 990-PF.");
EX(2,"Form 990 due date","The Form 990 series is due on the 15th day of the 5th month after the organization's year ends: May 15 for a calendar year.");
EX(2,"Automatic revocation of exemption","An exempt organization that does not file its required Form 990, 990-EZ or 990-N for 3 years in a row automatically loses its tax-exempt status. It must apply again to get it back.");
EX(2,"Unrelated business income (UBI)","An exempt organization pays tax on income from a business that it runs regularly and that is not substantially related to its exempt purpose. If gross income from such a business is $1,000 or more, it files Form 990-T. Needing the money for charity does not make the business related.");
EX(2,"UBI exceptions","Some activities are not treated as unrelated business: those run almost entirely by volunteers, selling donated goods (a thrift shop), and activities for the convenience of members, students or employees, like a hospital cafeteria. Passive income such as dividends and interest is also excluded.");
EX(2,"Farm income and expenses","Farmers report on Schedule F. Because farm income varies widely, they may use income averaging on Schedule J to tax part of a good year's farm income at the rates of the 3 earlier years.");
EX(2,"Farm NOL","Most businesses can only carry a net operating loss forward. A loss from farming may be carried back 2 years to get a refund of tax paid in good years, and then forward.");
EX(2,"Crop insurance proceeds","Crop insurance is normally income when received. A cash-method farmer whose practice is to sell the crop in the year after harvest may elect to report the insurance money in that following year, matching when the crop income would have come.");
EX(2,"Sale of livestock due to drought or weather","When drought or other weather forces a farmer to sell more livestock than usual, relief is available. Gain on breeding or dairy animals can be postponed if they are replaced within the allowed period, and income from other extra sales can be moved to the next year.");
EX(2,"Form 990","Form 990 is the yearly information return of a tax-exempt organization. It reports revenue, expenses, programs and governance, and it is open to public inspection.");
EX(2,"Form 990-T","An exempt organization files Form 990-T to report and pay tax on unrelated business income when gross income from it is $1,000 or more.");
EX(2,"Form 990-PF","Every private foundation files Form 990-PF each year, whatever its size. It reports assets, grants and the excise tax on investment income.");
EX(2,"Form 1023","Form 1023 is the application to be recognized as a 501(c)(3) organization. It is filed electronically with the organizing documents and financial information.");
EX(2,"Form 1024","Form 1024 is the application for exemption for organizations under other paragraphs of section 501(c), such as social clubs. Social welfare organizations under 501(c)(4) use Form 1024-A.");
EX(2,"Form 5500","Form 5500 is the yearly report an employer files for a retirement or benefit plan, giving information on its finances, investments and operation.");
EX(2,"Form 5558","Form 5558 asks for a single extension of 2½ months to file Form 5500.");
EX(2,"SEP","A SEP is a retirement plan where the employer puts money straight into an IRA for each eligible employee. Only the employer contributes, the same percentage of pay for everyone, and the plan is simple to set up and run.");
EX(2,"SIMPLE","A SIMPLE plan is for employers with 100 or fewer employees. Employees choose to defer part of their pay, and the employer must contribute each year, either by matching up to 3% or giving 2% to all eligible employees.");
EX(2,"HCE","A highly compensated employee owns more than 5% of the business or earned more than the yearly dollar limit in the prior year. Plans are tested to make sure these employees do not benefit much more than everyone else.");
EX(2,"DNI","Distributable net income is the trust's or estate's income available for distribution, figured under tax rules. It is the ceiling on the entity's deduction for distributions and on the amount beneficiaries must include.");
EX(2,"UBI","Unrelated business income is what an exempt organization earns from a business it runs regularly that does not further its exempt purpose, such as a museum running a public parking garage. It is taxed at corporate rates on Form 990-T.");
EX(2,"Simple trust vs complex trust","A simple trust must pay out all its income every year and does nothing else with the money. A complex trust is any trust that keeps income, pays out principal or gives to charity. The label is decided year by year.");
EX(2,"Grantor trust (meaning)","A grantor trust is treated for income tax as if it did not exist, because the person who created it kept powers or benefits. Its income goes on that person's own return.");
EX(2,"Private foundation","A private foundation is a 501(c)(3) organization funded by one family or company and not broadly supported by the public. It faces stricter rules: an excise tax on investment income, required yearly payouts, and limits on dealings with insiders.");
EX(2,"Vesting","Vesting is the process of earning the right to keep the employer's contributions to your retirement account. Leave before you are fully vested and you lose the unvested part. Your own contributions are always 100% vested.");
EX(3,"Circular 230 §10.29 vs §10.21","Two rules that are easy to mix up. Section 10.29 is about conflicts between clients: you may continue only with each client's informed consent in writing. Section 10.21 is about a client's past mistake: you must tell the client about it and what could happen, but you do not correct it yourself or report it to the IRS.");
EX(3,"Circular 230: what's 'willful, reckless, or gross incompetence'","Circular 230 punishes serious misconduct, not every mistake. A sanction generally requires that the practitioner acted on purpose, recklessly, or with gross incompetence. An honest, ordinary error is not enough under Circular 230, though it can still bring a preparer penalty under the tax code.");
EX(3,"Circular 230","Circular 230 is the set of Treasury rules for people who represent taxpayers before the IRS. It says who may practice, what duties they have, and how they can be disciplined. The Office of Professional Responsibility enforces it.");
EX(3,"Practice before the IRS (§10.2)","Practice before the IRS means acting for a client in dealings with the IRS: preparing and filing documents, writing or speaking to the IRS, giving written tax advice, and representing the client at meetings and hearings. Simply preparing a tax return is not, by itself, practice.");
EX(3,"Who may practice before the IRS (§10.3)","Three groups have unlimited rights: attorneys, CPAs and enrolled agents. They can represent any taxpayer on any matter at any IRS office. Enrolled actuaries and enrolled retirement plan agents are limited to their fields. Unenrolled preparers with the filing season record have only limited rights.");
EX(3,"Unenrolled preparer representation rights","An unenrolled preparer can represent a client only if they hold an Annual Filing Season Program record, and only for a return they prepared and signed, and only during an examination or with customer service or the Taxpayer Advocate. They cannot handle appeals or collection matters.");
EX(3,"Limited practice without enrollment (§10.7)","Some people can deal with the IRS without being a practitioner because of their special relationship: yourself, an immediate family member, an employer represented by an officer or full-time employee, a partnership by a general partner, and an estate or trust by its fiduciary.");
EX(3,"How to become an EA","To become an enrolled agent by exam: get a PTIN, pass all 3 parts of the Special Enrollment Examination within 3 years, apply on Form 23 within 1 year of passing the last part, and pass a check of your own tax compliance and background. Former IRS employees can qualify through experience.");
EX(3,"SEE pass score validity","A passing score on one part does not last forever. Each part stays valid for 3 years from the date you passed it. If you do not pass the other parts within that time, the oldest pass expires and that part must be retaken.");
EX(3,"SEE attempts per window","The exam is normally offered from May 1 through the end of February and is closed in March and April for updating. The 2026–27 window started late, on July 1, 2026, and ends February 28, 2027. Within one testing window you can take each part up to 4 times.");
EX(3,"EA continuing education","Enrolled agents must complete 72 hours of continuing education in each 3-year cycle. At least 16 hours must be done each year, and 2 of those hours must be ethics every year. The courses must come from IRS-approved providers.");
EX(3,"EA renewal","Enrollment is renewed every 3 years on Form 8554. Which year you renew depends on the last digit of your Social Security number. Separately, the PTIN must be renewed every year.");
EX(3,"New EA's first enrollment cycle CE","Someone who becomes an enrolled agent in the middle of a 3-year cycle does not need the full 72 hours. The requirement is 2 hours for each month enrolled in that cycle, including 2 hours of ethics each year.");
EX(3,"CE records retention","Keep proof of continuing education, such as certificates and course details, for 4 years after the date of the renewal for which the hours were used.");
EX(3,"Failure to renew or meet CE","An enrolled agent who does not renew, or does not finish the required education, is moved to inactive status and may not practice or use the title. If the problem is not fixed, enrollment is terminated.");
EX(3,"§10.20 Information to be furnished","When the IRS makes a proper request for records or information, the practitioner must provide them promptly, unless there is a good-faith belief that they are privileged. If the practitioner does not have them, they must tell the IRS who they believe does. They need not question the client beyond a reasonable inquiry.");
EX(3,"§10.22 Diligence as to accuracy","A practitioner must use due diligence: prepare returns and documents carefully and make sure what is told to the IRS and to clients is correct. Relying on another person's work is acceptable if reasonable care was used in hiring, supervising and reviewing that person.");
EX(3,"§10.23 Prompt disposition","A practitioner may not drag out a case. Unreasonably delaying a matter before the IRS, for example by ignoring requests or missing appointments on purpose, violates Circular 230.");
EX(3,"§10.24 Assistance from disbarred persons","A practitioner may not knowingly work with someone who has been disbarred or suspended from practice on IRS matters. They also may not accept help from a former government employee where that would break the rules on former employees.");
EX(3,"§10.26 Notaries","A practitioner who is also a notary may not act as notary on a document in a matter where they represent the client or have a personal interest.");
EX(3,"§10.27 Fees: general","A practitioner may not charge a fee that is unconscionable, meaning grossly excessive. Fees that depend on the result are generally banned, with three exceptions connected to IRS examinations, certain refund claims, and court proceedings.");
EX(3,"§10.28 Return of client records","Records the client needs to meet their tax obligations must be returned promptly when asked, even if the client has not paid. The practitioner may keep copies. Work the practitioner prepared may be held back for unpaid fees only if state law allows, and even then the client must be given access to review and copy it.");
EX(3,"§10.29 Conflicts of interest","A conflict exists when helping one client would hurt another, or when the practitioner's own interests get in the way. Representation may continue only if the practitioner reasonably believes they can serve each client well, the law permits it, and each client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing within 30 days. The consents are kept 36 months.");
EX(3,"§10.30 Advertising: phrases an EA may use","An enrolled agent may describe the credential with phrases such as enrolled to represent taxpayers before the Internal Revenue Service, or enrolled to practice before the IRS. The descriptions must be accurate and not suggest something more.");
EX(3,"§10.30 Advertising: prohibited","Advertising must be truthful. An enrolled agent may not use the word certified, may not suggest they work for or are endorsed by the IRS, and may not use the IRS logo. Uninvited contact is not allowed if it is false, misleading or involves pressure.");
EX(3,"§10.30 Fee information","A practitioner may advertise fees: fixed fees for routine services, hourly rates, a range of fees, or the fee for a first meeting. Whatever was published must be honored for at least 30 days after the last time it appeared.");
EX(3,"Advertising records retention","A copy of every advertisement or solicitation, or for direct mail a list of who received it, must be kept for 36 months from its last use. The IRS can ask to see it.");
EX(3,"§10.31 Negotiation of taxpayer refund checks","A practitioner must never cash, endorse or deposit a client's federal tax refund check, and may not have a refund sent to an account they own or control. Taking the fee out of a refund this way is prohibited.");
EX(3,"§10.32 Practice of law","Being an enrolled agent allows practice before the IRS only. It does not allow giving general legal services such as drafting wills or representing clients in court, which belong to attorneys.");
EX(3,"§10.33 Best practices","Best practices are goals, not rules that are punished. They include being clear with the client about the work, getting the facts right, explaining the consequences of the advice, and dealing fairly with the IRS. People who run a firm should set up procedures that follow them.");
EX(3,"§10.34 Standards for returns","A practitioner may not sign a return, or advise a position, that lacks a reasonable basis or is an unreasonable position, when acting willfully, recklessly or with gross incompetence. The practitioner must also tell the client about penalties likely to apply and how disclosure can avoid them.");
EX(3,"§10.34(d) Relying on client information","A practitioner may generally accept what the client says without checking documents. But they cannot ignore what the information suggests. If something looks wrong, inconsistent or incomplete, they must ask reasonable questions.");
EX(3,"§10.35 Competence","A practitioner must have the knowledge and skill the matter needs. If they do not, they can become competent by studying the law or by consulting someone who knows the area.");
EX(3,"§10.36 Procedures","The people in charge of a firm's tax practice must take reasonable steps so that everyone in the firm follows Circular 230. If they fail to set up or follow proper procedures and violations happen, they themselves can be sanctioned.");
EX(3,"§10.37 Written advice","Written tax advice must rest on reasonable facts and assumptions and consider everything relevant. The practitioner may not rely on statements they know or should know are wrong, and may not base the advice on the chance that the return will not be audited.");
EX(3,"§10.50 Sanctions","There are four sanctions. Censure is a public reprimand. Suspension removes the right to practice for a set time. Disbarment removes it, and reinstatement can be requested only after 5 years. A money penalty can be added, up to the gross income from the misconduct.");
EX(3,"§10.51 Incompetence and disreputable conduct (examples)","Disreputable conduct includes being convicted of a tax crime or a crime of dishonesty, giving false information to the IRS, willfully not filing your own tax returns, misusing client funds, and improper solicitation. A practitioner's own tax compliance is part of fitness to practice.");
EX(3,"§10.52 Violations subject to sanction","A practitioner can be sanctioned for violating the Circular 230 duties willfully, recklessly or through gross incompetence. Recklessness means an extreme departure from ordinary care.");
EX(3,"Circular 230 disciplinary proceedings","A discipline case starts when the Office of Professional Responsibility files a complaint. The practitioner has 30 days to answer. An Administrative Law Judge holds a hearing and decides. Either side can appeal within 30 days to the Treasury Appellate Authority.");
EX(3,"§10.82 Expedited suspension","Some cases need no full hearing first. A practitioner who lost a professional license for cause, was convicted of certain crimes, or was sanctioned by a court in a tax matter can be suspended quickly, and stays suspended until they show the reason no longer applies.");
EX(3,"Preparer penalty §6694(a): unreasonable position","A preparer is penalized when a return understates tax because of a position that was unreasonable. The penalty is the greater of $1,000 or 50% of the fee earned for that return. It is avoided if the position had substantial authority, or had a reasonable basis and was disclosed.");
EX(3,"Preparer penalty §6694(b): willful or reckless","If the understatement comes from a willful attempt to understate tax or reckless disregard of the rules, the penalty is heavier: the greater of $5,000 or 75% of the fee earned. Any penalty for the same return under the lighter rule is subtracted.");
EX(3,"§6695 preparer penalties (per failure, inflation-adjusted)","These are penalties for breaking the routine duties of a paid preparer: not giving the client a copy, not signing the return, not putting the PTIN on it, not keeping a copy or a list of clients. Each failure has a set dollar penalty. Cashing a client's refund check is penalized here too.");
EX(3,"Preparer due diligence (§6695(g)) requirements","For certain credits and Head of Household status, a paid preparer must do four things: ask enough questions to know the client qualifies, complete Form 8867, compute the credit on worksheets, and keep the records for 3 years. A penalty applies for each failure on each return.");
EX(3,"Form 8867 records to keep","To prove due diligence the preparer keeps Form 8867, the worksheets used, copies of any client documents relied on, and notes of who gave the information, when, and what questions were asked and answered. These are kept for 3 years.");
EX(3,"§6107 Preparer record retention","A paid preparer must keep, for 3 years after the end of the return period, either a copy of every return prepared or a list with the taxpayers' names and ID numbers. The return period runs from July 1 to June 30.");
EX(3,"§7216 Disclosure or use of tax return information","A preparer may not disclose or use a client's tax return information for anything other than preparing the return, unless the client consents or an exception applies. Doing so knowingly is a crime, and there is also a civil penalty of $250 for each improper disclosure or use.");
EX(3,"§7216 consents","A client's consent to use or disclose return information must be in its own document, signed and dated before the use or disclosure, and must say exactly what is allowed. It lasts 1 year unless it states another period.");
EX(3,"§7525 Federally authorized tax practitioner privilege","Communications with an enrolled agent about tax advice get a confidentiality protection like the attorney-client privilege. It is narrow: only noncriminal tax matters before the IRS or in federal court, and it does not cover preparing a return or promoting tax shelters.");
EX(3,"§6700 / §6701","Two penalties aimed at people who help others cheat. Section 6700 punishes promoting abusive tax shelters. Section 6701 punishes knowingly helping to prepare a document that understates someone's tax: $1,000 per document, or $10,000 if it concerns a corporation's tax.");
EX(3,"§7407 / §7408","Besides money penalties, the government can ask a federal court for an order that stops a person from acting: section 7407 against a return preparer who keeps breaking the rules, and section 7408 against promoters of abusive tax schemes.");
EX(3,"Criminal: §7201 tax evasion","Tax evasion is the most serious tax crime: a willful attempt to evade or defeat a tax, with some affirmative act such as hiding income. It is a felony with up to 5 years in prison and a fine up to $100,000 for an individual.");
EX(3,"Criminal: §7203 willful failure to file or pay","Willfully not filing a return, not paying tax, or not keeping required records is a misdemeanor: up to 1 year in prison and a fine up to $25,000 for each year.");
EX(3,"Criminal: §7206 fraud and false statements","Signing a return or document that you know is false in an important way, under penalties of perjury, is a felony with up to 3 years in prison. It also covers a preparer who helps prepare a false return.");
EX(3,"Substantial authority sources","To decide whether a position has substantial authority, only certain sources count: the tax code, regulations, court decisions, IRS revenue rulings and procedures, treaties, committee reports and similar official material. Textbooks, articles and opinions of tax professionals do not count.");
EX(3,"Reporting standards (positions)","How strong a position is decides what must be done. With substantial authority, no disclosure is needed. With only a reasonable basis, the position must be disclosed on Form 8275 to avoid penalties. For tax shelters the position must be more likely than not correct.");
EX(3,"Accuracy-related penalty (§6662)","The accuracy-related penalty is 20% of the tax underpaid because of negligence, a substantial understatement, or a serious valuation error. It doubles to 40% for a gross valuation misstatement or an undisclosed foreign financial asset.");
EX(3,"Substantial understatement thresholds","An understatement is substantial for an individual when it is more than the greater of 10% of the correct tax or $5,000. Corporations have their own test. Example: correct tax $30,000, reported $26,000: the $4,000 understatement is below $5,000, so not substantial.");
EX(3,"Civil fraud penalty (§6663)","When part of an underpayment is due to fraud, the penalty is 75% of that part. The IRS has the burden of proving fraud by clear and convincing evidence, a higher standard than in ordinary civil cases. There is no time limit for assessing tax on a fraudulent return.");
EX(3,"Frivolous return penalty (§6702)","Filing a return that is obviously wrong on its face and based on a frivolous argument, such as wages are not income, brings a $5,000 penalty. The same applies to frivolous requests for hearings, offers or payment plans, though the IRS first gives 30 days to withdraw them.");
EX(3,"Erroneous refund claim penalty (§6676)","Claiming a refund or credit larger than allowed, without reasonable cause, costs 20% of the excessive amount. It discourages inflated refund claims even when no tax is underpaid.");
EX(3,"Office of Professional Responsibility (OPR)","The Office of Professional Responsibility is the part of the IRS that interprets and enforces Circular 230. It investigates practitioner misconduct and brings disciplinary cases.");
EX(3,"Return Preparer Office (RPO)","The Return Preparer Office handles registration matters: issuing PTINs, enrolling agents, overseeing the enrolled agent exam and continuing education providers, and running the Annual Filing Season Program.");
EX(3,"Annual Filing Season Program (AFSP)","The Annual Filing Season Program is a voluntary program for preparers who are not attorneys, CPAs or enrolled agents. They complete 18 hours of education each year, including a refresher course with a test, and receive a Record of Completion and limited representation rights.");
EX(3,"Form 23","Form 23 is the application to become an enrolled agent, filed after passing the exam. A fee is paid and the IRS checks the applicant's tax compliance and background.");
EX(3,"Form 8554","Enrolled agents file Form 8554 every 3 years to renew their enrollment and report that they completed their continuing education.");
EX(3,"Form W-12","Form W-12 is used to apply for or renew a PTIN, though most people do it online. Every paid preparer needs a current PTIN.");
EX(3,"Form 8867","Form 8867 is the due diligence checklist a paid preparer must complete and submit with any return claiming the earned income credit, the child tax credit group, the American Opportunity credit, or Head of Household status.");
EX(3,"Form 8275","Form 8275 tells the IRS about a position on a return that might be questioned. Disclosing a position that has a reasonable basis protects the taxpayer from the accuracy penalty and the preparer from the preparer penalty.");
EX(3,"Form 8275-R","Form 8275-R is the disclosure used when a return position goes against a Treasury regulation. The position must be taken in good faith and have a reasonable basis.");
EX(3,"Form 14157","A taxpayer or anyone else can use Form 14157 to report a tax return preparer to the IRS for misconduct, such as filing a return without consent or changing it to inflate a refund.");
EX(3,"OPR","The Office of Professional Responsibility enforces Circular 230. It has authority over practitioner conduct and can seek censure, suspension, disbarment or money penalties.");
EX(3,"RPO","The Return Preparer Office manages who can prepare returns and practice: PTINs, enrolled agent enrollment and renewal, the Special Enrollment Examination, and the Annual Filing Season Program.");
EX(3,"SEE","The Special Enrollment Examination is the test to become an enrolled agent. It has 3 parts: Individuals, Businesses, and Representation, Practices and Procedures. Each part has 100 multiple-choice questions.");
EX(3,"AFSP","The Annual Filing Season Program gives unenrolled preparers a yearly Record of Completion after 18 hours of education. Holders are listed in the IRS directory of preparers and get limited rights to represent clients whose returns they prepared.");
EX(3,"ALJ","An Administrative Law Judge is the independent official who conducts the hearing in a Circular 230 discipline case and issues the decision, which can be appealed to the Treasury Appellate Authority.");
EX(3,"Censure","Censure is the mildest sanction: a public reprimand. The practitioner may continue to practice, but the censure is published, and conditions may be attached for a period.");
EX(3,"Suspension","A suspended practitioner cannot practice before the IRS for the period of the suspension. They may still prepare returns, but may not represent clients or call themselves eligible to practice.");
EX(3,"Disbarment","Disbarment takes away the right to practice before the IRS. A disbarred practitioner can ask for reinstatement only after 5 years, and the IRS decides whether to allow it.");
EX(3,"Practitioner","In Circular 230, practitioner means someone authorized to practice before the IRS: an attorney, CPA, enrolled agent, enrolled actuary or enrolled retirement plan agent. The duties and sanctions of Circular 230 apply to them.");
EX(3,"Due diligence","Due diligence means doing the work with reasonable care: asking the right questions, not ignoring warning signs, applying the law correctly, and checking the work before it goes to the client or the IRS.");
EX(3,"Substantial authority","Substantial authority means the support for a position is strong when weighed against the authorities that go the other way. It is less than more likely than not but more than a reasonable basis. With it, no disclosure is needed to avoid the penalty.");
EX(3,"Reasonable basis","Reasonable basis is a fairly low standard, but higher than merely arguable or not frivolous. The position must be based on at least one real authority. It protects against penalties only if the position is disclosed on the return.");
EX(3,"More likely than not","More likely than not means better than even odds that the position would be upheld if challenged. It is the level required for tax shelter positions and reportable transactions.");
EX(3,"Contingent fee","A contingent fee depends on the outcome, for example 30% of the refund obtained. Circular 230 bans it for preparing an original return, because it encourages aggressive positions, and allows it only in a few situations involving IRS challenges and court cases.");
EX(3,"Conflict of interest","A conflict of interest exists when representing one client would directly work against another client, or when there is a real risk the representation will be limited by duties to someone else or by the practitioner's own interests. A common example is representing both spouses in a dispute.");
EX(3,"Frivolous position","A frivolous position is one that is plainly wrong, with no serious support in the law. Taking one can lead to penalties for the taxpayer and sanctions for the practitioner.");
EX(3,"Appeals and new issues","Appeals is there to settle disputes, not to audit again. It normally will not bring up new issues or reopen points the taxpayer already agreed with the examiner. It looks at the hazards of litigation: how likely each side is to win in court, and settles accordingly.");
EX(3,"Docketed vs non-docketed Appeals cases","A case is non-docketed when the taxpayer goes to Appeals after the 30-day letter, before any court filing. It is docketed when the taxpayer has already filed a petition in Tax Court; the court case is then usually sent to Appeals to try to settle before trial.");
EX(3,"Form 2848","Form 2848 gives a named person the power to act for the taxpayer before the IRS: get confidential information, argue the case, and sign agreements and consents. It does not by default allow signing the return or cashing a refund check.");
EX(3,"Form 2848: who can be named","Only individual people who are eligible to practice can be named on Form 2848, never a firm. The form must be specific: the type of tax, the form number, and the years or periods covered. A form that says all years is not accepted.");
EX(3,"Form 2848 / 8821: future periods","An authorization can cover any past period, but future periods only up to 3 years after December 31 of the year the IRS receives the form. Example: received in 2026: it can cover periods through 2029.");
EX(3,"Form 8821","Form 8821 lets a person or a company see and receive the taxpayer's confidential tax information. That is all: the person cannot speak for the taxpayer, argue a position or sign anything. It is often used by lenders and by preparers who only need transcripts.");
EX(3,"CAF","The Centralized Authorization File is the IRS computer record of who is authorized for which taxpayer. Each representative is given a CAF number to put on every Form 2848 or 8821 so the IRS can find the authorization.");
EX(3,"Revoking a Form 2848","To cancel a power of attorney, the taxpayer writes REVOKE across the top of a copy of the form, signs and dates it, and sends it to the IRS. Filing a new Form 2848 for the same matter and period also cancels the old one automatically, unless the box to keep the earlier representative is checked.");
EX(3,"Signing a return under a power of attorney","A return must normally be signed by the taxpayer. A representative can sign it only in narrow cases: the taxpayer has a disease or injury, has been outside the United States for at least 60 days before the due date, or has other good cause approved by the IRS. The power of attorney must be attached.");
EX(3,"Joint return and Form 2848","A power of attorney is personal to each taxpayer. Even when spouses file a joint return, each must sign their own Form 2848 to appoint a representative.");
EX(3,"Form 56","A fiduciary, such as an executor, trustee or guardian, is not a representative; the fiduciary takes the place of the taxpayer. Form 56 tells the IRS about this relationship, and no Form 2848 is needed for the fiduciary to act.");
EX(3,"Third-party designee (check box on return)","By checking a box on the return, the taxpayer lets one named person, often the preparer, talk to the IRS about how that return is processed: missing information, refund status, math errors. It ends one year after the return's original due date and does not allow representation in an audit.");
EX(3,"Enrolled agent representation rights","An enrolled agent can represent any taxpayer, on any tax matter, before every part of the IRS, including examination, collection and Appeals. The IRS is not a court, though: to appear in Tax Court a non-attorney must pass that court's own exam.");
EX(3,"Bypassing a representative","The IRS must normally work through the representative. If the representative is causing unreasonable delay or blocking the examination or collection, the employee can get a supervisor's approval to contact the taxpayer directly.");
EX(3,"Taxpayer interview rights","A taxpayer does not have to attend an IRS interview personally if a representative with a power of attorney goes, unless the IRS issues a summons. If the taxpayer says during an interview that they want to consult a representative, the interview must stop.");
EX(3,"Types of examinations","There are three kinds of audit. A correspondence audit is done by mail on a few simple items. An office audit takes place at an IRS office. A field audit is the most thorough, with a revenue agent visiting the taxpayer's home or business.");
EX(3,"Automated Underreporter notice","The IRS computer compares what is on a return with the Forms W-2 and 1099 it received. If income seems to be missing, it sends notice CP2000 proposing more tax. This is a matching notice, not an examination, and the taxpayer may agree or explain.");
EX(3,"30-day letter","After an examination the IRS sends a report of its proposed changes with a letter giving 30 days to respond. Within those 30 days the taxpayer can agree, or ask for a conference with the independent Appeals office.");
EX(3,"Notice of deficiency","If the taxpayer does not agree and the case is not settled, the IRS issues a formal notice of deficiency. From its date the taxpayer has 90 days (150 if abroad) to file a petition in Tax Court without paying first. The IRS cannot assess the tax during that time. The deadline cannot be extended.");
EX(3,"Appeals protest requirement","To go to Appeals, a formal written protest is required when the total disputed amount for any one tax period is more than $25,000. For $25,000 or less, a brief written statement or the small case request form is enough.");
EX(3,"Unsettled non-docketed case in Appeals","If Appeals and the taxpayer cannot agree in a case that has not been filed in court, Appeals issues the notice of deficiency. The taxpayer then has 90 days to petition the Tax Court.");
EX(3,"Tax Court small case (S case)","For disputes of $50,000 or less per year, including penalties, the taxpayer can choose the Tax Court's simplified small case procedure. It is quicker and less formal, but the decision is final: neither side can appeal, and it does not set precedent.");
EX(3,"Refund suits","Other courts hear tax cases only as refund suits. The taxpayer must first pay the full tax, then file a refund claim, and can sue after the IRS denies it or 6 months pass. A U.S. District Court offers a jury; the Court of Federal Claims does not.");
EX(3,"Assessment statute of limitations","The IRS generally has 3 years to assess more tax, counted from the due date or the date the return was filed, whichever is later. It has 6 years if more than 25% of gross income was left off. There is no limit when no return was filed or the return was fraudulent.");
EX(3,"Early-filed return and the statute","Filing early does not start the clock early. A return filed before the due date is treated as filed on the due date, both for the IRS's time to assess and for the taxpayer's time to claim a refund.");
EX(3,"Form 872 vs 872-A","When an audit cannot finish in time, the IRS asks the taxpayer to agree to more time. Form 872 extends the period to a specific date. Form 872-A leaves it open until either side ends it with Form 872-T. The taxpayer can refuse or limit the extension to certain issues.");
EX(3,"Refund statute (§6511)","A refund claim must be filed within 3 years from when the return was filed or 2 years from when the tax was paid, whichever is later. The amount that can be refunded is also limited: under the 3-year rule, to tax paid in the 3 years plus any extension before the claim.");
EX(3,"Claim for refund forms","The form depends on what is being claimed. Individuals amend with Form 1040-X and corporations with Form 1120-X. Form 843 is for asking the IRS to remove or refund penalties, interest and certain other taxes, not income tax.");
EX(3,"Audit reconsideration","Audit reconsideration lets a taxpayer ask the IRS to look again at an audit result that is already assessed, typically because they never responded or now have documents they did not have before. The tax must still be unpaid, and it is not a formal appeal right.");
EX(3,"Burden of proof shift (§7491)","Normally the taxpayer must prove the return is right. In court the burden moves to the IRS on a factual issue if the taxpayer presents credible evidence, kept the required records, backed up the items, and cooperated with reasonable IRS requests.");
EX(3,"First-time abatement (FTA)","First-time abatement removes a late filing, late payment or late deposit penalty without needing a reason, as a reward for a clean record. The taxpayer must have had no significant penalties in the prior 3 years, have filed all required returns, and have paid or arranged to pay the tax.");
EX(3,"Reasonable cause","A penalty can be removed if the taxpayer acted with ordinary care but still could not comply, for example because of a death or serious illness in the family, a fire or disaster, or being unable to get records. Lack of money or not knowing the law is normally not enough.");
EX(3,"Methods of contesting a penalty","A penalty can be challenged at three points. Before it is assessed, by answering the notice or going to Appeals. After it is assessed, by asking for abatement by letter or Form 843. After it is paid, by filing a claim for refund, and suing if the claim is denied.");
EX(3,"Interest abatement","Interest is a charge for the use of money, so it is almost never removed. The exception is interest caused by an unreasonable error or delay by an IRS employee in a routine procedural or managerial act, such as losing a file. Reasonable cause does not remove interest.");
EX(3,"Overpayment interest","The IRS pays interest on refunds, but has a grace period: no interest if the refund is issued within 45 days after the return's due date, or after the filing date if the return was filed late.");
EX(3,"Taxpayer Advocate Service (TAS)","The Taxpayer Advocate Service is an independent office inside the IRS. It helps when a taxpayer faces real hardship or cannot get a problem fixed through normal channels. Help is requested on Form 911, and the Advocate can issue an order requiring the IRS to act or stop.");
EX(3,"Taxpayer Bill of Rights","The Taxpayer Bill of Rights gathers 10 rights found throughout the tax code, such as the right to be informed, to quality service, to pay no more than the correct tax, to challenge the IRS and be heard, to appeal, to privacy and confidentiality, and to keep representation.");
EX(3,"Third-party contacts","Before the IRS contacts other people, such as banks, employers or neighbors, about a taxpayer's liability, it must notify the taxpayer in advance. The notice must be sent at least 45 days before the contact and covers a period of up to one year.");
EX(3,"IRS summons","A summons is an IRS order to appear, give testimony or produce records. When it is served on a third party, like a bank, the taxpayer gets notice and has 20 days to ask a court to cancel it.");
EX(3,"Fast Track Settlement / Mediation","These programs settle disagreements faster. An Appeals officer acts as a neutral mediator while the case stays with the examiner or collection officer. The goal is resolution within about 60 days for small business cases. Taking part is voluntary and no appeal rights are lost.");
EX(3,"Form 911","Form 911 asks the Taxpayer Advocate Service for help when IRS action or inaction is causing financial difficulty or a problem has not been solved through normal channels.");
EX(3,"Form 843","Form 843 is used to claim a refund or ask for abatement of penalties, interest or certain taxes other than income tax. It is the usual form for a penalty abatement request.");
EX(3,"Form 872","By signing Form 872 the taxpayer agrees to give the IRS more time to assess tax, up to a specific date written on the form.");
EX(3,"Form 872-A","Form 872-A extends the time to assess tax with no fixed end date. It stays open until 90 days after either the taxpayer or the IRS ends it with Form 872-T.");
EX(3,"Form 870","By signing Form 870 the taxpayer agrees with the examination changes and lets the IRS assess the tax right away, without a notice of deficiency. This stops more interest from building up. The taxpayer can still pay and file a refund claim later.");
EX(3,"Form 4549","Form 4549 is the examiner's report. It lists each adjustment to income, deductions and credits, and the resulting tax, penalties and interest.");
EX(3,"Form 12203","Form 12203 is the short way to ask for an Appeals conference when the disputed amount is $25,000 or less for each period. It lists the items the taxpayer disagrees with and why.");
EX(3,"Form 4506-T","Form 4506-T requests a free transcript, a computer summary of a return or account. Most people who need proof of income or filing use this.");
EX(3,"Form 4506","Form 4506 requests an actual photocopy of a filed return with its attachments. There is a fee and it takes longer than a transcript.");
EX(3,"POA","A power of attorney is the taxpayer's written authorization, on Form 2848, for an eligible person to represent them before the IRS and act in their place.");
EX(3,"TIA","A tax information authorization, Form 8821, lets the named person or business receive and look at the taxpayer's tax information. It gives no right to represent the taxpayer.");
EX(3,"TAS","The Taxpayer Advocate Service helps taxpayers who are suffering hardship or whose problems the IRS has not resolved. It is independent of the IRS offices it deals with and its help is free.");
EX(3,"TBOR","The Taxpayer Bill of Rights is the list of 10 fundamental rights taxpayers have when dealing with the IRS. IRS employees are required to act in keeping with them.");
EX(3,"SNOD","The statutory notice of deficiency is the IRS's formal claim that more tax is owed. It is the ticket to Tax Court: the taxpayer has 90 days to petition, and the IRS cannot assess or collect that tax during this time.");
EX(3,"AUR","The Automated Underreporter program is the IRS's computer matching of returns against Forms W-2 and 1099. A mismatch produces a CP2000 notice proposing additional tax.");
EX(3,"DIF","The Discriminant Function is a computer score given to each return showing how likely an audit is to find more tax. Returns with high scores are reviewed by people and may be chosen for examination.");
EX(3,"FTA","First-time abatement is an administrative waiver of certain penalties for a taxpayer with a clean record for the prior 3 years. No reasonable cause has to be shown.");
EX(3,"ASED","The assessment statute expiration date is the last day the IRS can legally assess additional tax for a year, normally 3 years after the return was filed or due.");
EX(3,"RSED","The refund statute expiration date is the last day a taxpayer can claim a refund: generally 3 years from filing or 2 years from payment, whichever is later.");
EX(3,"IRM","The Internal Revenue Manual is the IRS's handbook of instructions for its own employees. It is useful for understanding IRS procedures, but it is not law and is not authority for a tax position.");
EX(3,"Summons","A summons is the IRS's legal tool to require a person to appear, testify or hand over books and records. If ignored, the IRS can ask a federal court to enforce it.");
EX(3,"Protest","A protest is the taxpayer's written statement to Appeals. It lists the adjustments being disputed, the facts, and the law the taxpayer relies on. It is required when the disputed amount is over $25,000 for a period.");
EX(3,"Privilege","The practitioner privilege keeps tax advice between a taxpayer and a federally authorized tax practitioner confidential, in the same way as with an attorney, but only in noncriminal tax matters before the IRS or in federal court.");
EX(3,"Lien vs levy","A lien and a levy are different steps. A lien is the government's legal claim on your property to secure the debt; you still have the property. A levy is the actual taking of property, such as money in a bank account or part of your wages.");
EX(3,"Installment agreement vs Offer in Compromise","Two ways to resolve a tax debt. An installment agreement pays the full amount, plus interest and penalties, over time. An offer in compromise settles the debt for less than the full amount, when the IRS doubts it can collect it all or doubts it is owed.");
EX(3,"Trust fund recovery penalty: who is 'responsible'","The penalty can be charged to any person who had the duty and the power to collect and pay the withheld taxes and willfully did not. Title does not decide it: what matters is who could decide which bills were paid. Willful includes paying other creditors while knowing the taxes were unpaid.");
EX(3,"Trust fund taxes","Trust fund taxes are amounts an employer takes from employees' pay and holds for the government: withheld income tax and the employees' share of Social Security and Medicare. The employer's own share and unemployment tax are not trust fund taxes.");
EX(3,"Wage levy","A levy on wages keeps going every payday until the debt is paid or the levy is released. Part of each paycheck is protected, based on the standard deduction and the number of dependents the employee reports on the statement that comes with the levy.");
EX(3,"Bank levy holding period","A bank levy captures only what is in the account when it arrives. The bank freezes that money for 21 days before sending it to the IRS, which gives the taxpayer time to fix mistakes or make other arrangements.");
EX(3,"Levy release","The IRS must release a levy when the tax is paid, when the collection period has ended, when the levy creates an economic hardship (the taxpayer cannot meet basic living costs), or when an installment agreement is made that does not allow the levy to continue.");
EX(3,"Collection while an OIC or IA request is pending","While an offer in compromise or an installment agreement request is being considered, the IRS generally may not levy. The protection continues for 30 days after a rejection and during any appeal of the rejection.");
EX(3,"Collection statute expiration date (CSED)","The IRS has 10 years from the date the tax was assessed to collect it. After that the debt can no longer be collected. The clock stops while certain things are pending: an offer in compromise, an installment agreement request, a collection due process hearing, bankruptcy, or the taxpayer living abroad for 6 months or more.");
EX(3,"Federal tax lien: when it arises","A federal tax lien comes into existence by law, with no filing, once three things have happened: the IRS assessed the tax, it sent a bill demanding payment, and the taxpayer did not pay. It attaches to everything the taxpayer owns or later acquires.");
EX(3,"Notice of Federal Tax Lien (NFTL)","The Notice of Federal Tax Lien is the public filing that tells other creditors the government has a claim. The IRS must tell the taxpayer within 5 business days after filing, and the taxpayer then has 30 days to request a collection due process hearing.");
EX(3,"Lien release","The IRS must release a lien within 30 days after the debt is paid in full, becomes legally uncollectible, or the IRS accepts a bond guaranteeing payment.");
EX(3,"Lien relief options","Three ways to get relief from a lien without full payment. Withdrawal removes the public notice. Discharge removes the lien from one specific property so it can be sold. Subordination lets another creditor move ahead of the IRS, for example to allow refinancing.");
EX(3,"Levy","A levy is the legal seizure of property to pay a tax debt. Before it, the IRS must send a bill, and then a final notice of intent to levy that explains the right to a hearing, at least 30 days before the levy.");
EX(3,"Final Notice of Intent to Levy","The final notice of intent to levy is the last warning before the IRS takes property. From its date the taxpayer has 30 days to request a collection due process hearing with Form 12153, which stops the levy while the hearing is pending.");
EX(3,"Equivalent hearing","If the 30-day deadline for a collection due process hearing is missed, the taxpayer can still ask for an equivalent hearing within one year. Appeals holds a similar hearing, but collection is not automatically stopped and the result cannot be taken to Tax Court.");
EX(3,"Collection Appeals Program (CAP)","The Collection Appeals Program is a quick appeal of a wide range of collection actions: liens, levies, seizures, and denied or ended installment agreements. It is fast, but Appeals' decision is final and cannot be reviewed by a court.");
EX(3,"Levies that skip the pre-levy CDP notice","In a few cases the IRS can levy first and offer the hearing afterward: taking a state tax refund, when collection is in jeopardy, levies on federal contractors, and certain repeated employment tax cases.");
EX(3,"Federal Payment Levy Program","Through this automated program the IRS can take up to 15% of certain federal payments, including Social Security benefits, each time they are paid, until the tax debt is cleared.");
EX(3,"Seizure of a principal residence","A taxpayer's main home has special protection. The IRS can seize it only with written approval from a federal district court judge or magistrate, and never for a debt of $5,000 or less.");
EX(3,"Guaranteed installment agreement","The IRS must accept an installment agreement from an individual who owes $10,000 or less in tax, will pay it within 3 years, and during the past 5 years filed and paid on time and had no installment agreement.");
EX(3,"Streamlined / simple payment plan (individuals)","An individual who owes $50,000 or less can get a simple payment plan without giving detailed financial information, as long as the balance will be paid in full before the collection period ends (generally 10 years from assessment). Before March 2025 it was called a streamlined agreement and had to be paid within 72 months. It can be requested online.");
EX(3,"In-business trust fund express IA","A business that still has employees and owes $25,000 or less of payroll taxes can get an express installment agreement, paying within 24 months, without a financial statement.");
EX(3,"Installment agreement conditions","To get and keep an installment agreement the taxpayer must have filed all required returns and must file and pay on time in the future. Interest and penalties keep running, though the late payment penalty drops to 0.25% a month if the return was filed on time.");
EX(3,"Installment agreement forms","Form 9465 is the request for a payment plan. When financial information is needed, a collection information statement is used: Form 433-F in simpler cases, Form 433-A for individuals and Form 433-B for businesses.");
EX(3,"Partial payment installment agreement","When a taxpayer can pay something each month but not enough to clear the debt before the collection period ends, the IRS may accept a partial payment agreement. Full financial information is required, and the IRS reviews the taxpayer's finances every 2 years to see if payments can rise.");
EX(3,"OIC forms and fees","An offer in compromise is made on Form 656, with a financial statement on the offer version of Form 433-A or 433-B and a $205 fee. Low-income taxpayers pay no fee. An offer based on doubt that the tax is owed uses Form 656-L and requires no fee or payment.");
EX(3,"OIC payment options","There are two ways to pay an offer. Lump sum: send 20% of the offer with the application and pay the rest in 5 or fewer payments after acceptance. Periodic: send the first payment with the application and keep paying monthly while the IRS considers the offer. These payments are not refunded if the offer is rejected.");
EX(3,"OIC eligibility","The IRS will not consider an offer unless the taxpayer has filed all required returns, is current with estimated tax payments and payroll tax deposits, and is not in an open bankruptcy case.");
EX(3,"OIC deemed acceptance","If the IRS does not reject an offer within 24 months after receiving it, the offer is treated as accepted. Time spent on a dispute in court over the liability does not count.");
EX(3,"OIC after acceptance","An accepted offer has conditions. The taxpayer must file all returns and pay all taxes on time for the next 5 years, or the full original debt can return. The IRS also keeps any refund due for tax years up to the year of acceptance.");
EX(3,"Currently not collectible (CNC)","When a taxpayer cannot pay anything without giving up basic living expenses, the IRS can mark the account currently not collectible and stop collecting. The debt is not forgiven: interest and penalties continue, refunds are taken, and the 10-year collection period keeps running.");
EX(3,"Trust fund recovery penalty process","The IRS interviews possibly responsible persons using Form 4180. It then sends Letter 1153 proposing the penalty. The person has 60 days (75 if outside the U.S.) to appeal before the penalty is assessed.");
EX(3,"Discharging income taxes in bankruptcy","Income tax can be wiped out in bankruptcy only if it is old enough: the return was due more than 3 years before the bankruptcy filing, was actually filed more than 2 years before, and the tax was assessed more than 240 days before. There must be no fraud or willful evasion.");
EX(3,"Bankruptcy automatic stay","Filing bankruptcy automatically stops most collection, including IRS levies, while the case is open. In exchange the IRS's 10-year collection period is paused for the length of the stay plus 6 months.");
EX(3,"Innocent spouse relief types","Three kinds of relief from joint liability. Traditional relief: the other spouse's errors caused an understatement you did not know about. Separation of liability: for those divorced, separated or living apart, the tax is divided between the spouses. Equitable relief: when it would be unfair to hold you liable, including for tax shown on the return but not paid.");
EX(3,"Innocent spouse deadlines","Traditional relief and separation of liability must be requested within 2 years after the IRS first takes collection action against the requesting spouse. Equitable relief has a longer period: while the IRS can still collect the tax, or within the refund period if a refund is sought.");
EX(3,"Notice and demand for payment","After the IRS assesses a tax, it must send the taxpayer a notice stating the amount and demanding payment within 60 days. This notice is one of the steps required before a lien arises.");
EX(3,"Balance due notice sequence (individuals)","The IRS sends a series of letters before it levies. For individuals it starts with CP14, the first bill, followed by reminders, then CP504, which warns of a levy on state refunds, and finally the letter that gives collection due process rights.");
EX(3,"Form 656","Form 656 is the application for an offer in compromise based on doubt as to collectibility or effective tax administration. It states the amount offered and the payment terms.");
EX(3,"Form 656-L","Form 656-L is the offer used when the taxpayer truly doubts that the assessed tax is correct. No application fee, payment or financial statement is required.");
EX(3,"Form 433-A","Form 433-A is the detailed financial statement for individuals and the self-employed. It lists assets, income, expenses and debts so the IRS can decide how much the person can pay.");
EX(3,"Form 433-B","Form 433-B is the financial statement for a business: its assets, debts, income and expenses. It is used for business payment plans and offers.");
EX(3,"Form 433-F","Form 433-F is a shorter financial statement used mostly by the IRS's automated collection sites to set up payment plans for individuals.");
EX(3,"Form 12153","Form 12153 requests a collection due process hearing after a lien filing or a final notice of intent to levy. It must be sent within 30 days of the notice. The same form is used to ask for an equivalent hearing.");
EX(3,"Form 9423","Form 9423 starts an appeal under the Collection Appeals Program. The taxpayer first discusses the issue with the collection manager, and then files the form within a short deadline.");
EX(3,"Form 12277","Form 12277 asks the IRS to withdraw a filed Notice of Federal Tax Lien, which removes the public notice as if it had not been filed. The debt itself may still be owed.");
EX(3,"Form 14135","Form 14135 asks the IRS to discharge one specific property from the lien, for example so a house can be sold with clear title and the proceeds paid to the IRS.");
EX(3,"Form 14134","Form 14134 asks the IRS to subordinate its lien, letting another lender take priority. It is granted when that helps collect the tax, as with a refinance that frees cash to pay the IRS.");
EX(3,"Form 668-A","Form 668-A is the notice of levy sent to a third party holding the taxpayer's property, usually a bank. It reaches only what the third party holds at that moment.");
EX(3,"Form 668-W","Form 668-W is the levy on wages and salary sent to an employer. It continues until released, and includes a statement on which the employee claims the exempt amount.");
EX(3,"Form 668-Y","Form 668-Y is the document the IRS records with the county or state to give public notice of its lien.");
EX(3,"Form 4180","Form 4180 is the interview used in a trust fund recovery penalty investigation. It asks who signed checks, who decided which bills to pay, and who knew the payroll taxes were unpaid.");
EX(3,"CSED","The collection statute expiration date is the end of the 10 years the IRS has to collect an assessed tax. After that date the remaining balance can no longer be collected.");
EX(3,"CDP","Collection due process is the taxpayer's right to a hearing with the independent Appeals office after a lien notice is filed or before a levy. The result can be reviewed by the Tax Court.");
EX(3,"CAP","The Collection Appeals Program gives a quick decision by Appeals on a collection action, but the taxpayer cannot go to court if they disagree with the result.");
EX(3,"NFTL","The Notice of Federal Tax Lien is recorded publicly to tell creditors that the government has a claim against the taxpayer's property. It makes borrowing or selling property harder.");
EX(3,"OIC","An offer in compromise is an agreement in which the IRS accepts less than the full amount owed. The usual reason is doubt as to collectibility: the taxpayer's assets and future income cannot cover the debt.");
EX(3,"RCP","Reasonable collection potential is the IRS's measure of what a taxpayer can pay: the equity in what they own plus their expected future income after allowed living expenses. An offer usually must be at least this amount.");
EX(3,"PPIA","A partial payment installment agreement is a monthly payment plan that will not pay off the full debt before the collection period ends. What remains unpaid at that time expires.");
EX(3,"CNC","Currently not collectible is a status for accounts where collecting would leave the taxpayer unable to pay basic living expenses. Collection stops for now, but the debt remains and interest continues.");
EX(3,"TFRP","The trust fund recovery penalty makes a responsible person personally liable for 100% of the withheld payroll taxes a business did not pay over. It reaches past the protection a corporation or LLC normally gives.");
EX(3,"Lien","A lien is the government's legal claim against all of a taxpayer's property, present and future, as security for an unpaid tax. It does not take the property; a levy does that.");
EX(3,"Jeopardy levy","If the IRS believes it will lose the chance to collect, for example because the taxpayer is hiding assets or leaving the country, it can levy immediately without the usual 30-day notice. The taxpayer gets a hearing afterward.");
EX(3,"Individual return due date and extension","An individual return is due April 15. Form 4868, filed by that date, gives an automatic 6 months, to October 15. The extension is only for filing: the tax must still be paid by April 15 or interest and the late payment penalty apply.");
EX(3,"U.S. citizens abroad","A citizen or resident who lives and works outside the United States on April 15 gets 2 extra months, to June 15, without filing anything; a statement is attached to the return. Interest still runs from April 15. A further extension to October 15 can be requested.");
EX(3,"Timely mailing rule (§7502)","A return or payment is on time if it is postmarked by the due date, even if the IRS receives it later. This applies to the U.S. Postal Service and to approved private delivery services. If the due date is a Saturday, Sunday or legal holiday, it moves to the next business day.");
EX(3,"Due dates of entity returns (calendar year)","Pass-through entities file first so owners get their information: partnerships and S corporations on March 15. C corporations and estates and trusts file on April 15. Exempt organizations file on May 15. Business extensions use Form 7004; exempt organizations use Form 8868.");
EX(3,"Information return due dates","Forms W-2 and 1099-NEC are due January 31 both to the worker and to the government. Most other Forms 1099 go to recipients by January 31, and to the IRS by February 28 on paper or March 31 if filed electronically.");
EX(3,"Information return e-file threshold","A filer with 10 or more information returns in a year must file them electronically. All types are added together to reach 10: W-2, 1099, 1098 and others.");
EX(3,"Form 8809","Form 8809 asks for 30 more days to file information returns with the IRS. For Forms W-2 and 1099-NEC the extension is not automatic and is granted only for specific reasons.");
EX(3,"PTIN","Anyone paid to prepare, or help prepare, all or most of a federal tax return must have a PTIN and renew it each year. It goes on every return prepared, in place of the preparer's Social Security number.");
EX(3,"EFIN","A firm that wants to e-file returns for clients applies to the IRS and passes a suitability check. It then receives an EFIN, which identifies the firm on every return it transmits. It is for the firm; a PTIN is for the individual preparer.");
EX(3,"ERO","The electronic return originator is the business that starts the electronic filing of a return, after the taxpayer authorizes it. The originator is responsible for the signature authorization, timely transmission and safeguarding of taxpayer data.");
EX(3,"Publication 1345","Publication 1345 is the rulebook for e-file providers of individual returns. It covers signatures, how to handle rejected returns, record keeping, refund products and advertising standards.");
EX(3,"Form 8879","With Form 8879 the taxpayer authorizes the e-file provider to enter or generate the taxpayer's PIN as the electronic signature. It must be signed before the return is sent. The provider keeps it for 3 years and does not send it to the IRS unless asked.");
EX(3,"Form 8453","Some returns need paper attachments that cannot be e-filed. Form 8453 is the cover sheet used to mail those documents to the IRS after the electronic return is accepted.");
EX(3,"Rejected e-filed return","If the IRS rejects an e-filed return, the provider must tell the taxpayer within 24 hours. The return is still on time if corrected and sent again within 5 days. If it cannot be fixed, a paper return is on time if mailed by the due date or within 10 days of the rejection notice, whichever is later.");
EX(3,"Specified tax return preparer e-file mandate","A preparer or firm that expects to file 11 or more individual or trust income tax returns in a year must e-file them. If a client insists on paper, the preparer gets a signed statement from the client and attaches Form 8948 to the paper return.");
EX(3,"Refund direct deposit rules","A refund can be split among up to 3 accounts with Form 8888, and no more than 3 refunds a year can go into one account. A preparer may not have a client's refund deposited into the preparer's own account.");
EX(3,"Identity Protection PIN (IP PIN)","An Identity Protection PIN is a 6-digit number that changes each year. If a taxpayer has one, the return will not be accepted without it. Any taxpayer who can verify their identity may ask for one, not only victims of identity theft.");
EX(3,"Paid preparer duties on every return","Every paid preparer has four basic duties for each return: sign it, enter the PTIN, give the taxpayer a complete copy, and keep a copy or a list of taxpayers for 3 years. Each failure has its own penalty.");
EX(3,"Return period (§6107)","For preparer record-keeping rules, the return period is not the calendar year. It is the 12 months starting July 1 and ending June 30. Records are kept 3 years from the end of the return period in which the return was presented for signing.");
EX(3,"Certifying Acceptance Agent (CAA)","A Certifying Acceptance Agent has an agreement with the IRS to review an ITIN applicant's passport and other identity documents and certify them. The applicant then does not need to mail the originals to the IRS with Form W-7.");
EX(3,"Amended returns electronically","Form 1040-X can be filed electronically for the current tax year and the 2 years before it. Older years must be amended on paper.");
EX(3,"Form 8633","Form 8633 was the paper application to become an authorized e-file provider. The application is now completed online through IRS e-Services.");
EX(3,"Form 8948","When a preparer who is required to e-file submits a paper return, Form 8948 is attached to explain why, for example because the client chose to file on paper.");
EX(3,"Form 8888","Form 8888 lets a taxpayer divide a refund among two or three accounts, such as checking, savings and an IRA.");
EX(3,"Form 9325","Form 9325 can be given to a taxpayer as confirmation that the IRS received and accepted the e-filed return or extension.");
EX(3,"Form 8878","Form 8878 is the signature authorization used when an e-file provider files an extension, Form 4868, with an electronic funds withdrawal for the taxpayer.");
EX(3,"ETIN","An ETIN identifies a business that transmits electronic returns directly to the IRS. It is different from the EFIN, which identifies the provider that originates the returns.");
EX(3,"IP PIN","The IP PIN is a 6-digit number the IRS issues each year. A return filed with the taxpayer's Social Security number but without the correct IP PIN is rejected, which blocks an identity thief from filing.");
EX(3,"CAA","A Certifying Acceptance Agent is authorized by the IRS to verify the identity and foreign status documents of people applying for an ITIN and to send the application for them.");
EX(3,"MeF","Modernized e-File is the IRS's current system for receiving electronic returns. It accepts returns all year and returns an acknowledgment showing whether each one was accepted or rejected.");
